window.NOVEL_DATA=[{"title":"CHAPTER 1 — THE FIRST LIE","text":"The first lie I told Tokyo was that I had somewhere to sleep.\n\nNot a big lie. Structurally a very small lie — the kind immigration officers file under housing arrangements and never think about again, the way I did not think about it, standing in Terminal 2 with £400 and a suitcase that smelled of my childhood bedroom, which is to say: of damp and Dettol and my mother's cooking, which is to say: of a building I would never enter again.\n\nI had been in the country eleven hours. I had already lied twice. The second lie was I'm fine, told to a woman on the Skyliner who asked if I was alright because I was crying, and I wasn't crying, technically. My eyes were leaking. Different mechanism. British.\n\nThe truth was that my \"friend's place\" in Shibuya was a twenty-four-hour McDonald's on a corner, and that I had chosen it because it had outlets and no one asked you to leave if you bought a coffee every ninety minutes. I had done the maths on the plane. Four hundred pounds, eleven nights, thirty-six coffees. A person can live on coffee if they are stupid enough, and I was stupid enough, and I was also too tall and too Nigerian and too gay to sleep in a capsule hotel without the staff looking at me like I had brought a goat into a library.\n\nI had a plan, which is a thing I have never had. The plan had three steps. Step one: find the club. Step two: be extraordinary in the club. Step three: be extraordinary somewhere else, later, in a country that had heard of me. I had written ORIGINS in the notes app on my phone, in capital letters, with a drawing of a microphone next to it that looked like a spoon. I had been carrying that word around for three years like a loose tooth.\n\nThe group chat was called tokyo_afterhours and it had 3,000 members and no profile pictures and a single rule pinned at the top, written in English by someone whose English was better than most English people's:\n\ntonight's word: 雨は明日まで待てない\ndo not translate it. say it.\n\nI translated it. Of course I did. The rain cannot wait until tomorrow. Beautiful. Useless. I said it into my phone six times, beautifully, uselessly, in the voice of a man who had been awake for twenty-two hours and smelled of Dettol. Then I listened to the playback and discovered I had said it with the intonation of a man ordering chicken. I tried again. I sounded like a man ordering a different chicken.\n\nAt 21:04 a moderator with no name deleted my attempts and posted a thumbs-up emoji. This is the most encouragement I have ever received from a stranger and I am not okay about it.\n\nThe pin dropped on 丸山町, Maruyamacho, and I stood outside WASH & GO for eleven minutes in the rain because there was no club. There was a laundromat. There was a woman behind the counter with a face like a locked door, and six dryers going, and the sound of a city that had not agreed to meet me.\n\nI did the British thing. I stood in the rain and apologised to no one.\n\n\"Can I help you.\"\n\nNot a question. The woman did not look up from a basket of socks.\n\n\"Yeah. I'm — I'm looking for Tokyo.\"\n\n\"The city is outside.\"\n\n\"Right. Ha. Yeah. No, the — the club.\"\n\nNow she looked up. I have done drag for six years and I know a face that is deciding whether you are a problem. This face decided I was not a problem. This face decided I was a wanderer, which is a kind of weather.\n\n\"You sell the makeup?\" she said. \"Very nice. My daughter, she also like the makeup.\"\n\n\"I — no, I'm a —\" I stopped. My suitcase was wet. My hair was doing something that VEX would have sued me for. \"Yes,\" I said. \"I sell the makeup.\"\n\n\"Dryer four,\" she said. \"It sticks. You pull.\"\n\n\"Dryer four.\"\n\n\"Pull hard.\"\n\nDryer four did not stick. Dryer four opened onto a set of concrete stairs going down, and warm air came up out of them, and under the warm air was a bass line, and under the bass line was a sound I had heard in exactly one room in my life, which is to say the room I had been born in, which is to say: people being happy without asking permission.\n\nI went down.\n\nAt the bottom was a door with no handle and a person about twenty-two with silver hair and a face like a held breath. Momo, I found out later, though he never said it and I never asked. I said the sentence. I said it wrong — three words wrong, tones like I was ordering food — and Momo did not move, did not blink, simply looked at me with the professional patience of a border.\n\nI have been stopped at borders. I know the look. This one was better groomed.\n\nBehind Momo, a voice shouted something in Japanese. Then, in English, with an accent I would spend the next four months learning to place and failing:\n\n\"Let him in, Momo. He can say rain. Any rain. Let him in.\"\n\nAnd I, Theo Adeyemi, twenty-four, London E13, £400, eleven hours, stepped into a room that was forty-eight people too small and seventy-one people too full, and a woman at the far end of the bar pouring ginger into something that was not yet a drink looked at me the way you look at a prescription you have not decided how to read.\n\n\"Name,\" she said.\n\n\"Theo.\"\n\n\"No.\" She slid a glass across. \"Not here.\"\n\n\"That's my name.\"\n\n\"It's a lovely name.\" She poured. \"Drink first.\"\n\nI took the glass. I did not drink. I was looking at the room, because you don't get a room like this in E13. In E13 you get a function room above a pub with a sticky floor and a smoke machine that has never been serviced, and somebody's cousin on the decks, and a fight by 1 a.m. Here there was a drag queen with a wig the size of a small dog doing lipsync in front of a wall of wet concrete, and six people in the corner laughing like they had been let out of something, and a man with cement burns on his hands standing very still near the speaker as if the bass were holding him together.\n\nSomebody put a hand on my shoulder — small hand, cold fingers, a woman or not a woman, I could not tell and did not need to. \"You okay?\" they said, in English with that same syntax, the words arriving slightly out of order.\n\n\"I'm — yes. I'm fine.\" My mouth said it before my brain could file an objection.\n\n\"Fine is a very small word,\" they said, and walked away.\n\nBehind me the DJ killed the bass and a voice came out of the speakers. An old voice. A recorded voice. A man's voice, or a voice that had been a man once, speaking English very badly, very carefully, like someone reading a sentence they had written out by hand and were afraid to drop:\n\n\"You are allowed.\"\n\nThe room did not react. The room had heard it a hundred times. The room kept dancing.\n\nI stood there with a glass I hadn't paid for and a name I couldn't use and a voice in the wall that knew something about me that I had not yet learned.\n\n\"Who is that?\" I said.\n\nThe bartender looked at the speakers. Then at me.\n\n\"Tokyo,\" she said.\n\nAnd because I had only been in the country eleven hours, I believed her.\n\nLater — much later, after the drink had gone warm and the queen with the dog-sized wig had finished and gone to stand in the mirror room with her face in her hands — I found the bathroom, which was a sink with a curtain, and I took off my sunglasses.\n\nI wore them indoors. I wore them because of the patches. Not the club patches; the other ones, the ones I stick on my hip and change every Tuesday like a person paying a bill. Do not ask me why I wear a patch and also a wig and also a name that isn't on my passport. I am a very complicated man with a very simple problem, which is that I am more myself when I am not me.\n\nUnder the sink there was a mirror with a crack in it and a shelf with three things: a hairpin that looked older than me, a tube of something someone had left, and a strip of masking tape with a word written on it in marker. The word was 本音. I did not know it then. I know it now. I know a lot of things now that I did not know at eleven hours.\n\nI put my sunglasses back on. I looked at the crack in the mirror and did what VEX does, which is tilt the chin and decide the crack is a lighting choice.\n\nOutside, the rain had stopped and started again, which is a thing rain does here that I am still not used to. On the street a cleaning truck went by, slowly, brushing the gutters. It was 3:06. I had nowhere to be until morning, and for the first time in my adult life that fact did not feel like a failure. It felt like a four-hour room.\n\nI took out my phone. There was a voice note from my mother, three seconds long. She never wrote. She sent voice notes that were always three seconds long, and I always listened, and I had never once heard anything in them except breathing.\n\nI did not send one back. I put the phone in my pocket and pulled my collar up and walked toward the McDonald's on the corner, and I did not look at the laundromat, because if you look at a locked door for too long you start to think it owes you something.\n\nBehind me, from somewhere under the street, the bass came back on. Four hours. Then the lights. I did not know that yet either."},{"title":"CHAPTER 2 — LEDGER","text":"Forty-eight people. Fire code says forty-eight. Tonight there are seventy-one and one cat, and the cat is not on the permit.\n\nI know this because I counted. I count everything. Cups, ice, the number of times a man says just one more and then has three more. Money, of course. Money is the easiest thing in the world to count and the hardest thing in the world to keep.\n\nI do not ask stories. Stories are expensive and nobody pays. I watch hands. Hands tell me everything. Theo's hands shake before a show and stop the second the mic feeds back. Rin's hands never shake. This is the problem with Rin. Somewhere in her body there is a shaking that never got permission to come out.\n\nThe new one came in at 23:20. Wet hair, borrowed rain, sunglasses on his face like he was hiding from a country that had not yet noticed him. He ordered the ginger thing before he knew what the ginger thing was. This is either confidence or hunger. With men it is usually hunger.\n\n\"Name,\" I said.\n\n\"Theo.\"\n\n\"No.\" I slid the glass. \"Not here.\"\n\n\"That's my name.\"\n\n\"It's a lovely name.\" I poured. \"Drink first.\"\n\nHe drank it like he wanted the glass to explain something to him. Then he looked at the speakers, because everyone looks at the speakers sooner or later, and he said, \"Who is that?\"\n\n\"Tokyo,\" I said.\n\nHe believed me. Of course he believed me. He had been here eleven hours and he was already in love with a room.\n\nIn eleven hours, a person can fall in love with a room. In sixteen years, a person can fall out of love with a country and still have to live in it. This is the difference between a visitor and a resident. A visitor gets to arrive.\n\n\n\nThe ledger is under the bar, behind the bottles nobody orders. It is a Chinese accounting book with a red cover and 214 pages and it does not contain money. Money is in the bank, where it belongs, being boring.\n\nThe ledger contains this:\n\n2019 — someone slept here.\n2020 — someone didn't.\n2022 — a broken opener. The first one.\n2023 — a man sang in the sink. Nobody knew the words.\n2024 — Momo let a man in who could say rain.\n2024 — Rin came in with wet hair and I thought, there's one.\n\nThat's the whole book. Two hundred and fourteen pages of that. People think a ledger is about debt. No. A ledger is about who showed up.\n\nTonight I have not written in it yet. I will, after closing. There is a line at the bottom of page 214 that is still blank, and I am saving it, which is stupid, because a blank line is not a saving. It's just a blank line.\n\n\n\nMy father called me sister today.\n\nYesterday he called me by my name. Tomorrow maybe nothing. I shave his head every Sunday. He holds still for me like he used to hold still for nothing. When he was strong he held still for nobody. He was a man who walked through a room like he owned the floor. Then the floor took him back.\n\nI got the call at 4:20, before opening. My sister — no, not my sister, my cousin, who lives in Tokyo and has a face like an unpaid bill — said he had been sitting in the bath for two hours and would not come out because there was a woman in the house.\n\n\"Tell him I'm not a woman,\" I said.\n\n\"Oh, good,\" she said. \"That will be very helpful.\"\n\nI went over at five. He was in the bath, fully dressed, which is a thing he has started doing. His cardigan was floating. He looked up at me with those eyes that are still his eyes and said, in Taiwanese, \"妹妹, have you eaten?\"\n\nI said, \"I ate.\"\n\nI did not eat. I shaved his head in the bathroom with the cardigan still on because he would not let me take it off, and he held still, and afterwards he said thank you in Japanese, which he never used to say, because Japanese was for customers.\n\nHe called me sister again on the way out. I know he means daughter in the way a mind that is leaving a body sometimes drops the right word on the floor and keeps walking. I know this. It does not make the word smaller.\n\n\n\nThree minutes to closing. Theo is still on stage. Somebody is crying in the mirror room — somebody always is — and I put the ginger in and I don't look at the clock. The clock and I, we have an understanding: it does its job, I do mine, and at three exactly we both stop pretending.\n\n財務大臣 is eating the garnish again.\n\n\n\n\nAt 23:41 the landlord's son came by. Not the landlord. The landlord has not come by in four years because the landlord is in Taiwan and because the landlord is my father's old friend, which is a nicer word for creditor than I would choose. The son is thirty and wears a suit that costs more than my fridge. He stood at the door of 梅屋 with an envelope in his hand and the face of a man who has been told to do something and is enjoying it more than he should.\n\n\"Ninety days,\" he said. \"My father says ninety days.\"\n\n\"Your father says many things,\" I said. \"Most of them to a lawyer.\"\n\n\"This is different.\"\n\n\"It is always different.\"\n\nHe put the envelope on the bar. I did not pick it up. 財務大臣 jumped onto the stool beside it and put one paw on the paper, and for one beautiful second the cat was a better lawyer than either of us.\n\n\"April first,\" the son said. \"The whole block. They already have the permits.\"\n\n\"April first,\" I said. \"Of course. Funny month for it.\"\n\nHe did not laugh. He was not there for laughs.\n\nWhen he left, I opened the envelope. It was a notice. It was in Japanese and it was very clean and it said the word for demolition as if it were the word for lunch.\n\nI put it in the drawer with the other notices. There are four. I have never shown any of them to anyone, not even to Rin, who would read it three times and then ask a question I could not answer in a way that would satisfy either of us.\n\nNinety days is a long time if you are counting forward from a door closing. It is a very short time if you are counting backward from a room that has to be emptied.\n\n\n\nPeople ask me why I keep this place. I give them the answer with money in it because that is the answer they can hold. Rent. Debt. The loan on the bar. True, all true, none of it the reason.\n\nThe reason: in 2009 I was locked in a room for three days in Changhua and on the fourth day my aunt opened the door and said nothing, and that nothing was the only kindness I got that year. A room with a door that opens. That is the whole product. I sell doors that open.\n\nYou want a story? I got stories. They're all the same story. That's the problem.\n\n\n\n02:58. Theo is still on stage, which is fine. He is still on stage at 02:58 every night because he believes the lights are a suggestion. They are not a suggestion. They are a landlord with better lighting.\n\nThe new one — Rin — is behind the speakers. She does not dance. She does not drink. She came in with wet hair and stood near the utility sink for forty minutes doing something with a small recorder and a cassette, and when I asked her what she was doing she said, \"Work,\" in the voice of a person who has decided that the word is a locked door.\n\nFine. I do not ask stories.\n\nAt three exactly, the lights come on. All of them. No warning, no countdown. Seventy-one faces get ten years older in one second. Theo's glue cracks like a dry field. The queen in the corner stops mid-gesture, which is a kindness, because nobody should have to finish a gesture under fluorescent light.\n\nPeople go. They always go. They go slowly, because leaving a room you love is a physical act, like pulling a plaster off a wound. The new one stays by the sink. Theo comes down from the stage and stands in the light looking like a man who has been evicted from a country, which he has not yet been.\n\n\"Four hours,\" he says to nobody.\n\n\"Four hours,\" I say, because it is true and because he needs someone to say it back.\n\nI take the ledger out from behind the bottles. I open to page 214. The blank line is waiting. I write on it:\n\nsomeone came in.\n\nThen I put the ledger back. 財務大臣 sits on it.\n\nFine. The cat can have the last word."},{"title":"CHAPTER 3 — EXPIRY DATE","text":"The rain started in November and did not ask anyone.\n\nI count pills for a living. Three hundred and forty-two today, I think. I stopped counting the ones I take. That is a different kind of counting.\n\nThe pharmacy is on the corner of a street that has no name in English. Morning people come in with prescriptions and complaints. Afternoon people come in with children. Night people come in with their hands over their faces, and I put the medicine in a paper bag and do not look, because looking is a thing I have learned to charge for and they have not paid.\n\nAt 4 p.m. the sun comes through the window for eleven minutes. I know this because I have measured it. After that the light goes and the fluorescent takes over, and everything in the shop becomes the colour of a hospital corridor in a film nobody wanted to see.\n\nHere is the thing about the vials: they have a date on them. A real date. Not a feeling. Not a maybe. 2024.09. After that, the liquid is not lying, exactly. It is just no longer telling the truth.\n\nI keep them anyway. I keep a lot of things past their date.\n\nThere are seven of them in the box. Seven is my number. I have seven tamagoyaki pans at home, and each one has a purpose that I have never explained to anyone, and if you asked me I would lie, because the purposes are ridiculous. One is for the first roll, which is always wrong. One is for the second roll, which is always correct. One is for the days I do not eat. The others are for weather.\n\nThe label on the box says a name that is not mine. It says 佐藤 凛. It says it in the way the government says it, which is to say: without asking. I did not choose that name. I chose the sound of it once, at nine years old, in a kitchen, with my mother's back turned. I thought choosing a thing inside my head was the same as choosing it outside. It is not. Outside there are forms. Outside there are eleven phone calls, not twelve. I counted, because counting is what I do.\n\n\n\nMy mother called at 6:12.\n\n\"Did you eat?\"\n\n\"I ate.\"\n\n\"Money arrived.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Insurance,\" she said. \"Don't forget, the insurance.\"\n\nFifty thousand yen a month. I have sent it for four years, and she has called it insurance for four years, and she knows exactly what it is, because my mother is not a stupid woman. She is a woman who has decided that a thing cannot be said until I say it first. This is a kind of love. It is also a kind of locked room.\n\n\"Rin.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"It is raining there?\"\n\n\"It is raining.\"\n\n\"Then wear a hat.\"\n\nShe hangs up. She does not say I love you. She has never said it. When I was small she said it with ginger in soup, and now she says it with the weather.\n\nThis rain, it has been falling since March. I think it knows something.\n\n\n\nThe scar is on the inside of my left wrist. 2016. I do not tell people the year unless they ask, and they do not ask, because I wear the sleeve of the same grey sweatshirt over it, and the sweatshirt is too big and has been too big for four years. It is the colour of a concrete wall that has been rained on.\n\nTheo asked me once, in the alley, what the sweatshirt was.\n\n\"It's comfortable,\" I said.\n\n\"It's three sizes too big.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"So it's a room,\" he said. \"You're wearing a room.\"\n\nI did not answer. He says things like that and then he is already somewhere else, throwing the next sentence, not staying to see where the first one landed. This is not cruelty. This is what it looks like when you have never had a door closed on you from the inside.\n\nMy mother's door closed in 2016. I heard the lock. I remember the sound better than I remember her face that night.\n\n\n\nAt 22:40 I go behind the speakers at TOKYO with a small recorder and a cassette and a cable I have taped twice because the tape keeps coming loose.\n\nThe cassette is from a flea market in Shimokitazawa. It cost three hundred yen. No title I could read. On side B, between two songs that are both slightly out of tune, there is a man speaking English very badly, very carefully, as if he has written the sentence out by hand and is afraid of dropping it.\n\n\"You are allowed.\"\n\nI was nine. I was under the kotatsu with my mother's brother's sake in my stomach, which I had stolen because I wanted to know what it tasted like, and that voice came out of a cheap stereo in the next room and said, in English, very badly, you are allowed, and then laughed at itself, a small dry laugh, and then said something in Japanese under its breath that I have played fifty times and still cannot decide whether it is a prayer or an address.\n\nI believed it. I still do. This is either stupid or the only true thing I have.\n\nI do not know whose voice it is. I have never tried to find out. Finding out is a kind of naming, and naming is a thing I am trying to do carefully, one form at a time, eleven phone calls, in the correct order, with the correct stamps.\n\nI press record. I let the tape run. I take the sentence out of the cassette and put it into a file, and then into a loop, and then into the club's speakers, where it will come out at 00:17 and 01:52 and 02:41, every night, whether or not anyone needs it.\n\nMei says the room has heard it a hundred times.\n\nMaybe. A room can hear a thing a hundred times and still not have it.\n\n\n\nAt 00:17 the voice comes on. The room keeps dancing. Theo is on the small stage in a wig the colour of a fresh plaster wall, and he is watching the speakers with his mouth slightly open, like a man who has been handed a document in a language he almost reads.\n\nAfter the show he comes to the sink where I am washing a cup that is already clean.\n\n\"That voice,\" he says. \"Who recorded it?\"\n\n\"A man.\"\n\n\"Do you know him?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then why do you play it?\"\n\nI put the cup on the rack. The water is cold. My hands are red.\n\n\"Because it's true,\" I said.\n\n\"Who says?\"\n\n\"The man.\"\n\n\"The man on a tape from a flea market.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe laughs. Then he stops laughing, because he has heard something in my voice that he was not expecting, and Theo is not stupid either. He is loud, which people confuse with stupid.\n\n\"I'm going to do a show,\" he says. \"Called ORIGINS. I want — I want it to mean something.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Will you be there?\"\n\n\"I'm always there.\"\n\n\"Rin.\" He says my name like he is holding something hot. \"You say things like that and then you disappear inside them.\"\n\nI dry my hands on my sweatshirt. The sweatshirt has a small hole near the left pocket. I put my finger through it.\n\n\"I will be there,\" I say. \"That is not the same as disappearing.\"\n\nHe wants to say more. I can see it in his face, the sentence forming, the next throw already in his hand. But the bass comes back on and someone shouts for ginger, and he goes, because Theo goes where the noise is.\n\nI take the box of vials out of my bag and put it in the small fridge behind the sink, next to the ginger, where nobody will look. The label says my name the way the government says it. Not mine. Not yet.\n\nTomorrow I will call the city again. Not tomorrow. Monday. The forms are open Monday to Friday, 8:30 to 5:00, and a person has to be awake at 8:30 to use them, and I work until 2 a.m., and this is the shape of the problem.\n\nThe lights come on at three. On the way out I pass the mirror room and see Theo sitting in front of a mirror with his face in his hands, and the wig on the stool beside him, and a strip of masking tape on the wall that says 本音, which I did not put there and which I have decided not to ask about.\n\nOutside, the rain has stopped. The street looks washed and expensive and entirely indifferent.\n\nMy feet are wet. I walk home anyway. It is not far. It is never far. That is the other problem."},{"title":"CHAPTER 4 — FORTY MINUTES","text":"I am in Japan for making buildings. Forty-seven floors. I am on floor thirty-one and I put the steel where the steel goes. This I understand. Steel has a place. You put it there or the building falls.\n\nDown I do not look. Up I look and I am afraid. This is backwards, Iqbal said. Iqbal is from Makassar and he is afraid of nothing, even his wife. He says this and everybody laughs because his wife is a small person and Iqbal is not. Then he crosses himself, which is a thing he does even though he is Muslim, and he says it is for luck, and my mother would not like this but my mother is not here.\n\nMy mother is in Java. My brother is in Java. Aldi is in Bekasi and it is raining there too and we talk about the rain because the other things need more than forty minutes.\n\nEvery night with Aldi, forty minutes. The screen is small. I hold the phone with my left hand because the right hand has cement on it that does not come off. Iqbal says I should wash. I say I wash. He says, no, you wash. He has a way of saying English words that makes them sound like tools.\n\n\"Kangen,\" Aldi says. I miss you.\n\nI say it back. Then the signal finishes and I am holding a phone like holding a cup with no coffee.\n\nI have two hundred Japanese words. I use the same six every day. Otsukaresama means thank you for working, I think. Or it means you are tired. I say it to the security man at the gate and he says it back and we are both correct. Yesterday I said it to a photograph of my mother that I keep in my wallet. This is wrong, I think. But she is tired.\n\n\n\nThe accident is not tonight. Tonight is a normal night. Tonight we work until six and then the foreman says something in Japanese and points at the sky, which means the wind is coming, which means tomorrow we do not work on the outside.\n\nI like the wind less than the height. The height is a fact. A fact you can measure. The wind is a person touching you when you did not say yes.\n\nAt 19:00 we change clothes. Iqbal puts on a shirt that is too clean for a construction site and says he is going to see a woman. I say, \"Which woman.\" He says, \"A woman who will marry me, maybe.\" I say, \"You already have a wife.\" He says, \"This is why I say maybe.\"\n\nWe laugh. We are twenty floors up in our minds and three thousand kilometres from our mothers and we laugh like men who are not afraid.\n\nThen Iqbal says, \"You come with me.\"\n\n\"I cannot.\"\n\n\"You come. You stand outside. You do not have to do anything.\"\n\n\"I cannot.\"\n\nHe puts his hand on my shoulder. His hand is heavier than a tool. \"You are always cannot. Cannot drink. Cannot dance. Cannot tell Aldi. You make a cage in your head and then you live in it.\"\n\nI say, \"I am living in Japan for making buildings. I did not know I am also making a cage.\"\n\nHe does not understand. Or he understands and he is angry. Both are possible. He takes his hand away and goes.\n\n\n\nThe bar is a laundry machine.\n\nThis is the truth and I will say it exactly: the entrance to the club is a dryer in a laundry shop. A woman with a face like a locked gate sits behind the counter. I do not know her name. She looks at me and says, \"You sell the makeup?\"\n\n\"No,\" I say. \"I am for the steel.\"\n\nShe looks at my hands. Then she says, \"Dryer four. Pull.\"\n\nI pull. There are stairs. There is music under the stairs. There is warm air, and the warm air smells like ginger and sweat and something old, like a closet in a house where somebody has been crying.\n\nAt the bottom a person with silver hair holds out one hand. I know this is a door. I have built doors. You put the steel where the steel goes, and then a person can pass.\n\nThe word tonight is about rain. I practice it in the taxi on the way. I say it wrong in the taxi. The driver listens in the mirror and says something in Japanese and I think he is helping me and I say thank you and he says something else and we both smile. Later I find out he said, the rain is not your friend. Or maybe he said, the rain is not your fault. My Japanese is two hundred words and all of them are almost correct.\n\nI say the sentence to the silver-haired person. I say it wrong. He looks at me for a long time. Behind him a woman's voice shouts, and then says in English: \"Let him in. Any rain. Let him in.\"\n\n\n\nInside, there is a room with no windows and too many people. I have been on floor thirty-one of a building with no walls yet and this room is more dangerous. A building wants to stand. A room like this does not want anything. It just wants you to stay until three, and then it wants you gone.\n\nIqbal is somewhere. I cannot see him. I go to the wall because the wall is a thing I understand, and I stand with my back against it.\n\nA man in a dress comes over. He is very tall and his face is not a face, it is a decision. He puts a glass in my hand.\n\n\"Darling,\" he says. \"Are you here or are you here?\"\n\n\"I don't understand.\"\n\n\"Good. Better.\"\n\nHe laughs and goes. The glass in my hand is cold. I drink because that is what you do with a glass.\n\nThen a small person with cold fingers touches my arm and says, \"You want to see the mirror room?\"\n\nI follow. The mirror room has six mirrors and a table with glue and powder and a hairpin that looks very old. On the wall there are papers. Small pieces of paper with words on them. I can read four of them.\n\nYou look good today.\nDon't be afraid.\nCome back before three.\n\nI stand in front of the mirror for twenty minutes. I do not move. I am not beautiful. I am a man from Java with cement on his hands and two hundred words and a shirt I bought at a station. But the mirrors do not ask me to be beautiful. They just hold me. This is a new thing. A mirror that does not ask.\n\nOutside, a woman is singing. It is not a Japanese song. It is a song I know from a bus in Bekasi, from a karaoke place with a broken air conditioner, from my sister's wedding. I start to sing it under my breath. I get the words wrong in English. I get them right in my head.\n\nI stand there with my hands up because I have seen other people do this and nobody has told me to stop.\n\nThis is how it starts. Not with a dress. Not with a name. With a room that holds you and a song you already know.\n\nAt 02:59 the lights come on. Every face gets old. The tall man in the dress looks like a tired boy from London. I look like a man from the site.\n\nI go back to the wall. Iqbal is gone. The woman with the locked-gate face is washing cups and does not look at me.\n\nOn the street it is raining again. I say otsukaresama to the rain. This is wrong. I say it anyway."},{"title":"CHAPTER 5 — VEX","text":"Right, so — picture this. You spend twenty-two years being told you are too much. Too loud, too tall, too Nigerian, too gay, too negative space, apparently, whatever the hell that means, my mother used it once and I still don't know. Then you get on a plane to Tokyo with £400 and a suitcase that smells like your childhood bedroom, and you find out that here, you are not too much. You are simply wrong. Different category. Upgraded, technically. Like going from \"too spicy\" to \"not food.\"\n\nVEX is the only version of me that has ever made sense. VEX has cheekbones you could cut rice paper on. VEX does not wonder if her mother listened to the voicemail. VEX arrives.\n\nThe problem with Tokyo — the city, not the club, don't get me started on the club, the club is the only honest room in this country — the problem with the city is that it sold me a poster and I bought a life. Neon. Rain. A man in a coat turning around. I came here for the turning around. Instead I got a 4.5-tatami room with a ceiling leak and a landlord who texts me pictures of the leak to remind me the leak is my fault.\n\nAnd yet.\n\nTuesday night, six people, a scaffolding stage, Mei pouring something that tastes like regret and ginger, and this tiny Japanese pharmacist woman who looks at me like I'm a prescription she hasn't decided how to read — and for four hours, I am not wrong. I am not too much. I am exactly the right amount of person for this room.\n\nFour hours. Then the lights come on and I am Theo again, 24, visa in eleven months, mother on read.\n\nFour hours is a lot, though. Four hours is more than some people get in a lifetime.\n\n\n\nMy first proper show was a Tuesday. Not the Tuesday I arrived. A week later, after I had learned three things: the password changes on Sundays; Momo will let you in if you say rain with enough feeling; and if you put the patch on the left hip it stays put during lipsync and if you put it on the right it does not, which is a scientific finding I would like published in a journal with a very small readership.\n\nI called the number again. The microphone feedback came and I said, \"Good evening, I'm VEX, and I'm going to be your emotional damage for the next forty minutes,\" which got a laugh from six people and a worried look from the seventh, who turned out to be a man in a suit sitting in row three who had no English but knew the tone.\n\nThat's the thing about drag. You think you're performing in English. You're not. You're performing in tone. Everyone knows the shape of a woman being dismissed even if they don't know the words.\n\nI did a lipsync to a song my mother used to play while ironing, which is a choice I have never explained to anyone and would like to be never asked about, thanks. Then I did a comedy number about my visa, which involved a cardboard immigration officer and a lot of miming of forms, and then I came back out as myself — Theo, no wig, no glue, just a black shirt and my sunglasses, because the patch had started to peel and I was not doing a striptease for a room full of strangers who had been kind to me.\n\nThat's when the old man cried.\n\nRow three. Small. Grey coat. He had been there since opening and he had not touched his drink. When I came out as Theo he put his hand over his mouth, like he had swallowed something and then thought better of it. Then he took his coat off the chair, put four thousand yen on the table — four thousand yen, for a man in a grey coat in a basement — and left before the last song.\n\nI watched him go. I have done this job in three countries and I have never seen a man pay that much to leave.\n\n\"Who was that?\" I asked Mei.\n\nShe didn't look up from the glasses. \"A regular.\"\n\n\"Does he come often?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Why was he crying?\"\n\n\"Because you were good.\"\n\n\"No.\" I shook my head. \"That's not it. It was — I said something in English that he can't understand, and he looked at me like I'd read his diary.\"\n\nMei put a glass on the shelf. Then she said, in that voice of hers that arrives before the words, \"Maybe he knew the English.\"\n\n\"Does he speak English?\"\n\n\"No.\" She wiped the counter. \"He listens to a room that isn't here.\"\n\nI have no idea what she meant. I wrote it down anyway. I write down everything people say in this club, because I am collecting a country and I have very little luggage.\n\n\n\nAfterwards, in the mirror room, I sat with my face in my hands and the wig on the stool beside me looking like a small useless animal. Rin came in and stood at the door.\n\n\"You were good,\" she said.\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"Not good. Something else. I don't have the word.\"\n\n\"In Japanese?\"\n\n\"In English.\" She frowned. \"I don't have the word in either.\"\n\n\"Try.\"\n\nShe thought about it. I watched her think. She has a way of standing very still, like a person waiting for a train that has already left.\n\n\"Like a door,\" she said. \"You were a door.\"\n\n\"Doors are important here,\" I said. \"We sell them.\"\n\nShe didn't laugh. She never laughs at the jokes. She laughs at things that aren't jokes, which is much worse, because then you can't tell if you're funny or if you've accidentally been honest.\n\nThere was a strip of masking tape on the wall with a word on it. 本音. I pointed. \"What's that?\"\n\n\"Honne.\"\n\n\"What does it mean?\"\n\n\"True feelings.\" She took the tape off the wall and put it back, straight. \"We don't translate it.\"\n\n\"Why not?\"\n\n\"Because then it becomes a small thing.\" She looked at me. \"Your English is too big for some words.\"\n\n\"That's the nicest insult I've ever had.\"\n\n\"It's not an insult.\"\n\n\"I know. That's what makes it worse.\"\n\n\n\nLater. 3:07. Conbini. Rice balls under fluorescent light, and Rin standing beside me eating one standing up because neither of us has a table at home that isn't also a bed or a desk or a place to put the pans.\n\nThe voicemail from my mother came in at 3:02. Three seconds. I played it twice.\n\n\"She breathes at me,\" I said.\n\n\"Maybe she says something.\"\n\n\"No. It's breathing. I can tell the difference. I've been breathing for twenty-four years.\"\n\nRin bit into her rice ball. She chewed for a while. Then she said, \"My mother sends money. Every month. She calls it insurance.\"\n\n\"So she knows.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And you?\"\n\nShe looked at the plastic wrapper in her hand and folded it into a very small square. \"I think she is waiting for me to say it.\"\n\n\"Why don't you?\"\n\n\"Because then it becomes a thing.\" She said it the way you'd say then it breaks.\n\nI wanted to say: Say it. Just say it. My mother didn't say it for twenty-four years and now we're both here, two children in a car park outside a FamilyMart at three in the morning, and I am the one who flew nine thousand miles to stand in a room that closes at three, and I am still the one who has to tell you to open a door.\n\nInstead I said, \"Fair.\"\n\n\"Fair,\" she said.\n\nAnd we stood there. Two rice balls. One small square of plastic. The lights inside the shop were so bright they made the pavement look like a hospital.\n\nThat's the thing nobody tells you about Tokyo. It isn't neon. It's fluorescent. Neon is for the posters. Fluorescent is for the truth.\n\n\n\nOn the walk back I stopped outside WASH & GO and looked at dryer four for a bit. Mrs. Saito was inside, folding towels, and she waved at me through the glass and shouted something that I am fairly sure was your makeup today is very suitable for office.\n\n\"Good night!\" I shouted back.\n\nI have been in this country nine days and I have already told a laundromat owner that I sell cosmetics, cried on a train, begged a non-binary bouncer with the word rain, and been paid four thousand yen by a man who cried at my face.\n\nI am doing very well, all things considered.\n\nTomorrow I'm going to ask Mei for a show of my own. Not a Tuesday slot. A whole night. No cameras — I'll insist on that, even though I don't know why yet — no livestream, no phones, just forty-eight people in a room with the lights coming on at three. I'll call it ORIGINS.\n\nI have no idea where the name came from. I do. I just don't want to say it out loud yet.\n\nSome things have to stay in the mouth a bit longer before they become true."},{"title":"CHAPTER 6 — EXTRACTIVE","text":"I should say at the outset that I am aware of what this project is. It is extractive. I know the literature — I can cite four scholars who would describe my methodology as a form of polite violence, and I would agree with all of them, and then I would keep shooting, because the thing about critique is that it is a very comfortable place to live and the footage is still there to be shot.\n\nI am twenty-six — twenty-six — and I have a grant from a foundation whose board I will not name, and I came to Tokyo to find \"authentic queer interiority,\" which is a phrase I wrote at 2 a.m. and have been ashamed of every day since.\n\nLet me be more precise, because precision is the last available virtue. I am not here because I love Tokyo. I am here because I have spent twenty-six years constructing a documentary about a woman I have never met, and at some point the word mother became too small to put in a grant application, so I wrote Tokyo instead, and then diaspora, and then interiority, and by the time the cheque arrived I had successfully buried the actual sentence under six layers of academic language, which is what academic language is for.\n\nThis is not a confession. Confession is a genre with a redemption arc and I am not eligible.\n\n\n\nThe club is behind a dryer. I want to write that sentence in a way that would make an editor at a festival nod slowly — behind the domestic machine, the other room — but the truth is more embarrassing and more interesting: it is a dryer in a laundromat and you pull it and there are stairs. I pulled it four times before I got the angle I wanted. Mrs. Saito, who runs the place, watched me do this and said something in Japanese and then, in English, \"You make film? My daughter makes the TikTok.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" I said. \"Very similar.\"\n\nIt was not similar. It was the opposite. But I have found that if you say yes to people in this city, doors open, and if you say actually, they close. This is a small piece of honne/tatemae I have learned without being able to use the words correctly, which is my whole problem here in miniature.\n\nThere is a person at the bottom of the stairs whose name I learned later was Momo. They held out a hand. I said the password wrong. They looked at me. I have been looked at like that by border officials in fourteen countries and by one ex-girlfriend in Oakland, and in every case the message was identical: you may think you have a right to be here; I am paid to disagree.\n\nA woman's voice from behind them said, in English, \"She can say rain. Let her in.\"\n\nSo I was let in, which is a thing that happens to you when someone else decides you are a person. I have built an entire career on not noticing that this is what happens.\n\n\n\nInside: a room that is too full and too warm, wet concrete, a scaffolding stage, a bar made of plumbing. The light is pink. I want to say the light is locally legible but it is pink. There is an older woman behind the bar who watches hands, not faces, and a pharmacist who does not dance, and a very tall drag queen with the kind of cheekbones that make you angry about your own face.\n\nI put the camera up immediately. That is my method. You put the camera up first, so that everyone knows the camera is there, so that what follows cannot be called a surprise. This is not true. It is a thing I tell myself in the shower.\n\nThe first person I filmed was the pharmacist. She was standing near the sink with a small recorder in her hand, doing something with a cassette. Her hair was wet. Her sweatshirt was too big. In the viewfinder she became an arrangement of textures: wet hair, grey cotton, the red of her knuckles, an object with a label I could not read.\n\nI filmed her for three minutes and eleven seconds.\n\nThen she turned and looked into the lens.\n\nNot into me. Into the lens. This is a specific and terrible thing. People who are used to cameras look into the lens because they know the lens is a machine and machines can be negotiated with. People who are not used to cameras look into the lens because they have understood, too late, that the machine is a person with a story to tell about them.\n\nShe walked over. She did not hurry. I admire that about her now; I did not admire it then. I was still in the part of the encounter where I was thinking about framing.\n\n\"Delete it,\" she said.\n\nHer English is better than mine and quieter. This is another thing I have since had to think about.\n\nI said, \"I'm documenting — \"\n\n\"Delete it.\"\n\n\"I will.\" This is the sentence I am least proud of. I said it in the voice I use for airport staff and foundation board members: warm, efficient, already leaving. \"Of course. I'll delete it.\"\n\nI did not delete it.\n\nI want to be exact about this, because exactness is the only thing I have left. I reached for the camera, and my hand went to the wrong button — the display button, not delete — and then I thought about doing it properly and decided not to, because the footage was good. That is the whole moral architecture of the event. One button. One decision. A very small physical movement that I have replayed more often than any footage.\n\nShe watched my hand move. Then she walked away.\n\nI have a theory that the body keeps a ledger and the mind is only the bookkeeper, and the bookkeeper is always the last to know.\n\n\n\nAt 2:40 I pointed the camera at the drag queen, who was applying glue in the mirror room with the concentration of a surgeon. She looked at the lens, then at me, and said, \"You are the problem, yeah?\"\n\n\"I'm documenting —\"\n\n\"Yes. You are the problem. But you are my problem, so —\" she gestured at the mirrors, at the six of them, at the strips of paper on the wall — \"you can stay. Don't point that at Rin again.\"\n\n\"Rin.\"\n\n\"The pharmacist. She has a name. You should use it.\"\n\nI stopped filming. I have thought about this instruction more than any note I have ever received from a subject. It was not a request for privacy. It was a correction of method. She was telling me that the thing I called authentic interiority had a first name and a job and a habit of standing near sinks.\n\nI put the camera down. Then I picked it up again, because I am twenty-six and I have a grant and a very large, very comfortable word for what I am doing, which is process.\n\nThe camera chose that moment to die. Not dramatically. No flicker. Just a small black screen where the face had been.\n\nI have never been so grateful for a piece of plastic in my life.\n\n\n\nOn the street at 3:14, the cleaning truck going by, the rain starting again in the way rain starts here — not falling so much as being decided — I took out the SD card and held it in my hand. It is a small grey rectangle. It weighs nothing. It contains forty-one minutes of people who did not agree to be in my film, and one selfie I took in the mirror at the bar, which I have tried to delete four times and cannot, because some part of me insists on being in the footage too.\n\nThis is the sentence I would put in the grant report if I were honest, which I am not: I wanted to find a woman who left, and instead I found a room that closes at three, and I filmed it as if closure were a metaphor, when it was only a rule.\n\nLater I will tell myself that I was tired. That I was jet-lagged. That the battery died for a reason. These are all true and none of them are the reason.\n\nThe reason is that the footage was good.\n\nI am going to keep the card in my pocket for the next four months. I am going to tell myself that keeping it is different from using it. I am going to be very careful with the word different."},{"title":"CHAPTER 7 — HONNE","text":"The rain stopped at 2:40 and started again at 2:41. Between those two minutes the street looked honest, which is to say: wet and ordinary and not trying to be a poster.\n\nWe were outside the conbini again. Not the same night as before. Last week we stood here and said small things and went home. Tonight Theo had a cut on his lip from a wig comb and he kept touching it with his finger to check whether it was still there, which it was.\n\nHe bought two rice balls. I bought one. He gave me the second one anyway, because Theo gives things away like he is throwing them, and then he is already somewhere else.\n\n\"Eat,\" he said.\n\n\"I ate.\"\n\n\"Liar.\"\n\n\"Not a liar. A person who ate at 11 p.m.\"\n\n\"Eleven is not a meal. Eleven is a snack with ambition.\"\n\nI took it. The plastic was cold. He stood very close, which he does, because he is from a country where closeness is a default and distance is an insult. I am from a country where distance is a default and closeness is an apology. We are both correct and neither of us is comfortable.\n\n\"Say it,\" he said.\n\n\"Say what.\"\n\n\"The thing. The sentence you have in your mouth. You have had it since Tuesday.\"\n\nI looked at the vending machine. It was lit from inside like an aquarium. There was a can of coffee with a picture of a mountain on it. I wanted to talk about the mountain.\n\n\"My mother called me sister,\" Theo said. \"Not Theo. Not my name. Sister. In London she called me son for twenty years, and then one day she stopped, and she has never explained, and I have never asked, because asking is a door and I am afraid of what is behind it.\"\n\nHe said it fast, all one piece, like taking a plaster off.\n\n\"I sent her a voice note tonight,\" he said. \"Three seconds. Breathing. I can't say I am going to be someone you can love. I can only breathe at her.\"\n\nI bit into the rice ball. It was too cold. Everything is too cold in this country in December, including the words.\n\n\"I send money,\" I said. \"Fifty thousand every month. Four years. She calls it insurance. She knows it is not insurance.\"\n\n\"So say it.\"\n\n\"If I say it, it becomes a thing.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then she has to answer.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And if she answers wrong —\"\n\n\"Then you know.\" He said it without drama. Theo almost never says things without drama. This is why I believed him.\n\nI stood there with half a rice ball in my hand. The street was empty. A cleaning truck went past. It is a thing I have noticed about this city: the machines that clean are enormous and the people are small and nobody thinks this is strange.\n\n\"Do you know honne,\" I said.\n\n\"Is that the tape on the wall?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"What does it mean, really. Not the dictionary.\"\n\nI thought about it. My mother's voice saying did you eat for thirty years. My father saying nothing for the same period. Mei pouring a drink and looking at hands. The word on the tape, which I put up and took down and put up again.\n\n\"It means the thing under the thing,\" I said. \"The thing you say when the room is empty.\"\n\n\"Tatemae is the room.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And you are asking me which room we're in.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe looked at me then. Not at my face — at my hands. He does this sometimes. He watches the way I hold things too tightly.\n\n\"We're outside the conbini,\" he said. \"There is no room. We are standing in the street.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"So maybe the true thing can be said in the street.\"\n\nI have never liked this about him. He takes a word and turns it until it becomes a tool. It is very English. It is very American. It is also, occasionally, kind.\n\n\"I want to change one character,\" I said. \"On my family register. One. To make the whole line true. To make my mother's money insurance and my father's silence something else. I called twelve times. No — eleven. I counted. They say come in person, then the form is closed, then come with a doctor's letter, then the doctor says this will take time, which means this will take paperwork. In eleven phone calls there is no sentence for I am a woman.\"\n\nTheo did not say anything. This is a skill I did not know he had.\n\nThen he said, \"You can change your name. I can change my whole life and still not change my visa.\"\n\n\"Different.\"\n\n\"Same.\" He touched his lip. \"You know what is funny. In London I used to shout that I was real. Look at me, I'm here, I'm real. Here nobody shouts. Here everyone is real in a basement for four hours and then goes home to a family register that says something else.\"\n\n\"A person can be real in four hours.\"\n\n\"A person can be real in four minutes.\" He put the second rice ball in my hand. \"I just want more than four.\"\n\nThe lights inside the shop flickered. Not off. Just a small stutter, like a person deciding whether to say something.\n\n\"I want one person to know,\" I said. \"Only one. Not my mother. Not yet. One.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"I don't know who.\"\n\n\"Oh,\" he said. \"Right. Of course.\"\n\nHe said it in such a small voice that I looked up. He was smiling, but it was not a joke-smile. It was the smile people use when they have been given a seat on a bus and are about to stand up again.\n\n\"I'm going to do a show,\" he said. \"ORIGINS. In this club, before the landlord takes it. No cameras. I told Cass. She is not happy.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Will you help me?\"\n\n\"With the show?\"\n\n\"With the name. With the — I don't know. The truth part. You know the words. I only know the volume.\"\n\nI put the rice ball down on the bin beside us. I had eaten enough. I am always full at exactly the wrong moment.\n\n\"Ask me again later,\" I said.\n\n\"Okay.\"\n\n\"And don't tell anyone I said honne.\"\n\n\"I won't.\"\n\n\"And don't say I'm here like you're throwing it.\"\n\nHe laughed. Then he stopped, because he had heard something in my voice, and because Theo is not stupid.\n\n\"Okay,\" he said. \"I'll say it quietly.\"\n\nAt 3:03 the cleaning truck came back around the block. We both watched it go, because there was nothing else to watch, and because in this city at this hour the trucks are the only things that have somewhere to be.\n\n\"Rain,\" he said, in Japanese, badly.\n\n\"Rain,\" I said, correctly.\n\nHe grinned. \"See? We're learning each other's languages.\"\n\nI did not correct him. We were not. We were learning each other's silences, which is harder, and which nobody has made a textbook for."},{"title":"CHAPTER 8 — THE LEDGER, CONTINUED","text":"I have forty-seven bottle openers. All of them are broken. People come in and see the jar behind the bar and they say, why do you keep broken openers, and I say, they have personality, and then they laugh because they think it's a joke. It is not a joke. A broken opener is an honest object. It tells you exactly what it can't do.\n\nPeople think bartenders listen to stories. We don't. We listen to hands. Hands don't lie.\n\nTheo's hands: shake before, still during, shake after. Rin's hands: never. Yusuf's hands: cement, always, even when he washes, because cement is not dirt, it is a decision the skin has made. Cass's hands: everywhere, always, holding a machine, like a person who is afraid that if she puts it down she will have to be in the room.\n\nMy hands: fine. My hands are fine. This is the least interesting thing about me.\n\n\n\nThe money comes from a company that does not exist. This is not a crime exactly. It is a thing a lawyer helped me set up in 2015, and the lawyer is now dead of a thing that happens to lawyers, and the company sends money to TOKYO every month on the 28th, and nobody knows it is me.\n\nThree hundred thousand yen. Sometimes more. On the anniversary of the accident, six hundred thousand. This is the money from the settlement, which is a word that means: we will pay you so that you never say the word again.\n\nI put it into an account with a name like a small grey animal and the money comes out on the 28th and goes into the club's account, and Mei Ling is not connected to either side of the transaction. Clean. Boring. Excellent.\n\nI do not tell anyone because if I tell anyone it becomes a favour, and a favour is a bill with feelings.\n\nTheo found out because Theo finds out things. He is very good at finding out things and very bad at keeping them. He came in at 5 p.m. to 梅屋, which is a failing bar that opens at three and closes at six and has three customers, one of whom is here to hide from his wife. The television is always on and it is always baseball. In December it should not be baseball, but the television does not care about months.\n\nHe sat at the bar and said, \"Your accountant is a woman called Chika in Shinjuku.\"\n\n\"Good morning to you too.\"\n\n\"She handles the club's account. She handles yours. Same hands.\"\n\n\"So?\"\n\n\"So the anonymous donor is — \"\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\nHe stopped. He is quite good at stopping when a person says don't, which is one of the reasons I like him, although I would never tell him, and if I did tell him I would deny it in a court of law.\n\n\"You don't want thanks,\" he said.\n\n\"I don't want a scene.\"\n\n\"Same thing.\"\n\n\"No.\" I put a glass down. \"Thanks is a bill. A scene is a fire. I don't want either.\"\n\nHe looked at me for a while. Then he said, in that voice of his that arrives from about three countries away, \"When my father cut me off, I had four hundred pounds and a suitcase. You know what I did? I told everyone. I told the whole of East London. Look what he did. Look what he did. I thought if enough people knew, it would stop being true.\"\n\n\"Did it?\"\n\n\"No.\" He took a drink. \"It made it heavier. But at least everyone could see the shape of it.\"\n\nI wiped the counter. I have wiped this counter for eleven years and it has never once become clean.\n\n\"You want a story?\" I said. \"I got stories. They're all the same story. That's the problem.\"\n\n\n\nAt 6:20 the landlord came himself. Not the son. The landlord.\n\nHe is seventy-four and he wears a wedding ring on the wrong hand and he used to drink at 梅屋 in 2011 and he used to call me mei-chan, which is a thing you can call a person when you are paying for their father's mistakes and not otherwise.\n\nHe stood at the door with the envelopes in his hand. Two of them. One for 梅屋, one for the room behind the dryer. He did not sit down.\n\n\"April first,\" he said.\n\n\"You said ninety days.\"\n\n\"Ninety days from the first notice.\" He put the envelopes on the bar. \"This is another notice. This is different.\"\n\n\"It is always different.\"\n\n\"Yes,\" he said. \" He did not laugh. This time it is different.\"\n\nI almost laughed. I did not. He has a face that has been forgiven too many times by people who needed him to be a decent man, and it shows.\n\nHe looked around the bar. At the baseball on the television. At the jar of broken openers. At my hands on the counter.\n\n\"You know what your father did to me,\" he said. Not a question.\n\n\"He lost money.\"\n\n\"He lost my money.\"\n\n\"He lost money.\"\n\nWe looked at each other. This is a thing that happens in Taiwanese families: everyone knows the shape of the debt and nobody says the number, because the number is a person, and the person is your father.\n\nHe put his hat back on. \"I am sorry about your father,\" he said. \"His head is bad now.\"\n\n\"His head was bad before.\"\n\n\"Yes.\" He went to the door. Then he stopped. \"You should tell people about the room behind the dryer. When it is gone, nobody will believe it was there.\"\n\n\"Why would I do that?\"\n\n\"Because a thing nobody believes is a thing that never happened.\"\n\nThen he left. I watched him go down the street, and 財務大臣 came in and jumped onto the stool and put one paw on the second envelope, and I let him, because he is right about everything.\n\n\n\nAt 23:00 I open TOKYO. Rain. Seventy people. Theo is behind the curtain already, taping something to the scaffolding, whispering to Rin about a word he wants to use in the show. Cass is in the corner with the camera on her knee, not filming. Yusuf is standing near the sink with his hands in his pockets like a man who has been told he can stay and does not believe it.\n\nAt 00:17 the voice comes on. You are allowed. The room keeps dancing.\n\nI take the ledger out and open page 214. There is one blank line. I take the pen out and I hold it for a while.\n\nThen I write:\n\n2024 — the bill was paid by the person who owed it.\n\nNot a visitor. Not a boy from London. Someone. A person is the smallest word that is still big enough.\n\nI close the book. I put it back behind the bottles nobody orders. I do not show it to anybody. A ledger is not a gift. It is a bill with someone's name on it, and names are complicated in this country, and they are complicated in mine too.\n\nThree minutes. Theo is still on stage. At three exactly we both stop pretending.\n\nBroken opener, broken bar, broken father, broken city. It is a very good room."},{"title":"CHAPTER 9 — CONCRETE","text":"The building is forty-seven floors. I am on thirty-one and I put the steel where the steel goes. I know where it goes. I have put it there nine hundred times.\n\nDown I do not look. Up I look and I am afraid. Iqbal says this is backwards. Iqbal is not afraid of anything, even his wife, who is a small person with a loud voice and who once came to the site and shouting at him in front of forty men, and after she left he said, she is small but she has the volume of a country, and we all laughed, including the foreman, who understood none of the words.\n\nFriday. 6:40 a.m. Cold. The kind of cold that gets into the steel and stays there.\n\nThe foreman says something about the wind and we go up. The wind is not bad. The wind is a man touching you when you did not say yes, but today he is gentle. This is what I think at 6:40.\n\nWe are putting the cross-brace on the east side. Iqbal is on the other end of the beam. He holds his end the way he holds everything, which is fully, with his whole body. He is singing. He sings a song from a film, a love song, in English he does not know, and I know it because my sister played it at her wedding in Bekasi and I know all the words in my head but not in my mouth.\n\n\"My heart will go on,\" he sings, badly, beautifully.\n\n\"Your heart is in Makassar,\" I say.\n\n\"My heart is everywhere. This is my problem.\"\n\nHe laughs. Then the beam moves.\n\nThis is the thing about steel. You put it where the steel goes or the building falls. But a beam is a thing with two ends, and if one end is not where the other end thinks it is, then the beam has an opinion, and the opinion is physics, and physics does not care about your wife or your singing or the fact that you are thirty-one floors up.\n\nI do not hear a sound. This is the thing I tell people later and they do not believe me. There was no sound. There was a space where Iqbal had been, and the beam where the beam goes, and the wind still gentle, and my hands still on the steel.\n\nLater there are boots. Iqbal's boots, size forty-two, by the door of the site office. Nobody has moved them. It is four days before someone takes them, and when they do, I do not ask where they go.\n\n\n\nThe police come. A man in a suit comes. The foreman cries, which I have never seen a foreman do, and it makes me angry for a reason I cannot say in Japanese or Indonesian or English.\n\nThey ask me questions. I have two hundred Japanese words and I use forty of them. I say I saw. I say he was there. I say then not there. The man in the suit writes things down, and I think he is writing my words but he is writing a form, and the form has boxes, and the boxes are more important than Iqbal.\n\nAt 19:00 I go to the club. I do not decide to go. My feet go. This is a thing about bodies: they are smarter than passports.\n\nThe password tonight is about a door. I say it wrong. Momo looks at me. He must know, because he looks at my hands, which have cement on them, and at my face, which has the face of a man who has just seen a person become a space. He holds out his hand. He does not say anything. He never says anything. He is the best person in this building.\n\nInside: the room, the music, the pink light cutting faces in half. Rin is behind the speakers. Theo is on the stage with a microphone that is not working yet. Cass is in the corner with her camera down.\n\nI go to the mirror room and stand in front of the mirrors for twenty minutes. The mirrors do not ask. This is why I come here.\n\nIn my wallet I have a photograph of my mother. She is standing outside our house in Bekasi with a bucket. I take it out and put it on the shelf next to the old hairpin.\n\n\"Otsukaresama,\" I say to the photograph. Thank you for working. Or: you are tired. I do not know which. My Japanese is two hundred words and all of them are almost correct.\n\nThen I cry. It is not a big cry. It is a small mechanical thing, like the signal finishing on a call. Forty minutes, then the screen goes black. This is shorter than that.\n\n\n\nTheo comes in while I am wiping my face with my sleeve. He does not say are you okay. He says, \"Darling, your sleeve is wet and that is a fabric decision I respect,\" and he puts a glass in my hand, and then he stands next to me at the mirror.\n\n\"My friend died,\" I say.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"How you know?\"\n\n\"Your hands.\" He looks at them. \"They are shaking and they don't usually shake.\"\n\nWe stand there. Outside, someone is doing a lipsync and the room is laughing, and the laughing and the standing are in the same building and this is not strange to anyone except me.\n\n\"You want to dance?\" he says.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Good. Me neither.\" Then, after a bit: \"Someday. Not tonight. But someday, I'm going to put a dress on you and you're going to dance in it and the whole room is going to be quiet because you'll be the only real thing happening.\"\n\n\"You are crazy.\"\n\n\"Yes. This is my brand.\"\n\nHe goes back to the stage. I put the photograph back in my wallet. I say otsukaresama again, to the mirror, to the shelf, to the empty place where a man was at 6:40 this morning, and I get the grammar wrong again, and I say it anyway.\n\nAt 02:59 the lights come on. Every face gets old. Mine gets oldest.\n\nI go home. In the morning I will go back to floor thirty-one and put the steel where the steel goes, because a building does not stop wanting to stand just because a man stopped singing."},{"title":"CHAPTER 10 — TAXI METER","text":"Rain. Saturday. 1:40 a.m.\n\nPassenger, male, foreign, drunk. Smells of gin. Gets in the back. Says Shibuya. I say Shibuya is where we are. He laughs for a long time. Then he cries for a short time. Then he sleeps.\n\nMeter: 1,240 yen.\n\nI have driven eleven years. I know the sound of a man who is crying and the sound of a man who is sleeping. This one did both in the wrong order. Foreigners do this.\n\nThe back seat has a broken charging port. A packet of unused wet wipes. A 2011 earthquake evacuation map. I have not taken any of them out. Passengers see them and think I am prepared. I am not prepared. I am tired.\n\nI work nights because I do not sleep. This is simple.\n\n\n\nBehind the laundromat on Maruyamacho there is music. I have driven past it nine times. Ten with tonight. Tonight I stop at the light and the passenger is asleep and the window is down two centimetres because he asked for air in a voice like a door opening.\n\nThe music comes out. Bass first. Then a voice.\n\nNot a young voice. Not a woman's voice. A voice that goes into a room I have never entered, and follows me home into the taxi, into the rain, into the sink when I wash my hands.\n\n\"You are allowed.\"\n\nMy hand moves on the wheel. A small movement. Two centimetres. The passenger does not see. He is asleep with his mouth open, and his black sunglasses are on the seat beside him, and there is a patch on his hip where his shirt has lifted, and I do not look at it, because I have seen patches before and I know what they are and I do not ask.\n\nI have learned this: do not ask.\n\nThe light changes. I drive.\n\nMeter: 1,240 yen. It has said 1,240 yen for six minutes. This is because I have stopped twice and started twice and the meter does not care about stopping. It only cares about moving and not moving. This is a fair system.\n\n\n\nThe voice comes again. I listen. I do not say anything.\n\nMy son called me mother today.\n\nI did not correct him. I have never corrected him.\n\n\n\nThe passenger wakes at 2:11. He sits up too fast and says, \"Where the fuck is the door.\"\n\n\"I do not understand.\"\n\n\"The door. The — the dryer. Which one.\"\n\n\"I do not know.\"\n\n\"Four,\" he says. \"It's four.\"\n\nHe reaches for the glasses on the seat and takes them. Then he gets out. He pays in cash, too much, and he says, \"Keep it,\" and I say, \"No,\" and he says, \"Keep it, please,\" and I keep it. Foreign passengers tip. I do not know why. I do not ask.\n\nHe walks to the laundromat. He stands outside it for a moment with his hands in his pockets, and then he goes in, and a woman behind the counter looks up, and she does not smile, and she says something, and he nods like a man being given directions in a country where he does not know the word for left.\n\nI watch him through the rear window until the light changes.\n\n\n\nMy rating is 4.8. There is one review, from three years ago, in English. It says: driver kept crying.\n\nI have never told anyone I wrote that review myself. This is not true. A passenger wrote it. A woman, 2 a.m., rain, she got out at Ebisu and left an umbrella. I kept the umbrella for a year and then gave it to a man who did not need it, and the review stayed.\n\nPeople think a rating is a number. It is a room. Four point eight is a room with one person in it who is crying.\n\n\n\n2:58. I park outside the laundromat. Not close. Far enough that the sign is small. The music comes through the wall under the door, through the wet pavement, through the chassis of the car.\n\nThe voice says the sentence again. I listen to it the way a man listens to a meter.\n\nA woman comes out of the laundromat and stands under the awning. Small. Grey sweatshirt. Wet hair. She looks at the dryer, not at the door, and then at my car, and then away. I know her. She is the pharmacist on the corner. She buys medicine from my wife sometimes, before my wife stopped going out.\n\nShe does not get in. I do not open the window.\n\nAt three exactly the music stops. The lights come on somewhere under the street, and the street looks the same as it did before, which is a thing I have never understood.\n\nMeter: 1,240 yen.\n\nThe passenger is inside. He has somewhere to be for four hours. I have somewhere to be for the rest of my life.\n\nI turn the heat up."},{"title":"CHAPTER 11 — ORIGINS","text":"I have a theory about London, which is that everyone in London is from somewhere else and nobody has told the city yet. My mother is from Lagos. My father is from a part of London that his father was not from. I am from E13, which is a postcode and therefore a country with no flag and very bad weather.\n\nWhen people ask me where I am really from, I say London, and they say no, where are you really from, and I say E13, and they say no, but where are your parents from, and by the fourth round everyone is tired and someone has said something about curry. This is a game with no winning move. I have played it at every job I have ever had, including the one where I was a drag queen in a pub in Peckham and a man said, genuinely, but where do you get the confidence from?\n\nI said, From being asked that question every week for twenty-four years, darling.\n\nSo: ORIGINS.\n\nThe name came to me in a laundromat at 3:06 a.m. while I was pretending not to sleep standing up. There was a dryer going and a woman folding towels and a word on the wall in marker that I could not read, and I thought: origins. Not where I am from. What I have made of it. The word arrived whole, like a wig in a box, and I have been carrying it around ever since, and if anyone asks me where it came from I will say the laundromat, which is true, and leave out the part about the voice in the wall, which is also true and much less funny.\n\n\n\nI asked Mei at 5 p.m. in 梅屋, which smells like someone's grandfather's kitchen and baseball. Three customers. One hiding from his wife. One asleep. One who is definitely a tourist because he is taking a photograph of the broken openers, which is the most Tokyo thing I have seen and I have been here a month.\n\n\"One night,\" I said. \"A whole night. Not a Tuesday slot. ORIGINS. Forty-five minutes of drag, then —\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Let me finish.\"\n\n\"No. You have not said the important part yet.\"\n\n\"I have — \"\n\n\"You have not.\" She wiped the counter. \"Say the important part.\"\n\nI took a breath. This is a thing I have learned to do in this country. Breathe first. Talk second. Revolutionary.\n\n\"No cameras,\" I said. \"No phones. No livestream. No Cass.\"\n\nThere. I had said it. It came out wrong — I heard it come out wrong — because no Cass is a person, and you are not allowed to say a person like that in a sentence about a show.\n\nMei put the rag down. She looked at me for a while, in the way bartenders look at hands.\n\n\"You want a room with no door,\" she said.\n\n\"A room with a door that opens.\"\n\n\"Same thing. Different word.\"\n\n\"Is it?\"\n\n\"Yes.\" She picked up the rag again. \"People can come in. They cannot take it out. This is the whole trick. You want to be seen, not captured. I understand this.\"\n\n\"Mei —\"\n\n\"Do not thank me. I hate it.\"\n\n\"I wasn't.\"\n\n\"You were in your face. It was there.\"\n\nI shut my mouth. I have a face that says things before I do. It is one of my defects, along with the sunglasses indoors and the voicemail habit and the fact that I fall in love with rooms.\n\n\"One condition,\" she said.\n\n\"Anything.\"\n\n\"Do not say anything tonight.\"\n\n\"I don't understand.\"\n\n\"Tomorrow night. Christmas. You usually do a number at 1 a.m. about the visa, with the cardboard officer. Don't.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because a room that closes at three is a room with a deadline. You know this. I know this. Everybody else comes here to forget it. You keep reminding them.\" She poured something into a glass and pushed it at me. \"Give them one night where nobody counts.\"\n\nI drank. It was ginger. Everything here is ginger. I am becoming a ginger person. My mother would be furious. She makes a stew with ginger that could strip paint and she has never once apologised for it.\n\n\"No cardboard officer,\" I said. \"Fine.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"No visa joke.\"\n\n\"None.\"\n\n\"No mention of the landlord.\"\n\n\"Absolutely not.\"\n\n\"No —\"\n\n\"THEO.\"\n\n\"Yes. Sorry. I'll do a song.\"\n\n\"Do a song.\" She turned to the television, which was showing baseball in December, because the television in this bar has opinions about nothing and seasons less. \"And no sunglasses on stage. You look like a man hiding from an optician.\"\n\n\"They're not fashion,\" I said. \"It's medical.\"\n\n\"Everything is medical. Still take them off.\"\n\n\n\nCass found out in the club at 23:50. I told her myself, because I am a coward but I am not a coward in the way that lets other people find things out from bartenders.\n\n\"No cameras,\" I said.\n\nShe had the camera on her knee. She looked at it, then at me, then at the camera again, like a person checking that the thing she loves is still where she left it.\n\n\"You're asking me not to document the most important thing that has happened to me in this country,\" she said.\n\n\"I'm asking you to be in it.\"\n\n\"I am in it. That's the problem. I'm in it taking notes.\"\n\n\"Then put the notes down for forty-five minutes.\"\n\nShe laughed. It was not a happy laugh. It was the laugh of a person who has read four scholars and agrees with all of them.\n\n\"You know what that is?\" she said. \"That's a privilege. You get to say don't film me because you've already been seen. You've been seen your whole life. Being looked at is your native language. I'm asking to look because looking is the only thing I've ever been good at.\"\n\n\"That's not —\"\n\n\"And I know it's extractive. I know. I wrote a paper about it. I can cite myself.\"\n\n\"I'm not asking you to not look,\" I said. \"I'm asking you to look with your eyes.\"\n\nThat stopped her. It came out before I could put a joke on it, which is how the important sentences always come out of me, badly dressed and early.\n\nShe looked at me for a long time. Then she said, \"My mother left when I was four months old. I have a photograph of a woman holding a baby in a hospital in Oakland, and I have spent twenty-six years making films about rooms I was not in, looking for a person who is not in them.\"\n\n\"I didn't know.\"\n\n\"You didn't ask.\"\n\n\"I didn't.\"\n\n\"Fine.\" She put the camera on the table and did not pick it up again. \"Forty-five minutes. But I get to be in the room with my eyes.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And if you use the word authentic I will leave.\"\n\n\"Deal.\"\n\n\n\nAt 00:17 the voice came on. You are allowed.\n\nI stood at the back of the room with my sunglasses in my hand because Mei had told me to take them off and because Rin was standing near the sink looking at me, and I wanted her to see my face doing something that wasn't a joke.\n\nThe room was full. Seventy-one people, one cat, forty-eight on the permit. Yusuf was by the wall with his hands in his pockets. The old man in the grey coat — I had looked for him, and he was there, row three, with his drink untouched — was watching the speakers, not me.\n\n\"We need a name for this,\" I said, to nobody in particular.\n\nMei, from the bar: \"You have one.\"\n\n\"I have four. Theo. VEX. Sir, when the airport man is angry. Darling, when I'm lying.\"\n\n\"You want the fifth.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nShe poured. She did not look at me. \"Then earn it.\"\n\nSo that's the plan. Forty-five minutes. No cameras. No cardboard officer. One room, forty-eight people on paper and seventy-one in fact, and a name I have to earn like a thing at an airport, which is to say: with forms, with waiting, with a person behind a desk deciding whether I am a problem.\n\nI have eleven months on the visa. The room has ninety days. Neither of us is good at arithmetic when we're happy, which is why I have decided not to do it tonight.\n\nTomorrow is Christmas. Tomorrow the lights come on at three and nobody leaves. I don't know why yet. I just know that I will be standing there when it happens, with no sunglasses on and no joke in my mouth, and I will be frightened, and I will stay."},{"title":"CHAPTER 12 — CURFEW","text":"### THEO\n\nChristmas in Tokyo is a marketing decision. There are lights on the wires and a fake tree outside the station and couples in coats that cost more than my rent, walking very close and looking at each other like they have solved something.\n\nIn the club there is no tree. There is a scaffolding stage and a wet wall and Mei has put one string of lights around the mirror, which is the closest thing to decoration this room has ever had. She says it is for the customers. I know it is for us.\n\nI did the song. No cardboard officer. No visa joke. No mention of the landlord. I did a song my mother used to play while ironing, and I did not introduce it, and I did not explain it, and halfway through I forgot the words in English and sang them in a language I do not speak, and the room sang along anyway, badly, in six languages, none of them the one I started in.\n\nAt 2:58 I was still on stage. At 2:59 I was still on stage. At three the lights came on and I stopped, which is a new thing for me. I have always finished the gesture. Tonight I didn't. The light hit the glue on my face and I stood there in front of seventy-one people with my hands down.\n\nNobody moved.\n\nThat's the thing. Nobody moved.\n\nUsually the room empties like a lung. Coats, goodbyes, the small negotiations of who is sober enough to take the train. Tonight seventy-one people stood in their places under fluorescent light, blinking, looking old and tired and real, and nobody reached for a coat.\n\nMei was behind the bar with her hands flat on the counter. Rin was by the sink with one hand on the small recorder. Yusuf was by the wall with both hands out of his pockets for once. Cass had the camera on the table and her hands nowhere near it. Even the old man in row three was still sitting there with his untouched drink.\n\nAt 3:04 somebody coughed. It was the cat.\n\nI have never been so grateful for a cat.\n\n\n\n### RIN\n\nThe lights come on and nobody goes.\n\nThis is not a fact. It is a weather event. A thing that happens to a room and not to a person.\n\nMy hands are on the recorder. I should put it in my bag. I do not. The tape is finished. The voice has said the sentence twice tonight and the room has heard it a hundred times and I am the only person who keeps the tape in a box instead of throwing it away, which is the reason I keep a lot of things past their date.\n\nTheo is on the stage with the light on his face. He looks younger and older at the same time. This is what happens when glue dries.\n\nMei has not moved behind the bar. This is how I know something is wrong. Mei always moves. Hands, glasses, rag, hands. When Mei stops, the whole room stops with her.\n\nAt 3:06 a woman near the stairs starts to sing. Quietly. Not a performance. A person standing in a room with the lights on and singing because the alternative is to leave. Someone else joins. Then Yusuf, from the wall, in English that is almost correct, and I know the song, because I know all the songs in this room now, which is a thing I did not plan.\n\nI do not sing. I put the recorder in my bag. Then I take it out again.\n\nOutside, the rain has stopped. It has been raining since November and tonight it has stopped for eleven minutes and nobody has said anything about it, which is strange, because weather is what we talk about when we cannot talk.\n\nTheo says, \"Well.\"\n\nMei says, \"Well.\"\n\nNobody goes.\n\n\n\n### MEI\n\nAt three exactly the lights come on and nobody leaves.\n\nI have worked eleven years in this room. I have seen a man propose to a woman at 2:59 and be told ask me at five and finish his drink alone. I have seen a queen fall off the scaffolding and get up and finish the number because falling is not the same as stopping. I have seen a man cry into a glass and then pay for the glass, which is a thing you can only do in a country where crying has a price.\n\nI have never seen this.\n\nSeventy-one people under fluorescent light. One cat. No coats. No goodbyes. Nobody counting. Theo said no visa joke and somebody should tell the room, because the room is behaving like a man who has forgotten he has a deadline.\n\nMy hands are flat on the counter. The rag is under them. Hands don't lie, and mine are saying something I do not want to hear, which is: let them stay.\n\nLet them stay. Four more minutes. Four minutes is nothing. Four minutes is a cigarette. Four minutes is a room that does not have to close yet because nobody has told it to.\n\nI think about a notice in the drawer. April first. I think about the man in the suit and the man with the wedding ring on the wrong hand and the money coming out of an account with a name like a small grey animal on the twenty-eighth of every month, and I think: I am paying to keep a door open that nobody will let me lock.\n\nAt 3:12 I do the thing. I go to the stereo and I put the bass back on, quietly, so low you feel it in your teeth before you hear it. Not a song. Just the low part. A room needs a heartbeat.\n\nTheo looks at me from the stage. I do not look back. If I look back he will smile and then it becomes a moment, and a moment is a thing you can sell, and I am not selling tonight.\n\n財務大臣 jumps onto the bar and knocks over a glass. It breaks. Nobody moves to clean it up. Fine. The cat can have the glass too.\n\nAt 3:20 people start to go. Slowly. In ones and twos. Not because the music stopped — I leave it on — but because a room can only hold a thing for so long before the thing becomes too heavy to stand under.\n\nBy 3:38 there is nobody. Theo is still on the stage. Rin is still by the sink. Yusuf is still by the wall. Cass is at the table with her hands in her lap.\n\nI wipe the counter. I write one line in the ledger, and then I close it.\n\n2024 — nobody left.\n\n\n\n### YUSUF\n\nAt three the lights come on and nobody goes.\n\nI do not understand this. In my head, three means go. Three is a rule. Steel goes where steel goes, three means go, the call finishes at forty minutes, the building is forty-seven floors. Rules are the shape of the world. A person can be lost in a city but not in a rule.\n\nTonight the rule is broken and nobody is afraid.\n\nI stand by the wall with my hands out of my pockets. This is new. Iqbal used to say you make a cage in your head and then you live in it, and tonight I have my hands out of my pockets and I have not made a cage, and it is Christmas, which is a day my mother does not celebrate and Aldi celebrates because his mother likes the lights.\n\nAt 3:06 somebody sings. I know the song. It is the song Iqbal sang at 6:40 on Friday with his hands on the beam. I sing it in English I do not know, and I get the words wrong, and a woman next to me sings the Japanese words, and Theo sings something else entirely, and all three of us are correct.\n\nThen Rin looks at me. She does not smile. She looks at me the way a person looks at a thing that is standing when they expected it to be on the floor. I put my hands up a little. Not dancing. Just up.\n\nTheo sees and makes a face like I have just won a prize on a television show, and I laugh, which is wrong, because it is Christmas and a man died last week and my boyfriend is in Bekasi and my hands have cement on them and the room has seventy-one people in it and no permit.\n\nBut I laugh. It comes out. I do not stop it.\n\nOutside, the rain has stopped for eleven minutes. I say otsukaresama to the door on the way out, which is wrong, I know it is wrong, but the door has been working hard tonight.\n\n\n\n### CASS\n\nThe camera is on the table and my hands are in my lap. This is a sentence I have never been able to write before tonight.\n\nAt three the lights come on and nobody leaves. A room under fluorescent light with seventy-one people in it who have all agreed, silently, to pretend that the light is not a light but a kind of weather. I want to film this. I want to film it so badly my fingers are making small movements on my thighs, the way they do when I am holding a thing that isn't there.\n\nThe grant report wants this. Subject refuses closure. Community constitutes itself against temporal regulation. I can write the sentence. I can write it in my head while I sit here with my hands in my lap. That is the horror of it: the sentence is already written, even though I did not film it.\n\nI have been in this room for four hours with my eyes and no machine, and I have discovered that I do not know how to look. I keep reaching for a frame. I keep arranging the light in my head. I keep thinking about where the old man in row three would sit in the edit.\n\nAt 3:06 someone sings. I do not know the song. Three people are singing it in three languages, none of which they fully speak, and the effect is not a chorus, it is a small collapse. I watch the way Theo's mouth moves when he stops trying to be funny. I watch Rin watching Yusuf's hands. I watch Mei behind the bar with her hands flat on the counter, which is the most shot-like thing I have ever seen and which I cannot shoot.\n\nThis is what I have instead: a memory of a room, which will degrade. A recording that is already editing itself into a story about me.\n\nThe old man in row three gets up at 3:31 and leaves four thousand yen on the table. I look at the money. I do not film it. I want to know whose son he is. I want to know who he was before the taxi. I want to know everything, which is the hunger, and the hunger is the problem, and the problem is me.\n\nOn the street at 3:40, my hands are still in my pockets. I have not taken out the camera. I am twenty-six years old and I am walking through Shibuya at 3:40 in the morning with nothing to show for it, and I have never been so frightened in my life.\n\n\n\n### KENJI\n\nRain stopped. 3:02 a.m.\n\nI am parked on Maruyamacho. Engine off. Meter off. This is different. A taxi with the meter off is a room with nobody in it.\n\nThe music comes through the wall. Quiet. Then quieter. Then a different sound — bass only, very low, coming through the ground.\n\nI know this sound. I do not know where I know it from.\n\nAt 3:20 people come out. They stand on the street. They do not go home. This is strange. People at three go home. This is the whole point of three.\n\nA tall man comes out and stands under the awning. No sunglasses. No wig. He looks at the laundromat like a man looking at a place he has been thrown out of, or a place he has been let into, and I cannot tell which, because his face is doing both.\n\nA small woman comes out behind him. Grey sweatshirt. Wet hair. She sees my car. I do not move. She looks at the window, and at the licence, and then at the dryer, and she goes back inside.\n\nI have driven eleven years. I know when a person has seen me and decided I am a taxi, and when a person has seen me and decided I am a man. This one decided both and stayed with the second.\n\nAt 3:41 the music stops. The street becomes a street. The cleaning truck comes, slowly, brushing the gutters. I watch it pass.\n\nMy rating is 4.8. One review says: driver kept crying. Tonight I have not cried. This is new also.\n\nMeter: zero yen. A meter at zero is a strange thing. It is waiting. It has no number yet.\n\nI sit in the car with the heat off and the windows down a finger's width, and I listen to a room that is not there, and I do not go in.\n\nI have never gone in."},{"title":"CHAPTER 13 — DENIAL","text":"The letter comes in an envelope with a window in it. Through the window you can see a word. The word is DENIED. It is in English because Aldi sent the form in English, because my English is two hundred words and his is four hundred and he is braver than me.\n\nI hold the envelope for nine minutes. I do not open it. A closed envelope is a thing with a future. An open envelope is a thing with a fact.\n\nThen I open it. The fact is there. It has a date and a stamp and a box that is ticked, and the tick is very small, and it has more authority than my body, which is thirty-one floors tall and made of cement.\n\nAldi cannot come.\n\nHe cannot come because he is a man who loves a man and this country has not written that sentence into the form. The form is beige. This is the detail I cannot get over. A thing that decides your life is beige and has a box.\n\nI put the letter in my wallet, in the place where my mother's photograph is, and when Aldi calls at 22:00 I say nothing about it for twenty-one minutes.\n\n\n\nWe talk about the rain. It is raining in Bekasi. It is raining in Shibuya. We talk about the rain because the other things need more than forty minutes.\n\n\"Your mother goat,\" I say. \"Is okay?\"\n\n\"She is fine. She ate my shoe.\"\n\n\"Which shoe?\"\n\n\"Both.\"\n\nWe laugh. Forty minutes is a small country and we live in it and we have a goat.\n\n\"Your work,\" he says. \"How is your work.\"\n\n\"Steel.\"\n\n\"Always steel.\"\n\n\"Always steel.\"\n\nThen he says something in Indonesian that I cannot translate into English, and I know what it means anyway, and I do not answer, because my Indonesian is perfect and my English is broken and neither of them has a sentence for this.\n\nAt 22:34 he says, \"Yus, I have news.\"\n\nI take the letter out. I put it on the table. I hold the phone so the screen can see it, which is stupid, because the screen is in Bekasi and the paper is in Shibuya and there is no way for one to touch the other.\n\n\"I have news,\" I say.\n\n\"You first.\"\n\n\"You.\"\n\n\"No, you.\"\n\n\"Aldi—\"\n\n\"Yusuf, just say.\"\n\nThe signal finishes. This is the thing about forty minutes. It does not care about what you were saying. It ends on the word, and the word stays in your mouth, and you are holding a phone like a cup with no coffee.\n\nI call back. No answer. I send a message. It says I am fine, which is a lie in two languages.\n\n\n\nThursday. Karaoke. Theo takes me because Theo takes people places the way other people take umbrellas, automatically and with poor judgement.\n\nIt is a small room with a machine that has a broken microphone and a screen with the words in three languages. Iqbal would have loved it. Iqbal would have sung every song and then sung them again.\n\nTheo sings something by a woman with a very large voice. He sings it as a joke for the first thirty seconds and then he forgets it's a joke, and he is suddenly a man standing in a karaoke box in Shibuya with his whole face in a song he has no right to sing, and I look away, because a person should be allowed to be serious in private.\n\nThen he hands me the microphone.\n\n\"I am going to sing the Titanic song,\" I say.\n\n\"Of course you are.\"\n\n\"You know it?\"\n\n\"Every drag queen in the world knows the Titanic song. It's in the contract.\"\n\nI sing it. In English. Badly. I get the words wrong and then I get them right in my head and then I get them wrong again, and Theo is on the floor laughing, not at me, with me, and when I get to the part about the heart going on I stop, because my heart is not going on, it is sitting in an envelope with a window in it, and a small tick has decided where it can live.\n\nTheo stops laughing. He does not say are you okay. He says, \"Again. And this time I'm singing the harmony, which I do not know.\"\n\nSo we do it again. Two men in a karaoke box in Shibuya at 1 a.m., one from London and one from Java, singing a song about a ship going down, in an accent between three countries and a language neither of us was born in.\n\nThe machine gives us 82 points. A score out of 100 for a thing that cannot be scored.\n\n\"That's —\" I stop. \"That is very good,\" I say.\n\n\"Eighty-two is a pass,\" Theo says. \"In my country eighty-two is a university.\"\n\n\"In my country eighty-two is fertilizer.\"\n\n\"Then we are very educated farmers.\"\n\nWe laugh again. This is how a man survives a beige form. Not with dignity. With a bad song and a friend who knows the harmony he does not know.\n\n\n\nAt 23:00 I go to TOKYO. I put the letter in my wallet. The password tonight is about a window. I say it wrong. Momo holds out his hand, and I pass.\n\nInside, the room. Theo is doing a number with a broom, which is a joke about cleaning, which is a joke about housework. The room laughs. Rin is behind the speakers. Cass is in the corner with her camera on the table. Mei looks at my hands and says nothing, which is how I know she has seen the envelope.\n\nAt 00:17 the voice comes on. You are allowed.\n\nI stand by the wall. Two hundred words. A beige form. A goat in Bekasi. A man in row three with a grey coat who is not my father and who has never denied anything.\n\nThe voice says the sentence and the room keeps dancing, and for four hours I am a man who can be in a room without a form.\n\nAt 3:03 I call Aldi. He answers on the second ring.\n\n\"I have news,\" I say.\n\n\"Tell me.\"\n\n\"The letter—\"\n\n\"Yus.\"\n\n\"The letter is — it is not good.\"\n\nThere is a silence. Not a bad silence. A silence with a goat in it somewhere.\n\n\"I know,\" he says.\n\n\"You know?\"\n\n\"I saw the photo. You sent the photo before you told me. You are bad at secrets.\"\n\n\"I am bad at everything.\"\n\n\"No. You are bad at secrets. Different.\"\n\nForty minutes. We have maybe six left and we spend them on nothing. On the goat. On the rain. On the shape of a blue tarp over a motorbike.\n\nThen: \"Aldi, I—\"\n\nThe signal finishes.\n\nThis time I do not call back. I sit on the step outside WASH & GO with the phone in my hand, and I say otsukaresama to the screen, which is wrong, which is always wrong, and I go inside, because Mrs. Saito is waving at me through the glass and she has a towel in her hand and a face that had decided nothing that has decided, tonight, to open a little.\n\n\"Coffee?\" she says.\n\n\"Yes,\" I say. \"Please.\"\n\nI have two hundred Japanese words. Please is one of them. It is the most useful one."},{"title":"CHAPTER 14 — READ RECEIPT","text":"New Year in this country is a thing that happens twice. Once on the thirty-first, with a train and a temple and a bell, and once on the first, when everyone goes back to work and pretends the first part was a holiday and not a nervous breakdown with good lighting.\n\nI spent the thirty-first in 梅屋 with three customers and a television showing baseball. Mei said New Year was a children's holiday and adults should not participate. Then she poured me a drink and said, \"You are a child. Participate.\"\n\nAt midnight the television showed a countdown in a studio full of people in sequins, and outside the station a million people stood in the cold and counted down to a number, and inside the bar a man fell asleep on the counter with his forehead in a bowl of nuts. It was the most honest New Year I have ever had.\n\nThen my phone lit up.\n\nNot a voice note. A message. From my mother.\n\nThis is the sentence she sent:\n\nI listened.\n\nThat's it. Two words. No I miss you. No why did you leave. No are you eating. Just: I listened.\n\nI read it four times and then I turned the phone over on the bar, because a person can only be humiliated by hope a finite number of times per evening, and I had reached my quota.\n\n\n\nHere is the thing about being cut off by your family. Nobody believes you. You tell a friend and they say but do they know you're okay? and you say yes, and they say then what's the problem? as if the problem were logistics. The problem is not logistics. The problem is that a person can send you three seconds of breathing for two years and you will play it every time, and then one day they send two words and the whole building moves.\n\nI told Rin about it at 3:07 outside the conbini. She was eating a rice ball standing up, because she eats all her meals standing up, like a woman who has never been given a chair and has decided not to ask.\n\n\"She listened,\" I said.\n\n\"That's good.\"\n\n\"To three seconds of breathing.\"\n\n\"Maybe she listened to the breathing.\"\n\n\"Rin.\"\n\n\"I am trying.\"\n\nWe stood there under the light that makes the pavement look like a hospital. She had a small cut on her finger, from a tamagoyaki pan, I think, or from a form. She has a lot of small cuts. She never explains them.\n\n\"Are you going to reply?\" she said.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because if I reply, then it becomes a conversation, and if it becomes a conversation I will say everything, and if I say everything she will have to answer, and I am —\" I stopped. \"I am not ready for her to answer.\"\n\n\"Then don't.\"\n\n\"Is that honne or tatemae?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"That's not an answer.\"\n\n\"It is the only one I have.\"\n\nI laughed. Then I didn't. She has this effect. She gives you a joke and takes it back before you can hold it.\n\n\n\nJanuary in Tokyo is dry and expensive and very clean. The city washes itself in December and spends January being pleased with itself. The trains run. The vending machines glow. Everyone wears a mask except me, and I am the only person in the street with a cold, which I have had since Christmas, which I deserve, because I stood in the rain for eleven minutes on my first night and told a laundromat owner I sold makeup.\n\nMrs. Saito has started leaving a cup of coffee on the counter for me. She still says my makeup is suitable for office work. I have stopped correcting her. One day she will find out I am a drag queen and she will say, yes, of course, I knew, because women like her always knew, and they are never surprised, and they never once say I told you so, which is a kind of mercy.\n\nAt 1 a.m. on the eleventh I went into WASH & GO and she was there with a basket of towels and she said, \"You are sad.\"\n\n\"No,\" I said. \"I am tired. Different.\"\n\n\"Sad is tired with a reason.\"\n\nI stood there. A dryer was going. The machine that holds the door to the best room in this country was turning with somebody's underwear in it, and I was having a breakdown in a laundromat at one in the morning with a woman who had known for a month and said nothing.\n\n\"My mother wrote to me,\" I said.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Two words.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"You don't know what they were.\"\n\n\"Does not matter. Two words from mother is good.\" She folded a towel. \"My daughter sends one word. OK. Every Sunday. That is her whole life in one word. I am very lucky.\"\n\n\"She's in Taiwan?\"\n\n\"She is in Taiwan.\" Mrs. Saito looked at the dryer. \"One word is enough if it comes every Sunday.\"\n\nI thought about three seconds of breathing. Eleven hours. Four thousand yen from a man in a grey coat. A room that turns its lights on at three and doesn't care who you are when they do.\n\n\"Right,\" I said. \"Fine. Two words is enough.\"\n\n\"Then why are you sad?\"\n\n\"Because I want three.\"\n\nShe put the towel down. Then she did something I have never seen anyone do in this country: she put her hand on my arm, very briefly, and took it away, and went back to folding.\n\n\"Three is greedy,\" she said. \"Greedy is good. Greedy means you are still here.\"\n\n\n\nAt 2:58 I was on the scaffolding in TOKYO doing a number about a man who cannot fill in a form, which is not funny in January and which I did anyway, because I promised Mei no visa joke on Christmas and Christmas is over and I am a woman of my word for exactly eleven days.\n\nAt 00:17 the voice came on. You are allowed.\n\nI have started noticing that the sentence is in English. Why would a voice in a basement in Shibuya speak English? Why would a voice from a cassette bought in a flea market say the one thing I needed in a language I could understand? This is a question I keep putting in my mouth and taking out again, like a sweet I am not allowed to eat.\n\nRin says she doesn't know whose voice it is. Mei says the room knows. Cass wants to know everything. Yusuf says nothing and stands near the wall.\n\nI looked at the speakers and then at Rin behind them, and she was watching the cassette player with her face completely empty, like a person listening to a stranger say something about her that she has already decided to believe.\n\n\"Rin,\" I said later, in the alley. \"The voice. It says you are allowed. In English. To a room full of Japanese people.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Why English?\"\n\n\"It was a man with English.\"\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"I told you. I don't know.\"\n\n\"You keep saying that like it's a small thing.\"\n\n\"It is not a small thing.\" She put her hands in her sweatshirt pockets. \"It is a thing I have decided not to pick up.\"\n\nThere it is. I have heard that sentence in this room four times, and every time the person said it they looked at the floor first, which is a thing I have started doing too.\n\nIn London I would have picked it up. I would have turned it over, read the label, shouted at it, taken a photograph. This is what I am good at. This is what I came here to unlearn, apparently, because I have spent a month in a room where nobody picks anything up until three in the morning, and the room has made something in me that I don't recognise.\n\nIt is quiet.\n\n\n\nAt 3:07 I took out my phone. The message was still there. The two words were still there.\n\nMy thumb went to the reply box and stayed there. I could write: I'm in Tokyo. I'm doing a show. I'm not wrong anymore. Please say something else.\n\nI did not write it. I put the phone face down on the plastic table and watched the cleaning truck go past.\n\nOne day she'll send three words. Or she won't. Either way, two words are sitting in my pocket, and they are mine, and I am not going to spend them tonight.\n\nI walked back to WASH & GO and said good night to Mrs. Saito through the glass, and she waved a towel at me, and I went upstairs to a room with a ceiling leak and a landlord who texts me photographs of it, and I slept for four hours, which is more than some people get.\n\nThe lights come on at three. Somewhere under the street, the room does too."},{"title":"CHAPTER 15 — DOOR MONEY","text":"The twenty-eighth is payday for a company that does not exist. I like the twenty-eighth. It is a good number. Even. Boring. A number that does not ask questions.\n\nThe money moves at 9:03 a.m. while I am at my father's apartment with a razor in my hand. He sits in the bath with his cardigan on and he holds still for me, which is a thing he never did when he was well. His head is small now. Everything is smaller. The man who used to walk through a room like he owned the floor has been reduced to a chair in a bathroom in Nakano with a cardigan floating around him in the water.\n\n\"妹妹,\" he says. \"Have you eaten?\"\n\n\"I ate.\"\n\n\"Don't lie to your mother.\"\n\n\"I'm not lying to my mother.\"\n\n\"She is dead.\"\n\nI stop the razor. Then I start again.\n\nHe did not say that. He said something else and my head put the words in the wrong order, which is a thing heads do in January when they have not slept. He said sister, and then he said something about the sea, and then he looked at me with his own eyes, which are still his eyes, and said nothing else for twenty minutes.\n\nI shave what is left. I wash the cardigan in the sink. He does not notice, which is a mercy, and then he notices, which is not a mercy, and he says, \"Who took my cardigan,\" in the voice of a man who has been robbed in his own bathroom.\n\n\"I did. It was wet.\"\n\n\"Don't.\"\n\n\"Don't what?\"\n\n\"Don't take my cardigan.\"\n\nI put the cardigan back in the bath. He does not put it on again. He sits there with his hands in his lap and the water cooling around his knees, and I dry his head with a towel, and he says thank you in Japanese, which he never used to say, because Japanese was for customers.\n\n\n\nThe clinic called at noon. The doctor said the word for progress, then the word for unfortunately, then a number that was not a number, which is the way doctors say there is no number. I wrote nothing down. There is nothing to write down. I have a ledger at the bar and I do not put this in it, because the ledger is for people who came in, and my father has never once come in.\n\nHe is in a room in Nakano that costs 180,000 a month. My lawyer says the settlement money will last eleven more years if I am careful. My lawyer says if I am careful in the voice of a man who has never been careful about anything in his life.\n\nEleven years. That is a long time and not long enough. I have been in this country sixteen years and I have spent four of them shaving a head, and I will spend eleven more, and somewhere in that arithmetic there is a woman I used to be who owned a failing bar in Changhua and drank too much and did not look after anybody.\n\nShe is not dead. She is just busy.\n\n\n\nAt 4 p.m. three customers in 梅屋. One hiding from his wife. One asleep. One who is definitely a tourist because he is photographing the jar of forty-seven broken bottle openers again.\n\nHe asks me why I keep them, in Japanese, and I answer in English because his Japanese is worse than mine.\n\n\"They have personality.\"\n\nHe takes a photograph of the jar. I let him. A broken thing in a jar is a safe thing to photograph. Nobody gets hurt.\n\nThe television has baseball on. In January. The television does not care about months, and neither do I.\n\nAt 5:10 Theo comes in with a cold and a face like a man who has been wounded by two words. I do not ask. He does not tell. He sits at the bar and puts his phone down face first, which is a thing people do when a piece of plastic has defeated them.\n\n\"Ginger,\" he says.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Please?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"You are a cruel woman.\"\n\n\"I am a business.\" I pour the ginger. \"You are a regular. Different product.\"\n\nHe drinks. Then he says, \"I got a message from my mother.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Two words.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"You don't know what they were.\"\n\n\"Do not tell me.\" I wipe the counter. \"If you tell me, it becomes a thing with a witness. Better to keep it in your pocket.\"\n\nHe looks at me. \"You are very good at this.\"\n\n\"At what?\"\n\n\"At not asking.\"\n\nI have practiced thirty years. I am almost perfect.\n\n\n\nAt 22:55 the club. Rain. The password tonight is a proverb about a door, which is funny because I am thinking about doors, and I do not tell anybody because a coincidence is not a story.\n\nSeventy people, one cat. Theo does the number with the broom. It is about cleaning. He has stopped calling it a visa joke but it is still a visa joke and the room knows it and laughs anyway, which is a kindness of a particular kind: laughing at a thing because someone needs to laugh, not because it is funny.\n\nYusuf is by the wall. He has an envelope in his pocket; I have seen him touch it four times. He does not dance. He stands with his hands out of his pockets, which he has started doing since Christmas, and it makes him look like a man who is about to be given something.\n\nCass is at a table with the camera in front of her and her hands in her lap. She has not filmed for a month. This is either growth or a strike and I do not care which, because the room is quieter without the little red light.\n\nRin is behind the speakers. She is doing something with the cassette player that involves a screwdriver and a great deal of concentration, and I know she is not fixing it, because it is not broken. She is touching the thing that holds the voice. I have seen people touch things like that. It is not mechanical.\n\nAt 00:17 the voice comes on. You are allowed.\n\nThe room keeps dancing. The room always keeps dancing. This is the whole miracle and nobody notices it because they are busy being saved by a sentence in a language most of them do not speak.\n\n\n\nAt 2:40, between songs, the landlord's son comes in. Not the landlord. The son. He stands at the door in a suit that costs more than my fridge and he does not come in, because he is afraid of this room in the way only a man in a suit can be afraid of a room: with curiosity and contempt in equal measure.\n\n\"My father says the twenty-eighth is the last payment,\" he says.\n\n\"He says many things.\"\n\n\"This is different.\"\n\n\"I wiped the counter.\"\n\nHe puts an envelope on the bar. I do not touch it. 財務大臣 jumps up and puts one paw on it, and for one second the cat is a better lawyer than either of us.\n\n\"April first,\" he says. \"Eight a.m.\"\n\n\"Eight a.m. on April first. Very thorough.\"\n\n\"The crew needs the space.\"\n\n\"The crew can have the space. It's their space now.\"\n\nHe goes. He does not look at the room, which is strange, because the room is right there and it is the only interesting thing in the building. Men like him never look at rooms. They look at envelopes.\n\nAfter he goes I take the ledger out and open page 214. There is one line left that I wrote on 28 November: someone came in. Under it I write:\n\n2025 — still here.\n\nFour words. A small bill. A thing that is true today and may not be true in two months, which is the definition of a promise in this country.\n\nI close the book. I put it back behind the bottles nobody orders. The money from the company that does not exist has already moved into the account, and the club will be open tomorrow night at eleven, and at three the lights will come on, and nobody will have paid for the four hours they took, which is the best business model I have ever had.\n\nDoor money. That's what Theo calls it, joking. He is not joking.\n\nI sell doors that open. Even the ones that don't last."},{"title":"CHAPTER 16 — STRONGER NARRATIVE","text":"The foundation sent an email with the word impact in the subject line. This is a word that has done more damage to documentary ethics than any single camera manufacturer, and I say this as a person who has read the ethics guidelines of four camera manufacturers.\n\nThe email said, in essence: we are excited about the direction of the project, and we would like to see a stronger suffering narrative.\n\nA stronger suffering narrative.\n\nI read the sentence six times. On the seventh I stopped reading and started laughing, which is a thing I do when a sentence has successfully destroyed me and I want to be polite about it. My laugh in a quiet room is not attractive. It is a small dry sound, like a tape being pulled too fast.\n\nThey wanted more blood. Not more truth — more blood. They wanted the thing that fits on a festival poster with a close-up of a person who is crying in a language the audience does not need to understand because the subtitles will tell them what they already knew.\n\nI wrote back: I will consider this note in my shooting plan.\n\nThis is the language of a collaborator. It is also the language of a person who has agreed to do the thing while appearing to resist it. I am very good at this. I am so good at it that I have built a career out of it and called the career practice.\n\n\n\nI have not filmed for two months. My hands keep making the shape of the camera when I am not holding it. I have a small injury on my right thumb from gripping a thing that wasn't there. I told the doctor I had been lifting boxes. She said, which boxes, and I said, camera boxes, which was true in the way that a euphemism is true.\n\nI have been in this room for two months with my eyes and no machine, and the results are catastrophic. I have noticed things I cannot use.\n\nI have noticed that the old man in row three puts his glass down exactly a finger's width to the left each time he sets it down, so that by closing the glass has moved six centimetres across the table and he has never once noticed. I have noticed that Rin presses the same button on the cassette player twice, the first time to do the thing and the second time to make sure the thing has been done, and that she does this with the pill counter at the pharmacy too, I expect, though I have not filmed her there and will not. I have noticed that Yusuf says otsukaresama to objects. To a photograph. To a door. To the wall by the stage. This is not a mistake in his Japanese. It is a grammar he invented in which everything has worked hard and deserves to be thanked.\n\nNone of this is footage. All of it is mine in a way that footage never was, and I do not know what to do with it, because the only tool I have for keeping things is the one that turns them into evidence.\n\n\n\nI asked Mei for an interview. Not a shoot. An interview, which is a word that sounds like a conversation and functions like a form.\n\nWe did it in 梅屋 at 4:30, before the club, with the baseball on the television and three customers, one of whom was asleep. I had the recorder on the table, not in my hand. This was a deliberate ethical choice that I have since told four people about, which tells you how ethical it actually was.\n\n\"Tell me about the club,\" I said.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Tell me about why you keep it.\"\n\n\"I keep it because I have a mortgage.\"\n\n\"Mei.\"\n\nShe looked at the recorder. Then at me. \"You want the true thing.\"\n\n\"I want the thing you can give.\"\n\n\"There is no true thing. There are things I can give and things I cannot. You want the second one because it is worth more.\"\n\nI did not answer. This is the problem with Mei: she has no patience for the apparatus of my self-doubt, and my self-doubt is my favourite apparatus.\n\nShe said, \"You want suffering. The foundation wants suffering. My father is sick, the building is closing, the boy has no visa, the pharmacist cannot say one word to her mother. All of this is true. You want me to put it in a sentence and hand it to you like a drink.\"\n\n\"I want to be honest.\"\n\n\"No.\" She wiped the counter. \"You want to be seen being honest. Different thing.\"\n\nI have thought about this sentence every day since. It is the most accurate description of my entire practice that anyone has ever given me, and it came from a woman wiping a counter while baseball played on a television in December, and she did not even raise her voice.\n\n\"Do you know why the foundation gave me money?\" I said.\n\n\"Because you are good at asking.\"\n\n\"Because I am good at taking things from people and making them look like gifts.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Do you hate me?\"\n\n\"I don't have time.\" She put a glass down. \"Hate is a luxury. Like a clean kitchen.\"\n\nI laughed. She does that to me. She gives me a joke with a knife in it and I laugh because the alternative is to cry, and crying in front of a subject is a note I would never be able to edit out.\n\n\n\nAt 23:00 I went into the club with the recorder in my bag. Not the camera. The recorder, which is smaller and less violent and which I have been telling myself is a different instrument, which it is, which it isn't.\n\nThe room. Seventy-one people. Theo on the scaffolding with a broom, doing a joke about cleaning that is a joke about a form. Rin behind the speakers with the cassette player. Yusuf by the wall with his hands out. The old man in row three moving his glass a finger's width to the left.\n\nMei behind the bar, watching hands.\n\nI stood at the edge of the room for an hour with my hands in my pockets. Then I took out the recorder and I held it for a while, and then I put it away again, and then I took it out again, which is the entire moral history of my life in three physical movements.\n\nAt 1:52 the voice came on. You are allowed. The room kept dancing.\n\nI looked at the room and I tried to see it the way the foundation wants me to see it: a site of resistance, a community under threat, bodies negotiating closure. All of that is true. All of it is also a poster. I could film it right now with the recorder in my bag and it would be a good film and it would be a lie, because the thing that is actually happening is that seventy-one people are tired and happy in a room that will not exist in April, and nobody in this room wants to be a metaphor.\n\nAt 2:30 I went to the bar.\n\n\"Put it away,\" Mei said, without looking at me.\n\n\"I haven't taken it out.\"\n\n\"I can hear your breathing.\"\n\n\"You can't hear my breathing.\"\n\n\"I listen to hands. Your hands are loud tonight.\"\n\nI put the recorder back in the bag. Then I said the thing I had come to say, which I had written in my head four times and which sounded worse each time:\n\n\"I am not going to film the show.\"\n\n\"The show is in March?\"\n\n\"Yes. ORIGINS. Theo said no cameras and I said yes.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"I want to be in it. With my eyes.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Do you believe me?\"\n\nMei poured a glass and put it in front of me. She did not charge me. \"I believe you today.\"\n\n\"Today?\"\n\n\"People change by Tuesday. I only believe today.\"\n\nI drank the drink. It was ginger. Everything here is ginger.\n\n\n\nAt 3:12, on the street, I took out the SD card and held it in my hand under the streetlight. It is smaller than I remember. It has forty-one minutes of people who did not agree to be in my film, and one selfie I cannot delete, in which I am standing in this bar with a camera in my hand and a face that says I am allowed to look.\n\nThe foundation will want the suffering narrative. I will send them something. I will send them something that is true and that leaves out the parts I have decided not to pick up, which is another way of saying I will send them a lie with good lighting.\n\nBut tonight I put the card back in my pocket. I walk to the station with my hands empty, and for once they stay empty, and the sensation is so strange that I almost turn around and go back for the machine.\n\nAlmost.\n\nThat is the sentence I will not put in the grant report."},{"title":"CHAPTER 17 — ALLEY","text":"The alley has three bins, one broken streetlight, and a cat called 財務大臣 who owns all of it. There is no music here. This is why all the true things are said here and not in the room, where the bass makes everything sound like a decision.\n\nIt is 1:40 and Theo is smoking, which he has started again since January, and I do not smoke, but I stand next to him because the alley is narrow and there is nowhere else to stand that is not a bin.\n\n\"Your mother,\" he said. \"Just say it.\"\n\n\"She knows.\"\n\n\"She knows and she is waiting. That's different.\"\n\n\"Different how.\"\n\n\"If she knows, she is waiting for you. If you tell her, she has to do something with it.\"\n\n\"She has been doing something with it for four years. Fifty thousand a month. She calls it insurance.\"\n\n\"Then call it something else.\"\n\nI looked at the bins. One of them had a sticker with a word I could not read. The light was broken in the way lights are broken here: not off, just worse.\n\n\"It is not a word problem,\" I said.\n\n\"Everything is a word problem with you.\"\n\n\"No.\" I turned to him. \"Everything is a form problem. A number problem. A doctor who says this will take time when he means this will take paperwork. I go to the city and they say come in person and the form is closed and come back Tuesday with a letter, and on Tuesday the woman at the window says the letter is the wrong letter, and I go home and take the pills out of the box and put them back in the box, and my mother sends money for insurance, and my father drives a taxi past this alley every night and does not stop.\"\n\nHe stopped smoking. He was listening, which he does with his whole body, which is annoying, because then you cannot take anything back.\n\n\"I saw the taxi,\" I said. \"On Christmas. I saw it parked.\"\n\n\"So?\"\n\n\"So nothing. He didn't get out.\"\n\n\"Do you want him to?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Do you want him to know?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then why did you tell me?\"\n\nI did not answer. A cat came out from behind a bin and looked at us and went back in. 財務大臣 does not care about true things. This is why I like him.\n\nTheo put the cigarette out on the wall. Then he said, in the voice he uses when he is trying very hard not to make a joke, \"I told my mother I was a woman. In a voice note. Three seconds. I breathed at her. That was the whole message.\"\n\n\"She wrote back.\"\n\n\"Two words.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Yes. Good.\" He took off his glasses and put them in his pocket. Without them his face looked unfinished. \"Rin. In London I used to say it all the time. I'm here. This is me. Look. I thought saying it was the same as being it. Then I came here and I met you and you have a name in a box and you take it out and put it back in and it is still yours, and you have never once shouted it, and you are more —\" he stopped. \"You are more it than I have ever been.\"\n\n\"I am not brave.\"\n\n\"You are not loud. Different thing.\"\n\nHe stepped closer. Not close in the way men step close when they want something. Close in the way people step close when the alley is cold. He took off his jacket — a black jacket with a tear at the pocket, the one he wears over the wig cases — and put it over my shoulders, which is a stupid thing to do because I am wearing a sweatshirt and he is wearing a shirt and it is February.\n\n\"Put it on,\" he said.\n\n\"I'm fine.\"\n\n\"Put it on, you stupid pharmacist.\"\n\nI put it on. It smelled of smoke and cheap laundry detergent and ginger. It was too big, like the sweatshirt, like every piece of clothing I own, like the word woman when I take it out of the box and hold it up.\n\n\"Don't tell me what to do,\" I said.\n\nIt came out harder than I meant it. He looked at me and for a second there was no joke in his face at all, and I could see that there never had been, underneath, which is a horrible thing to see in a person who has been funny for twenty-four years.\n\n\"Rin—\"\n\n\"Don't tell me what to do.\"\n\nHe put his hands in his pockets and stepped back, and the jacket stayed on my shoulders, and the cat came out again and sat on a bin and watched us with the face of a small grey accountant.\n\nBehind the wall the bass came back on. Somewhere inside, someone shouted for ginger.\n\n\"I wasn't telling you,\" he said.\n\nBut the music was loud and I did not hear him, or I heard him and decided not to, which is a different thing and which I have decided not to pick up.\n\nAt 2:58 the lights came on through the wall, not in the alley — the alley has its own broken light, which is worse — but you could hear the room change, the way a room changes when a door opens and somebody leaves it open.\n\nI did not give the jacket back.\n\n\n\nAt 3:07 I walked to the conbini alone. The rice balls were in the same place. The lights were the same bright hospital lights. I bought one and stood outside with Theo's jacket on and my hands in the sleeves, which is a thing I have never done with a jacket in my life.\n\nA taxi went past slowly on the main road. Not his. Yellow lights, no passenger, the number on the roof lit up. I watched it go and then I watched the place where it had been, which is a stupid thing to do and which I do every time.\n\nMy mother is in Kobe. She has my fifty thousand and she has a word for it that is not true, and she waits for me to say a sentence that I have in my mouth and cannot get out. My father is in a taxi and he knows a voice that I play in a room and he has never once come through the dryer. The two of them are in the same city and they have not spoken in nine years and I am the reason and I am not the reason.\n\nTheo says say it. Theo says look at me. Theo throws sentences like he is throwing things from a moving train, and they land, and he is already somewhere else.\n\nBut he gave me the jacket.\n\nHere is the thing about the jacket: it is not a solution. It is not a sentence. It is a piece of clothing with a tear at the pocket, and it is warm, and it is his, and he did not ask me to wear it correctly.\n\nI ate the rice ball. It was cold. Everything is cold in February.\n\nAt 3:20 I went home. I put the jacket over the back of the chair and I put the vial box back in the fridge and I took out the seven pans and used the second one, which is the correct one, and I made an omelette that was too sweet, and I ate it standing up.\n\nOutside, the rain started again. It had been waiting all night. It is patient, this rain. It has been falling since March and it will still be falling in November, and nobody has ever once told it to stop, and nobody ever will.\n\nI put the jacket on again before I went to bed. I did not tell anyone. This is not a secret. It is just a thing I have decided not to pick up and examine."},{"title":"CHAPTER 18 — ASKING","text":"I am from a family where nobody asks. My mother tells. My father tells. My grandmother told in a language I did not understand and I understood it perfectly, which is the whole problem with tone. Eat. Go. Don't. Come here. Don't come here. Twenty-four years of imperatives and I turn twenty-four and move nine thousand miles to a country where the most common sentence is a soft one that means perhaps, if you insist, no.\n\nThe first time Rin said don't tell me what to do, I heard it as a door closing. The second time, thirty seconds later, I heard it as a door closing twice, which is a thing people do when they want you to understand that it is not a draft.\n\nI said, \"I wasn't telling you.\"\n\nThe bass came back on. She looked at me with a face that had decided not to hear me, which is a thing faces do, and she went.\n\nI stood in the alley with my hands in my pockets and my jacket gone and my cigarette gone and my joke gone, and 財務大臣 looked at me from the bin with the expression of a small grey man who has audited better men than me.\n\n\"Fine,\" I said to the cat.\n\nThe cat did not answer. Good cat. Best cat. A cat who does not answer is a cat who has understood the assignment.\n\n\n\nThe thing is, I was telling her. I was telling her to tell her mother. I was telling her to call the city again. I was telling her to take the thing out of the box and put it on the table where I could see it, because I am selfish and because I love a resolution and because I have a visa that expires in eight months and a show in March and a very limited supply of emotional infrastructure.\n\nI told myself I was helping. This is a thing I say to myself often, in the mirror, usually while applying glue, which is a job that requires honesty about what is a face and what is paint.\n\nIn London I had a friend called Nigel who used to say, Theo, you don't listen, you wait for your turn to talk. I said, that's what listening is, darling, and he said, no, that's what a debate is. I did not speak to Nigel for a year. Then he moved to Bristol and sent me a message with a photograph of a dog, and I forgave him, because a dog is a piece of evidence that cannot be argued with.\n\nRin is not a dog. Rin is a woman with eleven phone calls and seven pans and a name in a box, and when she says don't, the correct response is not a better argument. It is to shut up, which is a skill I do not have and which I have been practising in a country that is very good at it.\n\n\n\nAt 23:40 I went into the mirror room. She was there, standing in front of a mirror with my jacket folded over the stool beside her, not wearing it, which is a sentence all by itself.\n\nSix mirrors. Glue. Powder. Ginger in the air. The strips of paper on the wall: You look good today. Don't be afraid. Come back before three. Someone had added a new one in a hand I did not know. It said, in Japanese, something I could not read, and under it in English, very small: also come back after.\n\nI stood at the door. She saw me in the mirror and did not turn around.\n\n\"I have a sentence,\" I said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I said it last night and the music took it.\"\n\n\"The music takes everything.\"\n\n\"Can I say it again?\"\n\nShe looked at me in the mirror. Then she turned. Her hair was wet. She had been crying or washing her face, and I did not ask which, because I have learned something in eight weeks.\n\n\"I wasn't telling you,\" I said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"I was asking.\"\n\nShe said nothing.\n\n\"I'm asking you,\" I said. \"I'm not telling you. I'm asking. I want you to tell your mother. Not because it's right. Not because the West says so. Not because I can't bear it. Because I want to know you after, and I can't know you if you keep the door shut and only let the money through it.\"\n\nThis is not what I planned to say. I planned to say something funny and then something true and then leave the room before the true thing landed, which is my usual structure. Instead I said one long thing with no jokes, and now it is in the room, and I cannot pick it up.\n\nShe looked at the folded jacket. Then at me.\n\n\"Why do you want to know me after?\"\n\n\"Because before is four hours.\"\n\n\"Four hours is a lot.\"\n\n\"It is not enough.\"\n\n\"It is more than a lot of people get.\"\n\n\"I know.\" I said it too fast, because that is my defect, and because she had used my own sentence against me, which she does, which I deserve. \"I know. I know. I have said that to myself every night since I got here. Four hours is more than some people get in a lifetime. I am very grateful. I am so grateful I could scream. But I am twenty-four and I have eight months on a visa and I want to be a person for more than four hours, and when I want things I say them badly and loudly, and I'm sorry.\"\n\nShe stood there. I have never seen her stand still for so long. In this country stillness is a language and she is fluent and I am a beginner with a phrasebook.\n\nThen she said, \"I have seven pans.\"\n\n\"Rin—\"\n\n\"One for the first roll, which is always wrong. One for the second, which is always correct. One for the days I don't eat. The others are for weather.\" She picked up the jacket and held it. \"You asked. So I am telling you. This is what asking gets.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"You don't know. But it's a start.\"\n\nShe put the jacket on, properly this time, and pulled the sleeves down over her hands, and she looked at herself in the mirror, and the mirror held her without asking, which is the thing this room does that I have never understood.\n\n\"Will you tell me yours?\" she said.\n\n\"Mine?\"\n\n\"Your mother. The two words. You have them in your pocket. You never take them out.\"\n\nI took my phone out and put it on the table. I did not show her the screen. I said the two words out loud, in the mirror room in a basement in Shibuya, to a woman who was wearing my jacket and looking at me through six reflections.\n\nThen I said, \"Now you know.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Does it make it smaller?\"\n\n\"No.\" She turned to the mirror and smoothed the collar of the jacket. \"It makes it a thing with a witness. Mei was right. But it's fine.\"\n\n\"Why is it fine?\"\n\n\"Because the witness is leaving in eight months.\"\n\nShe said it without cruelty. It is a fact, and facts are the only things she trusts. Then she walked past me and out of the mirror room, and I heard her go back to the sink, and the bass came back on, and the room did what the room does.\n\nI stood there with my phone face down on the table and my sunglasses in my hand, and for once I did not put them back on.\n\nAt 00:17 the voice came on. You are allowed.\n\nI have heard that sentence fifty times. Tonight it sounded like a question.\n\n\n\nLater, in the alley, I did not smoke. I gave the cigarette to the cat, which is a joke, and the cat did not take it, which is a better joke.\n\nI told Yusuf about the show in March. I told him there would be no cameras and no phones and that I wanted him to be in it, standing by the wall with his hands out of his pockets, or dancing, if he wanted, in a dress or not in a dress, it did not matter, because the room did not care what you wore as long as you came back before three.\n\nHe said, \"I cannot dance.\"\n\nI said, \"I know.\"\n\nHe said, \"You are always telling me I can.\"\n\n\"No,\" I said. \"I'm asking.\"\n\nHe thought about this. He is a man who thinks slowly and in English that is almost correct, and he has a way of tilting his head like a man listening to a beam settle.\n\n\"Okay,\" he said. \"I will ask Aldi.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"He will say yes. Then I will be afraid. Then I will do it.\"\n\n\"That's the whole thing,\" I said. \"That's ORIGINS.\"\n\nThe cat came out from behind the bin and sat between us. Yusuf said otsukaresama to it, which is wrong, and the cat accepted it, which is a kind of grammar I will never learn.\n\nAt 3:03 the cleaning truck went past. The street was bright and empty and expensive, and my jacket was on a woman walking home in the rain, and my two words were in the air in a mirror room somewhere under the street, and I had asked for something instead of telling, and it had not killed me.\n\nThis is the first honest thing I have done in this country that nobody filmed.\n\nI am counting those."},{"title":"CHAPTER 19 — UNPAINTED","text":"Aldi said yes. He said it in Indonesian, over a bad connection, in the middle of the road in Bekasi with a goat in the background making a noise like a small engine.\n\n\"Do it,\" he said. \"If they look, let them look.\"\n\n\"They will look.\"\n\n\"Then let them look at you. Not at a costume.\"\n\nI said nothing for a while. Forty minutes is a small country and we were in it and the goat was in it too.\n\n\"I am afraid,\" I said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"My English is not good.\"\n\n\"Your English is beautiful. You say I am in Japan for making buildings. This is a poem.\"\n\n\"It is not a poem.\"\n\n\"It is a poem.\"\n\nWe laughed then. Then the signal finished on something else, as they always do, and I held the phone like a cup with no coffee, and I went to work, and I put the steel where the steel goes, and at 23:00 I went to the club with no makeup on my face, which is the most dangerous thing I have done in this country.\n\n\n\nTheo put a dress on the stool in the mirror room. It was black and it had no interesting details and it was not a costume, which he said four times, in case I did not understand.\n\n\"No wig,\" he said. \"No paint. No glitter. Nothing. You stand like you stand at the site. Hands out. That's the whole note.\"\n\n\"I look like a man from the site.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then why dress?\"\n\n\"Because a man from the site can wear a dress.\" He said it as if it were a sentence from a manual, which it was, in a manual he had written in his head over six years and in three countries. \"This is the whole trick, darling. The dress is not the transformation. The dress is the evidence that nothing needed transforming.\"\n\nI looked in the mirror. Six mirrors held me. A man with cement on his hands and two hundred Japanese words and a letter with a window in it. The dress on the stool looked at me. I have built forty-seven floors and I have never been so frightened of a piece of cloth.\n\n\"You don't have to,\" Theo said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"No, I mean it. If you don't, I'll do the number with the broom and nobody will know the difference.\"\n\n\"We both know the difference.\"\n\n\"Yes.\" He touched my shoulder and took his hand away. \"But I wanted you to know I know.\"\n\n\n\nAt 1:52 the bass stopped. Theo was on the scaffolding with a microphone and he said, \"I am going to ask someone to do something. They said yes. So now we are both in trouble.\"\n\nThe room laughed. I was behind the curtain with the dress over my shirt and my hands in front of me, and I could hear my heart, which is a thing I have never heard on a building site because the machines are louder, and here there are no machines, only seventy-one people waiting.\n\nThen I went out.\n\nNo music. This was the plan. Theo said there would be no music for the first minute, because a man walking out in a dress needs a minute in which nobody can mistake it for a number.\n\nSeventy-one people. Pink light on wet concrete. The old man in row three with his glass moved a finger's width to the left. Rin behind the speakers with her hand on the cassette player, not pressing anything. Cass at a table with her camera on the table and her hands in her lap. Mei behind the bar, watching hands.\n\nMy hands were out.\n\nI walked to the middle of the room, which is a short distance, four metres maybe, and it took a long time. My boots were the boots I wear on floor thirty-one. I had not cleaned them. There was cement on the left one.\n\nSomebody coughed. Then nobody coughed. The room went quiet in a way I have never heard a room go quiet. A room with no music is a room with no wall. You can hear everyone breathing and everybody can hear you.\n\nI stopped in the middle. I did not know what to do with my hands, so I left them out, which is what Theo said, and I looked at the room, and the room looked at me, and nobody laughed, and nobody took a photograph, and nobody shouted anything in a language I did not know.\n\nThen Theo, from the scaffolding, very quietly, said, \"There he is.\"\n\n\n\nThe music came back. Not a drag song. Not a remix. The song. The Titanic song. The one I sang in the karaoke box with Theo when the letter was in my wallet and Aldi was in Bekasi and a goat had eaten his shoe.\n\nI started to sing it in English I do not know. This is a thing I told Aldi I would do. If they look, let them look.\n\nI got the first line wrong. Then I got it right in my head. Then Theo started singing the harmony, badly, from the scaffolding, and Rin came out from behind the speakers and stood at the side of the room with her hands in her sleeves, and Mei put down the rag she was holding, and the old man in row three took his hand off his glass.\n\nI am not a dancer. I do not know steps. I moved the way I move on a site: weight on the left foot, then the right, shoulders square, hands up, because high up you learn to keep your hands where you can see them. This is not dancing. This is scaffolding with music.\n\nBut my hands were not shaking.\n\nMy hands were up.\n\nHalfway through the second verse I stopped. Not because the music stopped. Because I was laughing. It came out of me in a way I did not decide — a small sound, then a bigger one, standing in the middle of a basement in Shibuya in a black dress with cement on my boots and seventy-one people looking at me like I had just put the steel in the right place.\n\nI laughed and I could not stop, and Theo stopped singing to watch me, and someone in the room started clapping, and then everybody was clapping, and the clapping was not for a good performance because there was no performance, it was for a man who had stopped being afraid for eleven seconds and had made a noise about it.\n\nI finished the song badly. Eighty-two points out of a hundred, if the machine had been there. In my country eighty-two is fertilizer. In this country it is a room with no cameras and no form and a man in a dress singing about a ship.\n\n\n\nAt 00:17 the voice came on. You are allowed.\n\nI have heard that sentence many times and I have never once listened to it until tonight. I stood at the side of the room in the black dress and I let it say the thing, and I did not translate it, and I did not need to, because the room had just spent four minutes being quiet with me and there was nothing to translate.\n\nAfterwards, in the mirror room, I took off the dress and folded it and put it on the stool, and Theo came in and looked at it for a bit.\n\n\"You were good,\" he said.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Something else. I don't have the word.\"\n\n\"In English?\"\n\n\"In English.\" He sat on the floor against the mirror. \"You know what you did. You made a room quiet. That is the only thing I have ever wanted to do and I do it by making noise.\"\n\n\"You made the room.\"\n\n\"I made a room. You made it quiet. Different jobs.\"\n\nI folded the dress again, which is a thing you do with clothes, and I put it in a bag, and I said, \"I will keep this.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"And I will wear it once more.\"\n\n\"When?\"\n\n\"When Aldi comes.\"\n\nWe sat there. Outside, someone shouted for ginger. The cat was somewhere near the bar, doing what the cat does.\n\n\"I am going home in March,\" I said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Four days after the last night.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe did not ask me to stay, which is a new thing, and which I noticed, and which I am putting in the pocket with the other things I have decided not to pick up yet.\n\n\"Then come to the show,\" he said. \"On the last Saturday. No cameras. Forty-eight people. Bring the dress.\"\n\n\"Okay.\"\n\n\"Bring Aldi too, if you can.\"\n\n\"In my head,\" I said. \"Only in my head.\"\n\n\"Fine. He can be in the head. That's the best room anyway.\"\n\nAt three the lights came on. I went home with the dress in a bag and my boots still dirty, and on the train a woman in a suit looked at the bag and then at me and then away, and I said otsukaresama to her under my breath, which was wrong, and which I meant."},{"title":"CHAPTER 20 — TIE","text":"Rain. Tuesday. 11:40 p.m.\n\nMy son waits outside the station with a bag. He is nineteen. He wears a coat his mother bought. He has my hands, which he does not know.\n\nHe opens the back door. He does not get in.\n\n\"Papa,\" he says.\n\nThis is the word he uses when he wants money.\n\nI say, \"Get in.\"\n\n\"No. I come with you.\"\n\n\"I am working.\"\n\n\"I know.\" He gets in. He puts the bag on the seat. \"I have something.\"\n\nI look in the mirror. He is looking at the back of my head, not at my face. This is how he sees me now. A back of a head. A man in a front seat.\n\nI wait.\n\nHe takes out a tie. Blue. Cheap. A tie from a station shop with the paper still on it.\n\n\"For you,\" he says. \"For the interview.\"\n\n\"What interview.\"\n\n\"Not yours. Mine.\" He looks out the window. \"Next week. At a company. I need to look like a person who has a father who wears a tie.\"\n\nI say nothing.\n\n\"I bought one for me too.\" He holds up another tie. Grey. \"We can match. Not the same. Match.\"\n\nI start the car. Meter: 0 yen.\n\n\n\nHe sits in the back for forty minutes. No passengers. I drive toward Shibuya and then toward Nakano and then back, because he has not told me where to go and I cannot ask, because asking is a thing I do not do.\n\nHe talks. Nineteen years old. He talks about the company, about a woman in his class, about the train being late, about the food at university being bad, about a game. I listen in the mirror. He watches the back of my head.\n\nThen he says it. Not loudly. Not as a joke. As a thing said into a window.\n\n\"妈, you coming home for dinner today?\"\n\nI do not correct him.\n\nI have never corrected him.\n\nThe meter moves. 480 yen. 720 yen. The wipers go. The rain is the same rain as before and as 2011 and as every night for eleven years.\n\nHe says it again, softer, maybe to the window, maybe to me. \"You coming?\"\n\n\"Tuesday,\" I say.\n\nThis is not an answer. He knows it is not an answer. He puts his forehead against the glass.\n\n\"I made rice,\" he says. \"Your — she made too much. She asked me to ask you.\"\n\n\"She asked.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"She knows where I am.\"\n\n\"Yes.\" A pause. \"Everyone knows where you are.\"\n\nI drive. 960 yen. 1,040. He has the blue tie in his hand and he is turning it over and over, feeling the fabric, the way a man checks steel.\n\n\n\nHe gets out at 12:30. At the door he turns.\n\n\"You'll wear it?\" he says.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"I'll be at the station at eight. If you come, you come.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe walks into the building. He does not look back. This is a kindness. He has learned it from someone, and I do not know who, and I am afraid to ask, because asking is a thing I do not do.\n\nI put the blue tie on the passenger seat. I do not put it on.\n\nI have never worn a tie in this car.\n\n\n\nMy rating is 4.8. One review. Driver kept crying. I read it once a month. It is the only thing in the world that knows me, and it is written by a stranger, in English.\n\nAt 1:40 a.m. I park on Maruyamacho, far from the laundromat. The music comes through the wall. Quiet. The voice says the sentence.\n\n\"You are allowed.\"\n\nI listen to it the way a man listens to a meter.\n\nA woman in a grey sweatshirt comes out of the laundromat once in a while. She does not see me. Or she sees me and decides I am a taxi, which is safer.\n\nI have a tie on the passenger seat. A son who called me mother. A wife who asked him to ask me. A voice in a wall. I have heard it for four months and never named it.\n\nI know the shape of the voice. I do not ask who put it there.\n\nI still do not say this out loud. This is not the same as not knowing.\n\n\n\n2:58. The music stops under the street. The lights come on somewhere I cannot see.\n\nI put the tie in the glove compartment, with the 2011 evacuation map and the unused wet wipes. It does not fit. I close it on the tie and the map and the wipes and the small space where a man could keep a thing if he wanted to.\n\nMeter: 1,240 yen.\n\nHe said mother twice. I did not correct him either time.\n\nEight p.m. Tuesday. Station. I will not go.\n\nI have never gone."},{"title":"CHAPTER 21 — NOTICE","text":"Another notice came by registered post. This is a thing people in this country still do, which I respect. A fax would be more honest and less kind. A registered postman stands at your door with a clipboard and a face that says I did not write this, which is true, and which does not help.\n\nI signed for it at 4:10 in 梅屋 with the baseball on the television and one customer asleep and one hiding from his wife.\n\nThere are three customers, always three. On Mondays there are two. On the first day of the month there are four, because a man comes to collect a debt that is not his and then buys a drink with the money I give him not to collect it. This is the whole economy of this bar. I am not a bartender. I am a woman with a jar of forty-seven broken openers and a very specific talent for not being asked to leave.\n\nThe notice is in a window envelope. Through the window you can see a word. The word is not DENIED. It is a word that means removal and it is printed in a font that has been chosen by a person who has never had to remove anything.\n\nI put it in the drawer with the other notices. There are now six.\n\n\n\nAt 5 p.m. the landlord's son came again. He does not call. He comes in a suit and stands just inside the door and looks at the room like a man who has been sent to inspect a thing he does not want to inherit.\n\n\"My father says you should tell people,\" he said.\n\n\"I tell people.\"\n\n\"Who?\"\n\n\"Nobody who matters.\"\n\n\"That is not —\"\n\n\"Do not finish that sentence in my bar.\"\n\nHe finished it in his head. I could see him do it.\n\n\"Eight a.m. on the first of April,\" he said. \"The crew has a schedule. They will start with the laundromat.\"\n\nI looked up. \"The laundromat?\"\n\n\"The whole block. Mrs. Saito has been told.\"\n\nMrs. Saito has been told. This is worse than anything, because Mrs. Saito has worked that laundromat for twenty-two years and she has never once been told anything by anybody and she has a face that had decided nothing that opens for exactly one person, and now a man in a suit has told her that the door is coming out.\n\n\"She knows?\" I said.\n\n\"Since last week.\"\n\n\"And she still opens?\"\n\n\"She has a lease.\"\n\n\"She has a lease.\" I wiped the counter even though it was clean. \"Good. Good for her.\"\n\nHe left. He left the envelope on the bar, but I already had one in the drawer, so there were two of the same notice in the room at the same time, which is a thing that happens in bureaucracies and in bad marriages.\n\n財務大臣 jumped onto the stool and put one paw on the envelope. Good cat. The cat understands documents better than I do.\n\n\n\nAt 23:00 I open the club. Rain. The password tonight is about a ship. I do not know why. Somebody in the group chat picks these and they have a sense of humour that is either very good or very bad and I cannot tell which.\n\nThe room was full. The cat was where he always was. Theo is on the scaffolding taping something that is not a joke, which is suspicious. Yusuf is standing by the wall with his hands out of his pockets, and he has a bag with him, which I do not ask about. Rin is behind the speakers with the screwdriver again, fixing a thing that is not broken. Cass is at a table with the camera in front of her and her hands in her lap. The old man in row three moves his glass a finger's width to the left.\n\nAt 00:17 the voice comes on. You are allowed.\n\nThe room keeps dancing.\n\nI stand behind the bar and watch hands and I think about Mrs. Saito, who has been told and who still opens, and who gives Theo coffee and tells him his makeup is suitable for office work, and who has never once asked me what is behind dryer four, although she must know, because she is a woman who has worked in a laundromat for twenty-two years and dryers do not open onto staircases by accident.\n\nShe knows everything. Everyone knows everything. Mrs. Saito folded a towel and did not ask about the stairs, and I wiped a counter that was already clean, and the dryer went around with somebody's underwear in it.\n\nAt 2:00 a woman comes to the bar and puts a glass down and says, \"I heard about April.\"\n\n\"Who told you?\"\n\n\"Everyone knows.\"\n\n\"Everyone knows nothing,\" I said. \"Everyone knows a date. That is not the same.\"\n\nShe wants me to say it will be okay, which is a sentence that should be removed from all languages, along with it gets better and think positive and you should forgive him.\n\nI gave her a drink on the house. This is not an answer. It is a drink.\n\n\n\nAt 3:12, after lights, after people, I take both notices out of the drawer and put them on the bar. Two identical pieces of paper. A thing said twice does not become more true, but it does become heavier.\n\nI take out the ledger and open page 214. The last lines are:\n\n2024 — someone came in.\n2024 — nobody left.\n2025 — still here.\n\nUnder them I write:\n\nApril — a date.\n\nThat's all. A date. If I write the end it becomes a story, and this is not a story, it is a lease with a number in it.\n\nThen I close the book and I put it back behind the bottles nobody orders, and I put the notices back in the drawer, and I turn off the television, which is showing baseball in February because the television has never once cared about months.\n\nI do not tell Rin. I do not tell Theo. I do not tell Yusuf or Cass or the man in row three who pays four thousand yen to leave.\n\nThey will find out from a room that is not there, which is the worst way to find out, and which is the only way, because a room is the only thing that can tell them.\n\nTomorrow is the twenty-second. I have thirty-eight days.\n\nThirty-eight is a good number. Even. Boring. A number that does not ask questions.\n\nI put the broken openers back in the jar. Forty-seven of them. None of them work. All of them have personality.\n\nGood night, everyone. Drink first."},{"title":"CHAPTER 22 — DELETE IT","text":"The cat stole a strip of wagyu from the bar at 1:11.\n\nNot a whole steak. A strip. The fat was very white and the cat put his face into it like a man into a pillow, and Mei shouted something in Taiwanese, and Theo stopped mid-sentence on the scaffolding to watch, and three customers got up to help, and the cat ran behind the speakers with the meat in his mouth and looked at me.\n\nThis is 財務大臣. He is the fattest cat in Shibuya and he has never once apologised.\n\nI laughed. I do not laugh often. It surprised me. My hand was on the cassette player at the time and I laughed into my own sleeve, and for four seconds the room was not a room with a deadline, it was a room with a cat.\n\nThen the cat ate the wagyu and the moment was over.\n\n\n\nAt 1:40 I found Cass by the table at the back with the camera in front of her and her hands in her lap.\n\n\"Come outside,\" I said.\n\nWe went into the alley. Three bins. One broken streetlight. No music. This is where true things are said and I had decided, walking from the room to the alley, that I would say one.\n\n\"I know you filmed me,\" I said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"On the first night. By the sink. Three minutes and eleven seconds.\"\n\nShe looked at me. She did not ask how I knew. This is one of her defects and one of her virtues: she expects to be found out.\n\n\"Delete it,\" I said.\n\n\"Rin —\"\n\n\"Delete it.\"\n\n\"I will.\" She said it in the voice she uses with people who have power over her, warm, efficient, already leaving. I have heard that voice from the other side of a counter. I know exactly what it is worth.\n\n\"Do it now,\" I said.\n\n\"It's in a folder. There are — there are other files in the folder. It's not a simple —\"\n\n\"Three minutes and eleven seconds. One file. Delete it.\"\n\n\"Rin, I need it.\"\n\nThere it is. The true thing. Not I didn't. Not I forgot. I need it.\n\nI stood under the broken light. A taxi went past on the main road, far away, and I looked at it and then away, and I thought about my mother's fifty thousand yen and my father's tie and the voice in the wall that I play to a room of people who do not know it is a man.\n\n\"You need it,\" I said.\n\n\"I know what I'm doing is extractive. I know. I can cite —\"\n\n\"Don't cite.\"\n\n\"I am aware of the power imbalance and I have written about it and I —\"\n\n\"Don't cite at me in an alley.\"\n\nShe stopped. The alley has no echo. Her words just ended in the air between the bins, which is a better fate than they deserved.\n\nThen she said, \"You are the only real thing in the footage.\"\n\n\"I am a person.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"No, you don't. You know an arrangement of textures. Wet hair. Grey cotton. Red knuckles. An object with a label you couldn't read.\" I said it flatly. I have been saving the sentence for two months and it came out without any heat, which is worse. \"That's not a person. That's a shot.\"\n\nShe put her hand on the wall. Not dramatic. Just a hand on wet concrete because she needed somewhere to put it.\n\n\"I am going to make this film,\" she said. \"I am going to make it whether you say yes or no, because I have a grant and a deadline and a woman I have never met who I have turned into a country. This is the truth. The other truth is that I have not filmed for two months and my hands shake when I don't, and I am frightened of a room full of people who have decided to be happy without me.\"\n\n\"Then be frightened in the room.\"\n\n\"I can't.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because I need the footage.\"\n\nThere it was again. The circle. A thing she says and then says again with different words, like a form that asks the same question twice in two languages.\n\nI looked at her. I have known people who kept things past their date. Vials. Cassettes. A grey sweatshirt. A tie in a glove compartment. I am an expert in things that are kept when they should be thrown away. But there is a difference between keeping a thing and refusing to throw it away when somebody asks you to, and the difference is a person standing in an alley saying delete it four times.\n\n\"Because you'll only show the part that bleeds,\" I said.\n\nShe did not answer.\n\n\"I have a scar,\" I said. \"You didn't film the scar. You filmed my hands. You would show my hands and tell people what they mean. You would show the vial box if I let you near it. You would put a song under it.\"\n\n\"That's not —\"\n\n\"That's exactly what you do. You find a person and you make them into a sentence.\"\n\nShe took her hand off the wall. She looked at me for a long time, and her face did the thing faces do when they are being edited from the inside, when a person is deciding which parts of themselves to keep.\n\n\"I know,\" she said.\n\n\"Then delete it.\"\n\n\"I can't.\"\n\n\"Then you are not a witness. You are a thief.\"\n\nI went back inside.\n\n\n\nThe room kept dancing. 財務大臣 was under the speakers with a small piece of wagyu stuck to his whisker. Theo was doing a number about a man who cannot fill in a form, which was not funny and which he did anyway. Yusuf was standing by the wall with his hands out. Mei was watching hands.\n\nAt 00:17 the voice came on. You are allowed.\n\nI turned it off.\n\nNot forever. There is a switch behind the speakers and I turned it off for eleven seconds and then I turned it back on, because the room needed it and because the room did not know what had happened in the alley and because I am not the person who decides what a room needs just because I am the one standing at the switch.\n\nEleven seconds. I counted.\n\nThen I put my hand back on the player, and I made myself do the thing with the screwdriver that is not fixing, and I watched Cass come back in and sit at the table and put her hands in her lap, and the camera was still in front of her.\n\nAt 3:03 I walked home. My mother was in Kobe with my money. My father was in a taxi with my voice. A woman in Oakland had three minutes and eleven seconds of me in a folder, and she needed it, which is the worst sentence I have heard in this country, and I have heard a lot of sentences.\n\nThe rain waited until I was halfway home and then started.\n\nI did not put my hood up. I have a jacket in a chair at home that is too big for me and smells of ginger, and I am still not giving it back, and nobody has asked me to.\n\nThis is the thing I have. Not the vials. Not the name in the box. Not the eleven phone calls. A jacket with a tear at the pocket and a man who asked instead of told.\n\nAt 3:20 I got home and put the seven pans on the counter and used the first one, which is the wrong one, and the roll was too thick and I ate it anyway.\n\nAt 3:40 I took my phone out and looked at the number for the city, which I know by heart, and I did not call it, because it is 3:40 and the form is closed, and because tomorrow is Saturday, and because I have decided not to pick something up, and today I am very good at it.\n\nTomorrow I will be worse."},{"title":"CHAPTER 23 — FORM","text":"The immigration office in Shinagawa is beige in the way only a building designed by a committee can be beige — twelve plastic chairs, nine occupied, a woman with a printer that sounds like a small animal in distress, a man asleep standing up, which I did not know was possible until this country decided to teach me, and a child with a red backpack who looked at my sunglasses and then looked away, which is the correct response to a man in sunglasses indoors at nine in the morning.\n\nI took ticket B-17. The screen said B-03. This is a country that gives you a number before it gives you a chance, which is very fair really, because if they gave me a chance first I would only use it to say something funny and then we would all be in trouble.\n\nI fill in the form.\n\nSurname: A-d-e-y-e-m-i.\nGiven names: Theo.\nDate of birth: 14/06/2000.\nNationality: British.\nPassport number: copied twice. Wrong the first time. Correct the second.\nAddress: 4-8-2 Maruyamacho, Shibuya-ku. The room with the leak. I write it without the leak.\nEmployment: none.\nSponsor: none.\nPurpose of extension: performance.\n\nI stop at performance. I write it again. The pen is a cheap pen from a convenience store and it presses too hard.\n\nPurpose of extension: performance.\n\nThe screen says B-09.\n\nA woman at desk two calls a name. Not mine. Another. A man gets up and goes and comes back ten minutes later with a stamp on a paper and a face like a man who has been let out of something.\n\nB-12. B-13. B-14.\n\nThe child with the red backpack drops a crust of bread. The mother does not see. I pick it up and put it on the bin. The child watches me do this. I do not smile. This is not a thing I decided. It is a thing my face did.\n\nB-16.\n\nB-17.\n\nThe officer calls my name. She says: Tee-oh Add-ee-em.\n\nI say, \"Yes.\"\n\nShe says: Tee-oh Add-ee-yem.\n\nI say, \"Yes.\"\n\nShe looks at the form. She says: Tee-oh A-day-me.\n\nI say, \"Yes.\"\n\nThis is three. After three you stop correcting, which is a rule I have learned in nine months and which is the same rule as don't explain the joke twice, except that in this room the joke is your passport and nobody is laughing. After three, you take the name they give you and you carry it out of the room and you do not say it to anyone on the train, because on the train someone might ask you to spell it, and then you would have to do the whole thing again with a different vowel, and I have a show on Saturday and a very limited supply of vowels.\n\nShe checks the passport against the form. She checks the form against the passport. She uses a ruler, or something like a ruler, a small clear plastic thing with a line on it. She puts the line under my date of birth.\n\n\"Working,\" she says.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Working no.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Money.\"\n\nI show her the bank app. Four hundred and eleven thousand yen. The money is from Mei, who pays me for shows in cash in an envelope with no words on it, and from two Tuesdays at a bar in Shimokitazawa that I did not tell her about, and from a man who tipped me four thousand yen in a basement in Shibuya and told me nothing.\n\nShe looks at the screen. She looks at me. She looks at the sunglasses, which I have not taken off, which is a mistake I know I am making and which I am making anyway.\n\n\"Why glasses.\"\n\n\"Medical.\"\n\n\"Medical.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nShe writes something. I cannot see what. The form has a box that is empty and she does not fill it and does not tick it, and the empty box is the loudest thing in the room, which is impressive, considering the printer is still going and that small animal is really putting its back into it. An empty box is a very British thing, by the way. It's the national gift. We give them to people at airports and then we say nothing to declare and we mean everything.\n\n\"Address,\" she says. \"This address is share.\"\n\n\"No. One room.\"\n\n\"One room.\" She writes. \"Landlord know you live.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Contract.\"\n\nI have no contract. I have a text message from a man called Sato with a photograph of a leak and the words your fault in Japanese, which I have translated four times and which I now know means something else entirely, which is the rain is your fault, which is a joke, I think, or a statement of weather.\n\nI put the phone on the desk. She looks at the photograph. She looks at the leak. She looks at my face in the screen reflection.\n\n\"This is not contract.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nShe writes. The pen makes a small sound. The woman with the printer prints something. The child with the red backpack sits on the floor and plays with the crust of bread.\n\nShe says a sentence in Japanese. I know six words. I know form, Tuesday, please, more, wait, no. She says more and Tuesday and a word I do not know, and then she says, in English, carefully, \"Twenty-eight February. Bring paper from landlord. Bring paper from —\" she stops and looks at the sunglasses again — \"from doctor.\"\n\n\"Doctor.\"\n\n\"Yes. For glasses.\"\n\n\"It's not —\"\n\n\"Twenty-eight February.\"\n\nShe puts the form in a tray. The tray has six forms in it. Mine is the seventh. She calls the next number. B-18 does not come. A man with a grey coat stands up from the row behind me and goes to the desk and sits down where I was sitting.\n\nI take my passport. I take the receipt with the date on it. I put the sunglasses back on, which I had taken off for a moment and do not remember taking off.\n\nOutside. Steps. A man smoking by the door. A woman with a pram. The train signs are in four languages and I read all four and none of them say what I am doing here.\n\nAt 3:07 I am outside WASH & GO. The password tonight is about a letter. I say it wrong. Momo looks at me. Behind him a woman's voice says, \"Let him in. Any letter. Let him in.\"\n\nInside. Bass. Pink light. Mei behind the bar. Rin behind the speakers. Yusuf by the wall. The old man in row three moves his glass a finger's width to the left.\n\nMei puts a glass in front of me.\n\n\"Ginger,\" she says.\n\nI drink. The receipt is in my pocket. It has a date on it like the vials have a date on it, which is to say: a real date, not a feeling, not a maybe.\n\nAt 00:17 the voice comes on. You are allowed.\n\nI take the receipt out and look at it under the pink light. February twenty-eight. Fifteen days from the show. Five weeks from the end of the building as the notice still says.\n\nI put it back in my pocket and go to the scaffolding, because there is a show to do and the cardboard officer is retired and someone has to be funny in a room that has a date in it.\n\nThey did not stamp the paper. They gave me a date instead, which is the immigration service's way of saying we'll get back to you, which is the British way of saying we've already forgotten you, except that here they write it down, which is rude but efficient, and I respect efficiency in a country that has made a fine art of making me wait."},{"title":"CHAPTER 24 — TWENTY MINUTES","text":"Valentine's Day in this country is a thing women buy chocolate for men they do not like, which is a custom I have never understood and which I have no intention of understanding at fifty.\n\nIn 梅屋 at 5 p.m. there were three customers and one of them was hiding from his wife and one of them was asleep and one of them was a tourist taking photographs of the jar of broken openers. The tourist asked me if I was alone. I said yes. He said, you should be with someone today. I said, I am with forty-seven openers, all of them useless, all of them mine. He took a photograph of the jar and left.\n\nAt 9:40 a woman from the group chat sent a message. Four words. They got a tip.\n\nAt 9:41 another. Twenty minutes.\n\nTwenty minutes is a short time and a long time. In twenty minutes you can empty a room of seventy-one people and one cat, or you can stand behind a bar and watch hands and do neither.\n\nI did not call anyone. There is no phone tree in this club. This is not an accident. A club with a phone tree is a club with a list, and a list is a thing the police can take, and I have been in a country sixteen years and I know what a list is.\n\nI went down the stairs. Dryer four. The password was a letter does not arrive if nobody sends it, which is a sentence someone in the group chat wrote at 9:43 and which is too long for a password and which nobody said correctly, and Momo let everybody in anyway, because he could see my face and my face said twenty minutes.\n\n\n\nAt 2:39 the bass stopped. I did not stop it. The DJ stopped it, because he saw the same thing I saw through the crack in the door at the top of the stairs: a torch, moving in the laundromat.\n\nTwenty minutes had become seventeen. Or nine. Time does this when a room is in trouble. It does not go forward. It goes sideways.\n\n\"The alley,\" I said.\n\nI did not shout. I did not need to. I have worked eleven years behind this bar and I have one voice for customers and one voice for emergencies and they are almost the same voice, which is why people obey the second one.\n\nTheo came off the scaffolding. Rin's hand came off the cassette player. Yusuf moved away from the wall. Cass put the camera in her bag without being told, which I noticed, and which I put in the ledger later, in my head, because there was no time for a real book.\n\n\"The alley. Now. No phones. Walk, don't run. Momo, front.\"\n\nMomo, who never speaks, nodded once and opened the door behind the bar that goes to the alley, which is not on any plan of this building and which I put in myself in 2016 with a crowbar and a man who was paid in drinks.\n\nSeventy-one people went through a door two metres wide in four minutes. This is a thing I did not think was possible and which is possible when a room understands the word go.\n\n\n\nThe cat was not going.\n\n財務大臣 was on the bar, sitting on a clean glass with his whole fat grey body, and when I reached for him he hissed, which he has never done in his life.\n\n\"Cat,\" I said.\n\nTheo came back from the alley door. \"I can't believe this cat.\"\n\n\"Take the cat.\"\n\n\"I'm scared of the cat.\"\n\n\"Take the goddamn cat.\"\n\nHe picked up the cat, and the cat put all four feet into his chest and screamed, which is a sound I have never heard from a cat and which I will hear for the rest of my life, and the cat knocked over a bottle of something and the bottle broke and somebody swore in Tagalog.\n\nThis took eight minutes.\n\nEight minutes is a long time. In eight minutes a room can empty, and a room can fill with police, and a room can do both at once, which is what happened, because at 2:52 the torch came through the stairs and at 2:53 three officers were in the room and at 2:54 they were looking at the broken bottle and a cat who was still screaming in the arms of a very tall man in a wig who had forgotten to take the wig off.\n\nI put the night in the ledger. I wrote only what happened. The cat. The bottle. The time. The rest I left out, which is how I have survived eleven years behind this bar.\n\nThe cat went home that night too. He did not know he had done anything. This is the whole difference between a cat and a woman.\n\n\n\nThey asked for the owner.\n\nI said, \"I am the bartender.\"\n\n\"Owner.\"\n\n\"I am the bartender.\"\n\nA woman officer looked at the bar. At the bottles. At the sink with three metres of stainless steel and cups drying on the rack. At the ledger, which was not on the bar, which was behind the bottles nobody orders, which she did not find.\n\n\"Name.\"\n\n\"Mei Ling.\"\n\n\"Not here,\" she said. This is a sentence said by people who have decided you have a name that belongs somewhere else.\n\n\"Here is my name,\" I said. \"Not here is my mother's name. Different thing.\"\n\nShe wrote something. The pen was like Theo's pen. Cheap. Presses too hard.\n\nThey checked the fire exits. One. They checked the capacity. Forty-eight. They counted the people. One.\n\nMe.\n\n\"Everybody left,\" she said.\n\n\"At three,\" I said. \"You are early.\"\n\nShe looked at the clock. It said 2:57.\n\nAt three exactly the lights came on. All of them. No warning, no countdown. One face gets ten years older in one second, and my face is the only one in the room, so there is nobody to compare it to, which is a particular kind of loneliness I had not expected.\n\nThe glue on the stage cracked. A dry field. Theo's wig was on the floor by the alley door, left behind in the rush, and under the fluorescent light it looked like a small dead animal.\n\nI picked it up. I put it on the stool in the mirror room. I do not know why I did this. I have never done anything like it in my life.\n\n\n\nThey took the ledger.\n\nNo — they did not. I want to be exact, because exactness is the only thing I have left tonight. They took the book from behind the bar, which is a different book, a book with money in it, with numbers and dates and a bank account with a name like a small grey animal. They did not take the other one, which is under the sink behind the ginger, which I put there with my left hand while the woman officer was writing with hers.\n\nMoney is easy to take. A ledger of who showed up is not. You have to know it exists.\n\nThey left at 3:31. They did not arrest me. They wrote a number on a paper and gave me the number and said a word that means cooperation and a word that means henceforth, and the second word is much heavier than the first.\n\nI sat on a stool behind the bar. The room was very quiet. A room with seventy-one people in it is loud. A room with one person in it is a different room entirely, and it is the same room.\n\nAt 3:40 I took out the real ledger and opened page 214. The last line was April — a date. Under it I wrote:\n\n2025.02.14 — a bottle broke.\n\nThen I closed it and put it back behind the ginger, and I washed the glasses, all of them, one by one, and I did not look at the clock, because the clock and I have an understanding and the understanding is over.\n\nAt 4:10 I turned off the lights. Not at three. Later. For once, later.\n\nThis is the first rule I have ever broken in my own club, and I broke it alone, and nobody clapped.\n\n財務大臣 came out from under the speakers and sat on the stool beside me and put one paw on my forearm. Good cat.\n\nFine. The cat can have the last word too."},{"title":"CHAPTER 25 — CELL","text":"Mei spent the night in a cell.\n\nI found out at 6:40 from a man called Chen who works at 梅屋 on Mondays and who has a face like a folded receipt. He called me because he did not know who else to call, and because Mei had written my number on a piece of paper taped to the register under the word if.\n\n\"She is okay,\" he said. \"She is at the station. They kept her one night.\"\n\n\"One night.\"\n\n\"Yes. One night. She has cough.\"\n\nI put on the same sweatshirt and went to the station. It is a thing I do without deciding. The pharmacy is closed on Saturday until nine and I have the key and I did not take the key, which is a detail I notice later, in the train, when I am already halfway to Nakano.\n\n\n\nMei's father was on the floor.\n\nNot dead. On the floor. This is a thing I have learned to distinguish in the last year: the difference between a body that is still here and a body that has gone somewhere else. He was still here. He was in his bathrobe with one shoe on and the other shoe under the table, and he had fallen getting out of the bath, and there was a small cut above his eyebrow that was not bleeding much but was the right shape to worry about.\n\nHe was looking at me when I came in.\n\n\"妹妹,\" he said. \"Have you eaten?\"\n\n\"I ate.\"\n\n\"No,\" he said. \"You don't eat. She doesn't eat.\"\n\nWho she was, I do not know. Me, probably. Or his wife. Or his sister. In this family the word for you moves around the room like a person looking for a seat.\n\nI called an ambulance. I called the clinic. I called Chen back and told him Mei's father had fallen and Chen said, in a very small voice, \"I cannot go to the hospital. I have the bar.\"\n\nThe bar. The bar is open at three and closed at six and Chen is sixty-one years old and has a mortgage, and there is no one else.\n\n\"I'll go,\" I said.\n\n\"You work at nine.\"\n\n\"I'll go.\"\n\nI sat with the old man on the floor for eleven minutes until the ambulance came. He held my wrist — the left one, the inside, where the scar is — and he held it with a strength that a man who cannot stand still has, and he said nothing else, and I did not pull away, which is a thing I would have done a year ago.\n\n\n\nAt the hospital a doctor used the word for observation and the word for unfortunately and a number that was not a number, which is the way doctors say there is no number. I have heard this sentence in three rooms in the last six months. It is always the same sentence.\n\nMei came at 11:20. She had a cough and a paper with a number on it and a face that had decided nothing that has been opened from the outside and does not approve.\n\n\"Don't look at me like that,\" she said.\n\n\"I'm not.\"\n\n\"You are looking with the whole face.\"\n\n\"I'm looking with one eye. The other one is for the vial box.\"\n\n\"Good.\" She sat down. \"Your eye is very busy today.\"\n\nWe sat in the hospital corridor. The chairs were hard. There was a television showing a drama in which a woman was crying in a hospital corridor, which is a thing television does when it wants you to be comforted by a mirror.\n\n\"One night,\" she said.\n\n\"Chen told me.\"\n\n\"One night in a room with a woman who cried. Not me. Her. She cried for two hours. I told her to stop. Then she stopped and asked me why I was in a cell, and I said, because I own a room that closes at three, and she said, that's a nice room, and then she slept.\" Mei coughed. \"The cough is from the crying woman. Or the cell. Different.\"\n\n\"Your father fell.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"He is okay.\"\n\n\"Yes.\" She looked at the door with his name on it. \"He was trying to get out of the bath to make rice. For me. He has not made rice in nine years.\" She put the paper down. \"He wanted to make rice.\"\n\nI did not say anything. There is a thing you say in this situation in English and in Japanese and in Taiwanese, and it is the wrong thing in all three, and I know it, and I keep my mouth shut, which I am very good at.\n\nAt 12:10 she said, \"He called me sister today.\"\n\n\"Yesterday.\"\n\n\"Today. Every day now.\" She stood up. \"Come on. I need to go to a bar that is failing and open it, because a man is hiding from his wife and he has nowhere else to go.\"\n\n\n\nAt 23:00 the club was open. Mei behind the bar with the cough and the paper in her pocket. Seventy-one people, one cat. The cat came out from under the speakers and sat on the bar and looked at Mei and then at me and then at Mei again, and Mei said, \"Don't. You already had your night.\"\n\nTheo was on the scaffolding doing a number about a form. He has stopped calling it a joke, and the room has stopped laughing at it in the way you laugh at a joke, and started laughing at it in the way you laugh at a wound you recognise. Yusuf was by the wall with his hands out. Cass was at a table with the camera in her bag, which is where it has been for two months.\n\nAt 00:17 I pressed play. The voice came on. You are allowed.\n\nThe room kept dancing.\n\nI looked at the door behind the bar that goes to the alley and I thought about a taxi parked on Maruyamacho that I have seen twice and never once approached, and I thought about a tie in a glove compartment, and I thought about a man who had a voice before I had a name, and I put my hand on the player and I did not pick anything up.\n\nAt 2:11 I went outside for air. The alley. Three bins. Broken light. The cat followed me, which he does now, which is new.\n\nA taxi was parked at the end of the street. Not close. Far enough that the sign was small.\n\nI stood under the broken light and looked at it for a while. The taxi did not move. Nobody got out. The window was down a finger's width, which I could not see from where I was, but which I have decided was down a finger's width, because that is the kind of detail I invent when I have decided not to ask a question.\n\n\"Otsukaresama,\" I said, quietly, to the taxi.\n\nThe word came out correctly. That was the strange part. I stood in an alley and said a word in my own language and it went nowhere, and the taxi did not answer, which is what taxis do.\n\nThe taxi did not answer. At three the lights came on inside, and through the wall you could hear the room change, and I went back in, because the room needed me at the switch, and because there is a difference between standing outside a door and going in, and I have spent my whole life standing outside doors.\n\nNot tonight. Tonight I am a woman with seven pans and a vial box and a mother in Kobe who is waiting for one word, and I have already said it to an alley, which does not count, and to a taxi, which cannot hear, and to a room that has heard a voice in a language most of it does not speak for four months.\n\nOne person. That's all I want. One.\n\nI am standing next to the person. I do not know yet."},{"title":"CHAPTER 26 — PARKED","text":"Rain. Saturday. 2:03 a.m.\n\nI park. Engine off. Meter off. This is the third night.\n\nThe laundromat is open. A woman inside folds towels. She sees me through the glass and I know that she sees me, because she looks once and then she looks again, which is a thing people do when a thing is familiar and not explained.\n\nI do not get out.\n\n\n\nMy son called at 1:30. I did not answer.\n\nHe called again at 1:34. Then a message.\n\nEight p.m. Tuesday. Station. I made rice.\n\nI read it three times. I put the phone face down on the passenger seat, next to the blue tie, which is where I keep it now, in the glove compartment, with the map and the wipes.\n\nThe tie is in the glove compartment. I did not go.\n\nI have never gone.\n\n\n\nAt 2:11 a woman comes out of the club into the alley. Grey sweatshirt. Wet hair. Small. She stands under the broken light with a cat at her feet.\n\nShe looks at my car. I know that she looks at my car. A person can look at a car in two ways: at the number, or at the man in it. She is looking at the man.\n\nI do not move.\n\nShe says something. I cannot hear it through the window and the rain. Her mouth moves. The cat sits.\n\nThis is the third time. On Christmas she looked and went back inside. Tonight she stands there for nine minutes.\n\nNine minutes with the meter off is a strange thing. A meter at zero is a thing that is waiting. A man at zero is the same.\n\nThe voice comes on inside. I know it is on because the wall changes. I cannot hear the words from here, only the shape of them, which comes through concrete better than through air and which I do not name.\n\nShe puts her hand on the wall of the building. Not on the door. On the wall. Then she wipes her face with her sleeve and goes back inside.\n\nThe cat stays. The cat looks at my car for a while and then goes in after her.\n\n\n\n\nMy rating is 4.8. One review. I have read it every month for three years, and tonight I thought about writing a reply, which I would not do, because a reply is a thing a person writes when they want to be known, and I do not want to be known, I want to be called something else by one person, which is a different thing and which I will not ask for.\n\nThe son said mother. Twice. I did not correct him.\n\nShe said the word to a taxi tonight. I could not hear it. I know what it was, because I know the shape of a person's mouth saying a thing to a car they think is empty.\n\nI have a tie in the glove compartment. I have a voice in a wall. I have a daughter standing in an alley saying a word to a man who will not open the window.\n\nI do not open the window.\n\nAt three the music stops. The lights come on somewhere I cannot see. The street becomes a street again. The cleaning truck comes and brushes the gutters. I watch it pass.\n\nI put the heat on. Then I turn it off. A man who does not sleep does not need heat.\n\nMeter: zero.\n\nI sit here until 3:40. Then I start the car and drive, because a taxi with the meter off is a room with nobody in it, and I have sat in that room long enough.\n\nAt 3:44 the meter moves. 180 yen. 240. 320.\n\nA number is a thing you can trust. It starts at zero and it goes up and it does not care what you did before.\n\nI drive until morning. No passengers. This is allowed. This is what I do.\n\nI have never gone in."},{"title":"CHAPTER 27 — RELEASE","text":"Two weeks ago they let me out at 9:40 on Saturday with a paper and a cough and no apology. This is a country that does not apologise. It cooperates. It gives you a number and a word that means henceforth, and you put them in your pocket next to nothing and you go home.\n\nHome is 梅屋, which is a failing bar that opens at three and closes at six and has three customers, one of whom is hiding from his wife. On Friday there are two. The tourist who photographs the jar of broken openers has gone, which is a mercy, and the man who sleeps has been replaced by a woman who drinks alone and watches the door, which is the same customer in a different coat.\n\n\"Good morning,\" I said.\n\n\"Is it morning?\" she said.\n\n\"No.\" I poured. \"It is a bar. Time is a customer. It can come in whenever it wants.\"\n\n\n\nMy father is in a room on the third floor of a hospital in Nakano with a view of another building. He has one shoe on at home and no shoes here, and he keeps asking the nurse for his cardigan, which is in the bath, in the water, where I put it back because he told me not to take it, because he is a man who has decided that the wet thing is his and nobody can touch it.\n\nI go every day at 4:30, after the bar, before the club. I shave his head with a razor and I do not take the cardigan. He holds still for me. He has started saying thank you in Japanese, which he never used to say, because Japanese was for customers.\n\nOn Friday he called me by my name.\n\n\"Mei Ling,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Where is my sister.\"\n\n\"I am here.\"\n\n\"No.\" He looked at me with his own eyes, which are still his eyes, and he said something in Taiwanese about a door, and then the door went somewhere else in his head and he was gone for twenty minutes.\n\nHe came back. He said, \"Have you eaten?\"\n\n\"I ate.\"\n\n\"Don't lie to your mother.\"\n\n\"She is dead.\"\n\n\"Then lie to me.\"\n\nWe looked at each other. He laughed. It is the first time he has laughed in four years and it is because I said something true and he decided it was a joke, which is the way most things are said in this family.\n\n\n\nA notice says 1 April, 8 a.m. I put it on the bar and read it again on Monday at 4 p.m. with the baseball on the television and the woman watching the door.\n\nThe twentieth is thirty-four days from the raid. Ninety-two days from when the son first put an envelope on this bar. I have been counting since November and I have lost count twice and started again, which is a thing people do with dates that matter.\n\nTheo came in at 5 with a cold that has lasted two months and a piece of paper in his hand. The receipt from the immigration office. February 28. Fifteen days from the show.\n\n\"I need a letter from my landlord,\" he said.\n\n\"I am not your landlord.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"The man who texts you photographs of the leak is your landlord.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Ask him.\"\n\n\"He says the leak is my fault.\"\n\n\"Then fix the leak.\"\n\n\"I am a drag queen.\"\n\n\"So fix the leak.\"\n\nHe put his head on the bar, which is a thing he does, and I put a glass of ginger beside it, which is a thing I do.\n\n\"ORIGINS,\" he said, into the bar. \"The last Saturday. March fifteenth.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"No cameras. No phones. Forty-eight people.\"\n\n\"Seventy-one.\"\n\n\"Forty-eight on the permit.\"\n\n\"Seventy-one in the room.\" I wiped the counter. \"This is the last one. Do it properly.\"\n\nHe lifted his head. His eye makeup from last night was still on, which he had not washed off, which is a thing people do when they have no mirror at home that isn't also a sink.\n\n\"Properly how?\"\n\n\"You know how.\"\n\n\"No. Say it.\"\n\nI do not say things like this. I do not say you were good or I am proud or this mattered, because those are sentences that belong to a mother, and I have had a mother and I have a father who calls me sister and I know what sentences can do to a person.\n\nBut the man is lying on my bar with last night's eyeliner on his face and today's appointment about a form behind him, and the room is closing on the twentieth, and the voice in the wall is a man I have known for eleven years without knowing his name, and my father is upstairs in a hospital asking where his sister is.\n\n\"Like you mean it,\" I said. \"That's how.\"\n\nHe looked at me. Then he said, \"Right.\"\n\n\"Right.\"\n\n\"Properly.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe sat up and drank the ginger. Then he said, \"What if I forget and do the joke?\"\n\n\"Then I will throw something.\"\n\n\"You have forty-seven broken openers.\"\n\n\"I have excellent aim.\"\n\nHe laughed. I laughed. This is the last thing I want to say about this conversation: it was funny, and it was the truest thing either of us said all month, and neither of us has repeated it, which is why it is still here.\n\n\n\nAt 23:00 I open. Rain. The password is about a house. I do not know who picks these. I suspect Rin, who has a face like a person who knows everything and has decided not to say, but she denies it every time I ask, which is a denial with too much detail, which is a thing that means yes.\n\nThe room was full. The cat was where he always was. The old man in row three moves his glass a finger's width to the left. At 00:17 the voice comes on and the room keeps dancing.\n\nI stand behind the bar and watch hands.\n\nYusuf's hands: cement, always, but he has stopped touching the wall. Rin's hands: on the cassette player, then off, then on. Cass's hands: in her lap. Theo's hands: still, before the show, for the first time since I met him.\n\nMy hands: fine.\n\nMy hands are fine. This is the least interesting thing about me.\n\nAt 3:12 I take the ledger out and open page 214. The last lines:\n\n2025.02.14 — a bottle broke.\nApril — a date.\n\nUnder them I write one line. I write it slowly, because a line in this book is a bill, and I am the one who pays it, and I have paid it every month on the twenty-eighth for ten years, and this is the last one I will pay in this room.\n\nMarch 15 — one show. No cameras.\n\nThen I close the book and put it back behind the bottles nobody orders, and I turn off the television, which is showing baseball in February because the television has never once cared about months.\n\nNinety-two days is a long time if you are counting forward from a door closing. It is a very short time if you are counting backward from a room that has to be emptied.\n\nI have never been good at arithmetic. I am excellent at doors."},{"title":"CHAPTER 28 — NINETY-FOUR SECONDS","text":"Deletion is not a clean act. This is the first thing I learned. People talk about deleting footage as if it were a moral gesture with a beginning and an end, like signing a cheque or leaving a room. It is not. It is a series of small decisions about which folders to open, and in each folder there is a face, and each face is a reason not to delete the next one.\n\nI started at 4 p.m. in a 4.5-tatami room in Shibuya with a ceiling leak and a landlord who texts photographs of the leak. I had forty-one minutes of footage in one folder and one selfie I could not delete in another. This is what I had instead of a mother, which is a sentence I have been building for twenty-six years and have finally said out loud in an empty room with a plastic cup of water on the floor.\n\nThe folder was called ROUGH. Not subjects. Not the people. ROUGH, which is a word that means not finished and also not careful, and I have been both.\n\nI opened it.\n\nRin at the sink. Three minutes and eleven seconds. Wet hair, grey cotton, red knuckles, an object with a label I could not read.\n\nI put my finger on the delete button. Then I took it off. Then I put it on again.\n\nThis is the whole moral architecture of my life, repeated in private, with no witness, which is worse than doing it in front of someone, because there is nobody to stop me and nobody to forgive me.\n\n\n\nI have a theory about why I could not delete it. The theory is this: I did not want to keep her. I wanted to keep the version of myself who was capable of taking it, because that version is young and certain and has a grant, and I have been nothing but uncertain for two months, and certainty is a substance people become addicted to in their twenties.\n\nThe second theory is worse and shorter: I wanted the footage.\n\nI am aware of the literature. I can cite four scholars. I can cite myself. This is the problem with self-awareness: it is a very comfortable place to live and the footage is still there to be deleted.\n\nAt 7 p.m. I deleted Rin's clip.\n\nIt took eleven seconds. When it was gone I put my hand on the table and sat there for a while, and the room did not change, and I did not become a better person, which is the thing I had been secretly waiting for, the small click, the moral click, the sound of a lock opening.\n\nThere was no click. This is the truth I would put in the grant report if I were honest, which I am not.\n\nThen I deleted everything else.\n\nYusuf by the wall, hands out of pockets, four minutes and eight seconds. Theo on the scaffolding with a broom, ninety seconds of a joke about a form which was not a joke. Mei behind the bar watching hands, two minutes and forty seconds, in which she does not say a word and looks at six different glasses and nothing else. The old man in row three with his glass moving a finger's width to the left, which I did not know I had filmed and which appeared in the folder like a small accusation.\n\nI deleted the sound of a room I cannot get back. This is the other thing nobody tells you about deletion: it is not the image that hurts, it is the room tone. The bass under everything. The voice coming through the wall saying a sentence in English to a room that does not need translation.\n\nI kept one file.\n\nNinety-four seconds. I have not shown it to anyone. I will not show it to Rin. I have told myself this is because she does not want to see it, which is true, and because showing it would make it about me again, which is also true, and because I am frightened of what is on it, which is the only true one that matters.\n\n\n\nI took the SD card out of the camera at 9 p.m. and held it in my hand under the desk lamp. A small grey rectangle. It weighs nothing. It has held forty-one minutes of people who did not agree to be in my film, and now it holds ninety-four seconds, and I have carried a thing around for four months that I kept telling myself was different from using it.\n\nIt is not different. It was never different. This is the sentence I have been walking toward for four months and I have finally arrived, and there is no room at the end of it, just a desk lamp and a leak in the ceiling and a plastic cup of water on the floor.\n\nI put the card in an envelope. I wrote nothing on the outside, because a name on an envelope is a claim, and I am trying to stop making claims. Then I took a cloth napkin from my bag — from an aeroplane, from the flight in, from nine thousand miles up when I was still calling this project Tokyo and still believed a place could be a method — and I wrapped the card in it.\n\nI wrote a note. It took me forty minutes to write one sentence.\n\nI was looking for my mother. I'm sorry I used your face to do it.\n\nI put the note in the envelope. I did not sign it. This is not anonymity. This is the smallest possible version of I am sorry that I can send without asking for anything back, which is a distinction I have spent twenty-six years failing to understand and which I finally understand now, in a 4.5-tatami room in Shibuya at 9:40 p.m., holding an envelope like a small animal that might bite.\n\n\n\nAt 23:30 I went to the club with the envelope in my pocket.\n\nThe password tonight is about a window — the one about a letter. I said it wrong. Momo held out his hand. Behind him a woman's voice said, \"Let her in. Any letter. Let her in.\"\n\nInside. The room was full. The cat was where he always was. Theo on the scaffolding with no sunglasses on and no joke at the end of the number, which I noticed, and which I did not film. Rin behind the speakers with her hand on the cassette player, pressing a button twice, once to do the thing and once to make sure it had been done.\n\nI stood at the back for a while with my hands in my pockets, which is where they have been for a month, and then I walked to the sink and put the envelope on the stainless steel next to her.\n\nShe looked at it. Then at me.\n\n\"The card,\" I said. \"It's yours. All of it. What's left.\"\n\nShe did not pick it up.\n\n\"I deleted the rest,\" I said. \"I kept ninety-four seconds. I don't know what's on it. I mean — I know what's on it, but I haven't watched it since the night I took it, and I am not going to, and I am not going to tell you what I think is on it because that would be another thing I took from you.\"\n\nShe put her hand on the envelope. She did not open it.\n\n\"Why ninety-four?\" she said.\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"Try.\"\n\nI thought about it. The room was loud. The pink light was cutting her face in half and she was standing very still and waiting, which she does, which she is good at, which I have spent four months turning into footage without once understanding that it was a skill and not a texture.\n\n\"Because it's the length of a song,\" I said. \"No. Because it's the only part where the room is quiet and you're not in frame. You're off to the left. You're — you're looking at someone, and the camera caught the direction of your eyes and not your face.\" I stopped. \"It's not about you. That's why I kept it. I kept the part where you were looking at something else.\"\n\nShe looked at me for a long time. Then she picked up the envelope and put it in the pocket of the too-big sweatshirt.\n\n\"I don't forgive you,\" she said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Don't say I understand.\"\n\n\"I won't.\"\n\n\"Don't cite anything.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Don't tell me about your mother again.\"\n\n\"Okay.\"\n\nShe turned back to the cassette player. Then, after a while, she said, \"Ninety-four seconds.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"That's a long time.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Longer than four hours.\"\n\nI did not answer. She had taken my one good sentence and turned it around, which is what people do when they have been filmed and have decided to speak, and I deserved it, and I am going to carry it for a long time.\n\nAt 00:17 the voice came on. You are allowed.\n\nThe room kept dancing.\n\nI stood at the sink with my hands empty and my camera in my bag, and for the first time in four months I did not reach for either. My hands stayed empty. This is not growth. It is a fact. Facts are the only things I trust now, and I have very few of them.\n\nOn the street at 3:14, the cleaning truck going past, the rain starting again in the way rain starts here — not falling so much as being decided — I took out my phone and opened the foundation's email and typed one line in reply.\n\nI am no longer able to complete this project.\n\nI sent it before I could read it again.\n\nThen I walked to the station with my hands in my pockets, and I kept them there, and I have kept them there every night since, except when I am holding a cup or a pen or somebody's hand, which is a different thing entirely, and which I am learning."},{"title":"CHAPTER 29 — GOING HOME","text":"I am going home on the twenty-third. Four days after the last night.\n\nI have eleven months in this country and I spent nine of them on a building I will never enter. Floor thirty-one. Forty-seven floors. I put the steel where the steel goes, and the building is still standing, and I am going back to a house in Bekasi with a blue tarp over a motorbike and a goat that eats shoes.\n\nAldi got a message. Not a visa. A message from his mother, who says the goat is fat and the roof leaks and when is he coming home. This is how a life is decided: not with a form, but with a goat and a roof.\n\n\"I can come back,\" he said, over the phone. \"I can apply again. In Indonesia. From there.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Or you can stay.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"You want to stay?\"\n\n\"I want to go home.\"\n\n\"Then go home.\" He was quiet for a bit. The goat was loud in the background. \"But take the dress.\"\n\n\n\nThe dress is in a bag under my bed. I have never washed it. There is cement on the left boot still, on the dress where the boot touched it when I folded it in the mirror room that night, and I have decided not to wash it out, which is a stupid decision and which I am keeping.\n\nOn the night I danced, Theo gave me his sunglasses. Not on stage. In the mirror room, afterwards, when I was taking the dress off and he was sitting on the floor against the mirror with his face in his hands.\n\n\"These are medical,\" he said. \"Don't tell anyone I gave you medical glasses.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because then people will think I'm generous, and I have a reputation to protect.\"\n\nI said, \"You are generous.\"\n\n\"I am a nightmare.\"\n\n\"Both.\"\n\nHe put the sunglasses in my hand and closed my fingers over them, which is a thing he does, closing people's fingers, as if a gift is a thing that needs help arriving.\n\nIn the weeks after that I kept them on the shelf in the mirror room with the old hairpin and the paper that says also come back after. I did not wear them. They are too dark for a room with no windows. I took them out once and held them up to the light and saw nothing through them, which is the whole idea, and which is a thing I am learning about men who hide things in plain sight.\n\n\n\nAt 6 p.m. I went to Rin's room. Six tatami. One bed. One fridge with seven vials in a box and a bottle of ginger that Mei had given her, which she does not drink, which I have noticed. A whole shelf of tamagoyaki pans, seven of them, and she told me the purposes, which I did not understand and which I liked.\n\nOne for the first roll, which is always wrong.\nOne for the second, which is always correct.\nOne for the days she does not eat.\nThe others are for weather.\n\nShe cooked on the second pan. She always cooks on the second pan now when I am there, which is a thing I have noticed and which I have decided not to mention, which is a new skill.\n\nWe ate standing up, because there is no table that is not also a bed or a desk or a place for pans.\n\n\"My flight is on the twenty-third,\" I said.\n\n\"Four days after.\"\n\n\"Four days after.\"\n\nShe put a piece of omelette in my hand. It was too sweet, like always.\n\n\"Will you come back?\" she said.\n\n\"I don't know.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"Why good?\"\n\n\"Because if you say yes, you will be lying, and I don't want a lie in my room.\" She took a bite. \"My mother is in Kobe. I have not spoken to her in four years. She sends money. I call it insurance. She calls it insurance. We are both lying and neither of us is happy.\"\n\n\"You should call her.\"\n\n\"Do not tell me what to do.\"\n\n\"I am asking.\"\n\nShe looked at me. Then she said, \"Oh. You learned that.\"\n\n\"From Theo.\"\n\n\"Good student.\"\n\nWe ate. The window looked onto another wall. This is the whole view and it is enough. A person can live in a room with a wall for a window if the room has a door that opens, which is Mei's whole product, and which Rin's room has, and which mine in the site has never had.\n\nAt 7:20 I took the sunglasses out of my bag and put them on the table.\n\n\"These are Theo's,\" I said.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Take them.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"You are staying.\"\n\n\"For four more days.\"\n\n\"Four days is more than four hours.\" I said it slowly, in English that is almost correct, and I waited for her to correct me, and she did not. \"He gave them to me and I carried them and now I am going home and I don't want to carry them on a plane. They have his patches in the case. I didn't know that until I opened it. I am not supposed to know that.\"\n\nShe picked them up. She held them in both hands. They are black and very dark and they make a person's face into a decision.\n\n\"Why me?\" she said.\n\n\"Because you are the one who stays.\"\n\n\"That's not a reason.\"\n\n\"It is the only reason I have.\" I took the dress out of the bag and put it beside the glasses. \"Keep this too.\"\n\n\"I can't keep a dress.\"\n\n\"Keep it for the wall.\"\n\n\"There is no wall here.\"\n\n\"Then keep it for the next wall.\"\n\n\n\nAt 23:00 I went to TOKYO. ORIGINS is on Saturday, and this is just a Monday, and there is a difference, which I know, because I have been counting.\n\nSeventy-one people. One cat. Theo on the scaffolding with no sunglasses, which I have never seen before, and which made him look like a man who had been asked to be a person and had agreed.\n\nHe saw me look at his face.\n\n\"They're on the table at Rin's,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Good.\" Then he said, \"I asked her to take them. Not you.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I wanted you to have them. You carried them. But you're going.\"\n\n\"I'm going.\"\n\n\"Then she keeps them.\" He said it in that voice he uses when he is being generous and pretending he is not, which I now recognise, which is a thing I did not recognise six months ago. \"That's the whole rule, isn't it. You don't keep the thing. You pass it.\"\n\n\"Steel stays,\" I said. \"People go.\"\n\n\"Yes.\" He touched my shoulder and took his hand away. \"But the dress was yours.\"\n\n\"Tomorrow I give it back.\"\n\n\"No.\" He shook his head. \"Keep the dress. Wear it on the plane.\"\n\n\"In Bekasi?\"\n\n\"In Bekasi.\" He grinned. \"Aldi's mother will love it.\"\n\n\"She will kill me.\"\n\n\"Then I will have done my job.\"\n\nAt 00:17 the voice came on. You are allowed.\n\nI stood by the wall with my hands out. I have stood by this wall for four months. It has heard everything I have said in this country and it has never once repeated anything, which is a better record than any person I know.\n\nAt three the lights came on. I went home.\n\n\n\nAt 4 a.m. I packed. Two shirts, one pair of boots with cement on them, the dress, a photograph of my mother with a bucket, the letter with the window in it, and nothing else. A man can live with fewer things than he thinks. This is a thing I learned on floor thirty-one and have never been able to tell anyone in a way that makes them believe me.\n\nI said otsukaresama to the room, which is wrong. I said it to the dress and the photograph and the letter and the wall, which is also wrong, and which I meant.\n\nIn the morning, at the airport, I will hold a boarding pass with my name on it. Yusuf Pratama. This is the name my father gave me and the name on my work visa and the name my mother shouts across the road when the goat eats her shoe. It is a true name. It is not the only true thing about me, which I have learned in this country, in a room behind a dryer, with a man in a dress who asked instead of told.\n\nOn the plane I will put the dress on my knees and I will say otsukaresama to the seat, which is wrong, and which I will mean.\n\nThe show is on Saturday. I will be on a plane on the twenty-third. I have asked Theo to stand by the wall for me, which he said he would do, which is a joke and also not a joke, which is his whole talent.\n\nTheo keeps the room. Rin keeps the glasses. I keep the dress and the goat and the woman who said yes in the middle of the road in Bekasi with a goat making a noise like a small engine.\n\nThis is how a thing ends. Not with a door closing. With a thing handed to somebody else who is staying."},{"title":"CHAPTER 30 — TOKYO","text":"Rain. Wednesday. 2:58 a.m.\n\nI park. The meter runs. 1,180 yen.\n\nA woman comes out of WASH & GO. Grey sweatshirt. Wet hair. Too-big sleeves. Sun glasses in her hand. Black. I have seen them before. On a man in my back seat. On Christmas.\n\nShe opens the back door. She gets in.\n\n\"Shibuya,\" she says.\n\n\"We are in Shibuya.\"\n\n\"Then drive.\"\n\nI drive.\n\nShe holds a small recorder. Black. Tape. A cable taped with white tape. She works at the pharmacy. I know this. She buys nothing. She comes in sometimes with wet hair.\n\nThe recorder slips. It falls on the seat. The cassette comes out.\n\nShe says, \"Ah.\"\n\nI stop at the light. I pick up the cassette.\n\nIt is small. Brown. A label on side B. Blue ink. My hand. My writing. I know my writing. Twenty-five years do not change a man's writing.\n\nThe label says one word.\n\nTOKYO.\n\nUnder it, a date. 1999.03.\n\nI hold the cassette.\n\nThe light changes. A car behind me beeps. I drive.\n\n\n\n1999. I was twenty-six.\n\nA room under a pachinko hall in Shinjuku. Sign with half a character missing. Forty people. Maybe fifty. Music too loud. A thing made of pipe. Two metres high.\n\nI went up.\n\nI stood up there. A man. One night.\n\nA woman with a wig held my hand before I went up. She said in English, badly, you are allowed. I laughed at the English. I said it back. Then I said something in Japanese under my breath.\n\nShe recorded it. On a cassette. She gave me a copy. I put the label on myself. Blue ink. My hand.\n\nIn March the building burned. The man on the pipe burned with it.\n\nIn October I married a woman. I was good at it. I was good at being her husband. I was good at being his father.\n\nShe knew. From the third year. She never said.\n\nI never said.\n\nThe cassette went into a box. The box went into a drawer. The drawer went into a room that was not mine. Then a flea market. Then a girl of nine under a kotatsu. Then a woman in a grey sweatshirt with a recorder and a cable taped with white tape.\n\nI hold the cassette.\n\nThe wipers go. 1,240 yen.\n\n\n\n\"Sir,\" the woman says. \"The cassette.\"\n\nI give it back.\n\nThis is an action. It is the only action.\n\nShe takes it. She puts it in the recorder. She presses one button. Then another. This is a thing she does twice. Once to do the thing. Once to make sure.\n\nThe tape hisses.\n\nThen the voice.\n\n\"You are allowed.\"\n\nIn my back seat. In a taxi. In the rain. Twenty-five years. My voice. Bad English. Careful. Then the small dry laugh. Then the Japanese under the breath.\n\nThe woman watches the recorder. She does not look at the mirror. She does not look at me.\n\nI drive.\n\n1,240 yen. 1,240. 1,240.\n\nShe says, \"You know it?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"I found it in Shimokitazawa. Three hundred yen. No title I could read.\"\n\n\"Good price.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nShe puts the recorder in her bag. She holds the bag on her knees. She does not know. I do not tell her.\n\nI do not tell her.\n\n\n\nAt 3:04 we stop outside a building with another wall for a window.\n\n\"Here,\" she says.\n\nI do not move.\n\nThe meter says 1,240 yen. It said 1,240 yen before. I have driven nine minutes and the meter says 1,240 yen. This is wrong. The meter does not care. It only cares about moving and not moving.\n\n\"Here,\" she says again.\n\nI put the hand brake on. I turn. I look at her.\n\nShe has the sunglasses in her hand. Black. Theo's. She puts them on the seat beside her. She leaves them.\n\n\"Keep them,\" she says.\n\n\"I do not keep things.\"\n\n\"Then throw them away.\"\n\n\"I do not throw things away.\"\n\n\"Then keep them.\"\n\nShe gets out. She shuts the door. She stands on the pavement in the rain with her hand on the bag, and the bag holds the recorder and the tape and the voice.\n\nI watch her in the mirror. She walks to the door. She does not look back.\n\nAt 3:06 the door closes.\n\n\n\nI do not ask where she takes the tape. I do not ask what room she puts it in. A man who drives at night learns not to ask.\n\nI know a label. Blue ink. My hand. TOKYO. 1999.03.\n\nI know a man who stood on a pipe for one night.\n\nI know a woman who held his hand and said a sentence badly.\n\nI know that sentence came back to me in the back of my own car and I gave it back to the person who carried it, and I said nothing, and this is the thing I do. This is the whole thing I have done for twenty-five years.\n\nI say nothing. I have practiced.\n\n\n\nThe sunglasses are on the back seat. Black. Medical. The man in the back seat that night was foreign and drunk and said Shibuya when we were in Shibuya. He laughed. Then he cried. Then he slept. He paid too much. He left the glasses. Then he came back for them. I watched him do it.\n\nI keep it. Now there are two things on the back seat. A tie in the glove box. Sunglasses on the leather. A man who never keeps things, keeping things.\n\nI pick up the sunglasses. I put them in the glove box with the tie. It does not fit. I close it on the blue tie and the black glasses and the 2011 map and the unused wipes. The small space where a man could keep a thing if he wanted to.\n\nThe rain gets heavier. The wipers go.\n\nI sit with the meter running. It says 1,240 yen. It has said 1,240 yen for nine minutes. A man can sit in a car for a long time with the meter running and go nowhere. This is allowed. This is what I do.\n\nThe son called me mother. I did not correct him. The wife knew from the third year. She never said. The woman in the back seat had my voice on a tape and did not know, and I gave it back to her and did not say.\n\nOne night. A pipe. A sign with half a character missing.\n\nI put the car in gear.\n\nThe meter says 1,240 yen.\n\nI drive."},{"title":"CHAPTER 31 — TOWER","text":"Mei let me in at 1 p.m. because I said the wire to the left speaker was loose and because I have learned that a technical sentence opens more doors than a true one.\n\nThe room in daylight is a different animal. Wet concrete, grey plastic chairs stacked on tables, the smell of ginger and old glue and mop water. The mirror room with its six mirrors and its strips of paper. The sink with three metres of stainless steel and no cups on it. The stage with the scaffolding, which is called 東京タワー, Tokyo Tower, which is two metres high and which nobody in this room has ever gone up, because the room has decided it is not for anybody.\n\nMei said, \"Don't fall.\"\n\n\"I won't.\"\n\n\"I am not asking.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nShe went back behind the bar to count something, and I put the screwdriver in my pocket and climbed.\n\n\n\nThe scaffolding is not a tower. It is four pipes and a board with duct tape on the edges. But it is two metres up and from up there the room looks like what it is: a rectangle with a leak.\n\nThere is a name carved into the board.\n\nNot painted. Carved. A small knife or a key or a piece of metal, one letter at a time, in English, badly, like a man writing a sentence he has waited twenty-five years to be allowed to write.\n\nKENJI.\n\nUnder it, smaller: 1999.03.\n\nAnd under that, a word in Japanese which is not a name and which I know because I have said it to myself in the mirror for four years. 東京. TOKYO.\n\nI put my finger in the groove of the K. The wood is old and the groove is deep and somebody pressed hard enough to split the grain, which is a thing a person does when they are not sure the name will stay.\n\nI stood up there for six minutes. The room did not change. The room never changes. This is the whole problem with rooms: they hold things and they do not tell you.\n\nI have been playing a voice in this room for four months. A man's voice. Bad English. Careful. A small dry laugh. A sentence under the breath in Japanese that I have played fifty times and still could not decide whether it was a prayer or an address.\n\nI got down. I did not fall. Mei did not come out from behind the bar.\n\n\n\nAt 1:40 a.m. a taxi was parked on Maruyamacho. Far from the laundromat. Far enough that the sign was small.\n\nI walked to it and opened the back door and got in.\n\n\"Shibuya,\" I said.\n\n\"We are in Shibuya.\"\n\n\"Then drive.\"\n\nHe did not drive. He put the hand brake on and he sat with both hands on the wheel, looking through the windscreen at the rain.\n\nI had the cassette in my bag. I had the recorder. I had the screwdriver, which I had forgotten to take out of my pocket and which was not for him and which I put on the seat between us so that he would not think it was a weapon, which is a thing I decided in the second before I put it down.\n\n\"The voice,\" I said.\n\nHe said nothing.\n\n\"Why did you let me use it.\"\n\nHe watched the meter. It said 1,240 yen. It had said 1,240 yen for nine minutes, which I knew because I had been standing outside the car for six minutes and sitting in it for three, and because counting is what I do.\n\n\"It was already in the room,\" he said.\n\n\"That's not an answer.\"\n\n\"No,\" he said. \"It's the answer.\"\n\nThe wipers went. The rain was the same rain as November and as March and as every night for four months.\n\n\"You knew it was your voice,\" I said. \"In the car. On the twelfth. You picked up the tape and you saw your writing and you gave it back to me and you said nothing.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"No shape for it.\"\n\n\"There is a shape. There is a word for it.\"\n\n\"Not in my English.\"\n\n\"In Japanese there is one.\"\n\n\"I don't use that word.\"\n\n\"Which word?\"\n\nHe put the heat on. Then he turned it off. A man who does not sleep does not need heat.\n\n\"You were nine,\" he said.\n\nI looked out of the window. The laundromat was bright from inside and Mrs. Saito was folding towels and a dryer was going and nobody in there knew that a man was sitting in a cab outside with a tie in the glove compartment and a voice in a box on the back seat.\n\n\"You were nine,\" he said again. Not louder. The same. He has one sentence and he says it twice, which is his whole vocabulary of apology.\n\n\"I was nine,\" I said.\n\n\"You believed it.\"\n\n\"I believed it.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nThat is the sentence that broke it. Not I'm sorry. Not I am your father. Not I wanted to be. Just: I know. A man who heard a tape at nine under a kotatsu and a man who stood on a pipe at twenty-six and said the same sentence into a microphone in a room under a pachinko hall, and the tape went into a drawer and a flea market and a cheap stereo, and the two of them sat in a taxi twenty-five years later with a meter that had stopped at 1,240 yen.\n\nI did not forgive him.\n\nHe did not ask.\n\n\n\n\"I need to know one thing,\" I said.\n\n\"Ask.\"\n\n\"Did you tell her. My mother. Did you tell my mother about the tape.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"She knew.\"\n\n\"From the third year.\"\n\n\"Did you ever say it.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Why not.\"\n\n\"I was good at being her husband.\"\n\n\"Were you happy?\"\n\n\"Yes.\" A pause. \"No. Both. I was good at it.\"\n\nI put my hand on the screwdriver and took it off again. I have a great many objects in my hands in this conversation and none of them help.\n\n\"I changed one character,\" I said. \"On the family register. Eleven phone calls. A doctor who said this will take time. In November. I have the letter in my room.\"\n\nHe looked at me in the mirror. Not at my face. At the back of my head, which is how he sees me now, which is how he has always seen me, which is the whole problem.\n\n\"Are you a woman,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then it is done.\"\n\n\"It is not done. It is eleven phone calls and a letter and a doctor's sentence. It is not the same as being one.\"\n\n\"Same where I live.\"\n\n\"Where you live is a taxi.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI laughed. I did not want to. It came out the way the laugh came out in the alley that night with the cat and the wagyu, which is to say: against my whole architecture.\n\nHe did not laugh. But his hand moved on the wheel. A small movement. Two centimetres. The same movement from the first night, when the voice came through the wall and he was driving a drunk foreign man who said Shibuya when we were in Shibuya.\n\nI opened the door.\n\n\"Rin,\" he said.\n\nI stopped with one foot on the pavement.\n\n\"I have something,\" he said.\n\nHe opened the glove compartment. It did not fit. He closed it on the tie and the sunglasses and the 2011 evacuation map and the unused wet wipes, and then he opened it again and took out the blue tie, which had been folded badly for three weeks, and he held it out.\n\n\"For Yuma,\" I said.\n\n\"For you.\"\n\n\"I am not Yuma.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nI stood in the rain with the door open and the tie in his hand between us, which is a thing a father gives a daughter in this country and in mine and in none of the languages I speak properly.\n\nI took it. It was still warm from the car.\n\n\"Wear it,\" I said.\n\n\"I never wore it.\"\n\n\"Then don't start.\"\n\nI got out. I shut the door. I walked to the laundromat through the rain with my hands in my sweatshirt sleeves and the cassette in my bag and a screwdriver in my pocket that I had not used on any wire.\n\nAt 2:01 I pressed play. At 2:41 I pressed play again. The room kept dancing. The voice said the sentence to seventy-one people who did not know that a man was sitting in a parked car outside with the meter at 1,240 yen, and I stood at the switch with my hand on it, and for the first time in four months I let the whole thing run without counting the seconds.\n\nNot forgiveness. Not reconciliation. A room with a voice in it and a woman who finally knew whose.\n\nAt three the lights came on. I went outside. The taxi was gone.\n\nIn the morning, on the board under the stage, I put a piece of masking tape over the name. Not to hide it. To mark it. On the tape I wrote one word in English, because it is the only language the room understands:\n\nSTAY.\n\nThen I went to work and counted three hundred and forty-two pills and did not count the ones I take, which is a different kind of counting, and which I have decided to stop lying about on Tuesdays."},{"title":"CHAPTER 32 — GLOVE COMPARTMENT","text":"Rain. Friday. 2:10 a.m.\n\nShe gets out. The door shuts. The car is a room with one person in it again, and one person in a room is a different room entirely.\n\nThe glove compartment holds the 2011 map and the unused wipes. It closes with a small sound, like a mouth. The blue tie is gone. She took it.\n\nI take the black glasses out of the glove compartment and put them on the seat, then take them into WASH & GO and put them on the counter. Mrs. Saito does not ask. She puts them in a paper bag.\n\nI sit.\n\n\n\nShe asked me if I was a woman.\n\nI said yes.\n\nThis is the first time I have said it. Twenty-five years. One word. In a taxi. Meter 1,240. She got out. Rain.\n\nI did not say it to my wife. She knew from the third year. She never said. I never said.\n\nMy son called me mother. Twice. I did not correct him.\n\nI said it to a woman. Wet hair. Screwdriver. Tape on a board. A name I carved with a key in 1999. English. Badly.\n\n\n\nAt 3:04 a man gets in the back. Drunk. Foreign. Smells of gin.\n\n\"Shibuya,\" he says.\n\n\"We are in Shibuya.\"\n\nHe laughs for a long time. Then he says, \"Dryer four.\"\n\n\"I do not know.\"\n\n\"You know.\"\n\nHe pays too much. He says keep it. He gets out and goes into the laundromat, and behind the counter a woman looks up and does not smile and says something, and he nods like a man being told where left is.\n\nThis is the same man. Or a different man. All foreign men are the same man in my car at 3 a.m., and all of them are going to a room I have never entered.\n\nMeter: 1,240 yen.\n\n\n\nI drive until morning. No passengers after that. This is allowed. This is what I do.\n\nAt 8 a.m. my son is at the station. He said eight p.m. Tuesday. That Tuesday was a long time ago. I did not go. I have never gone.\n\nHe made rice. His mother asked him to ask me. She knows where I am. Everyone knows where I am.\n\nAt 8:12 a.m. I drive past the station. I see a boy in a coat his mother bought standing under the awning with a bag. He is nineteen. He has my hands, which he does not know.\n\nI do not stop.\n\nThe blue tie is in a woman's room on the other side of Shibuya. The black glasses are in a paper bag on a laundromat counter. I have a voice in a wall and a daughter who plays it to seventy-one people and did not know, and I gave it back to her and did not say, and she asked me why and I said you were nine, which is the only true thing I have ever offered anyone, and she took it, which is more than I deserved.\n\nThe meter goes up. 980 yen. 1,120. 1,180. 1,240.\n\nA number is a thing you can trust. It starts at zero and it goes up and it does not care what you did in 1999.\n\nAt 9:03 a.m. the money moves out of an account with a name like a small grey animal into the account for a room that will be empty in six days. I know about the money. I have known for four years. I do not know why I know. A man who drives at night hears things through walls.\n\nI keep driving.\n\nThe rain stops. It has been falling since November. It has been falling since March. It has been falling my whole life, and nobody has ever once told it to stop, and nobody ever will.\n\nMeter: 1,240 yen.\n\nI have said it. I said it to her. I said yes.\n\nI drive."},{"title":"CHAPTER 33 — ORIGINS","text":"Fire code says forty-eight. Tonight there are forty-eight.\n\nMei counted at the door like a bouncer at a function, which she is not, which is a job I have now seen her do twice in four months and which she is frighteningly good at. Momo stood beside her with his hand out and his face like a held breath, and when the forty-ninth person arrived she said, in English, \"Not tonight. Tonight is a number.\"\n\nThe person said, \"But last week —\"\n\n\"Last week was seventy-one. Tonight is a number. Come Tuesday.\"\n\nThis is the first time I have seen a room choose to be smaller, and I want to write about it in a way that would make a foundation cry with joy, but I am not filming, and I have decided not to turn anything into a sentence tonight, including myself.\n\n\n\nMomo gave me a paper bag before I went up. No note. Inside: my sunglasses. Black. The patch case empty, cleaned out, folded neatly. I held them for a bit in the mirror room under six mirrors, and I put them on, and I took them off, and I put them on again, because a man can change his mind about a face four times in thirty seconds and call it a creative process.\n\nThere was a piece of masking tape stuck to the mirror. STAY. I did not write it. I know whose hand it is, which is a thing I would not have known a month ago, and which I have decided not to say out loud, because the room has heard enough voices in English for one lifetime.\n\nAt 00:10 I went to the scaffolding. The wig was on the stool: a black bob with a fringe that could cut rice paper. Mei had ironed it. She will never admit this. I found a small crease in the fringe that was ironed flat and I know an ironed crease when I see one, because my mother ironed everything in our house in E13, including, once, a plastic bag, which she then wore out to the shops, which is a different story.\n\nI said, \"You ironed the wig.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"You did.\"\n\n\"Drink first.\"\n\n\"Mei.\"\n\n\"Break a leg. Drink first.\"\n\n\n\nI wrote ORIGINS in four parts. I want to be exact, because nobody filmed it and exactness is the only record it has.\n\nPart one: breathing.\n\nI came out as Theo. No wig. No sunglasses, in the end, because I took them off at the last second and put them on the stool beside the wig. Black shirt. Face washed. The patch on my hip under my trousers where nobody could see it and where I had put it carefully, which is a thing I have done four hundred times and which I will do again, and which is not the show and which is part of the show, which is the whole problem with bodies.\n\nI played a voice note. Three seconds. My mother's breathing. In a room of forty-eight people in a basement in Shibuya at midnight, the smallest sound in the world came out of a cheap speaker and nobody moved, and I let it run its whole three seconds, which I have never done at home, where I always skip to the end because hearing a person breathe at me for three seconds is too much to survive in a kitchen alone.\n\nThen I stopped it. I said, \"That's my mother. That's the whole message. She sent it for two years and one day she sent two words and I haven't replied.\"\n\nA laugh. A small one. Not at me. With me. The room knows the shape of a mother not replying.\n\nPart two: names.\n\nI said, \"In this club we don't use real names. This is a rule. I broke it on my first night and a woman behind the bar slid me a drink and told me to drink first, which is the most useful sentence I have learned in this country.\"\n\nMei, from the bar: \"Still true.\"\n\nI said, \"So I'm not going to use yours. I'm going to use the ones you gave yourselves, which are the only ones that count, which is a thing I believe, which is a thing I said loudly for twenty-four years in a city that didn't care, and which I am saying quietly here, where you all care too much.\"\n\nI named no one. I looked at the old man in row three. I looked at Yusuf by the wall with his hands out of his pockets. I looked at Rin behind the speakers with one hand on the cassette player. I looked at Cass at a table with her camera in her bag and her hands in her lap. I looked at the cat on the bar.\n\n\"VEX,\" I said. \"That's mine. It's not a name I was given. It's a name I made. Some of you made yours in a room like this. Some of you are still making it in your head and haven't said it yet, which is fine, which is also a name.\"\n\nThen the bass came in very low, and I did a lipsync in four languages, none of which I speak, to a song about a woman who leaves and a man who cannot follow, and the room sang the chorus in the words each of them knew, and for three minutes we were all wrong in the same key, which is the closest thing to a country I have ever felt.\n\nPart three: the form.\n\nNo cardboard officer. No joke. I promised.\n\nI took a piece of paper out of my pocket. A real form. The one from Shinagawa, folded into eight, with a box on it that the officer did not fill and did not tick, and the empty box was the loudest thing in that room, and I wanted forty-eight people to see it.\n\nI held it up. I walked the length of the small stage with it. I put it on the floor and stood on it. Then I got down, picked it up, and folded it again, and put it back in my pocket, which is what a person does with a form when there is no other thing to do with it.\n\nNobody laughed. This is the truest thing I did all night.\n\nPart four: origins.\n\nI took off the black shirt. Under it I had a dress. Not a costume. Black. The same black as the one in the bag under Yusuf's bed in Shibuya, with cement on the left side. I put it on over my head in front of forty-eight people under pink light, and I did not make a face, and I did not wink, and I did not say isn't this funny, which is the sentence I have used for six years to stop anyone from looking too closely.\n\nThen I put the wig back on. VEX arrived. VEX has cheekbones you could cut rice paper on. VEX does not wonder if her mother listened. VEX does not have a visa. VEX is not from anywhere and is therefore allowed to be from everywhere, which is a very English way of saying a very simple thing, and which I have spent six years getting wrong.\n\nI did the last song without the joke at the end.\n\nThis is the thing Mei asked for. No joke at the end. I got to the last line, and the old muscle in my mouth reached for something about landlords or forms or my mother's stew, and I shut it, and I finished the song on the actual last note, and then I stood there with my hands down under the fluorescent light, which came on at three exactly, as it always does, as it must.\n\nForty-eight people. No photographs. No phones. No livestream. The old man in row three put four thousand yen on the table and did not leave. His hands were shaking, which I have never seen before, and he kept them flat on the table, the way a man holds a thing steady when it is not his to hold.\n\n\n\nAt 3:06 I was still on the stage. At 3:12 I sat down on the edge of it with the wig in my hand and the dress on my body and the sunglasses on the stool beside me, and I looked at the room under the lights, which is a room that has no secret any more and is therefore just a room, which is the best thing a room can be.\n\nRin came and stood next to the stage. She put two fingers on the sunglasses on the stool and moved them a finger's width to the left, which is a thing she does with the pill counter, twice, once to do the thing and once to make sure.\n\n\"Are they yours?\" she said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Do you want them?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then take them.\"\n\nI picked them up. I put them in my pocket. I did not put them on, because a person can want a thing and keep it in a pocket, and this is a new skill and I am very bad at it.\n\n\"The show,\" she said.\n\n\"Yes?\"\n\n\"It was a door.\"\n\nThere it is. Her word. I have waited four months for her to use it about something I did, and I can't think of a single funny thing to say, and if I say thank you it will become a scene, and Mei will throw a broken opener at me, and she has excellent aim.\n\nSo I said, \"Drink first.\"\n\nShe almost smiled. I saw it. Six mirrors would not have shown it better.\n\n\n\nAt 3:20 the room was empty except for us and Mei and the cat. The last Saturday. One show. No cameras. Forty-eight people who will never be in a film and who will therefore be the only ones I remember exactly.\n\nMei put a glass on the bar in front of me. Ginger. Everything here is ginger. I am becoming a person who associates ginger with being allowed.\n\n\"Tomorrow,\" she said.\n\n\"Tomorrow.\"\n\n\"No joke,\" she said.\n\n\"No joke.\"\n\nShe poured. She did not look at me, which is how I knew she had heard every word of the form part, and that it was okay, and that it was not okay, and that both of those things were in the room and neither of them needed a photograph.\n\nOutside, the rain waited, the way it waits. In four days there will be a wall where the dryer was, and a club that is not anywhere, and a man in Berlin who will hear the lights come on at three in a room with no alley and will make one, which is something I know now that I did not know when I got here, and which I am not going to write down, because a thing you write down becomes a poster.\n\nFour hours. Then the lights.\n\nI got the last one right. I did not add a joke at the end.\n\nI am counting that."},{"title":"CHAPTER 34 — LAST NIGHT","text":"Last night. The crew comes at eight in the morning. Not seven. Not nine. Eight, which is a time chosen by a man with a schedule and no imagination.\n\nThe notice said April first for eleven years. Then the son came on Monday with a new one, thinner paper, same font, and a date moved up twelve days. The twentieth. Eight a.m. He said schedule. I said fine, which is a word I have never used before in this room and which I will never use again.\n\nI opened at eleven like always. There was no sign outside that said LAST NIGHT. I considered one. I wrote LAST NIGHT on a piece of paper and put it on the door and took it off again, because a sign is an admission, and an admission is a thing you make when you need someone to comfort you, and I have three customers in a failing bar and a father in a hospital and forty-seven broken openers and no intention of being comforted by a piece of paper.\n\nMrs. Saito came down at 10:30 with a basket of towels and a face that had decided nothing. She stood in the room for three minutes with her hands on the basket and looked at the scaffolding, the mirrors, the sink, the cat.\n\n\"Twenty-two years,\" she said, in Japanese.\n\n\"Twenty-two,\" I said.\n\n\"Where will you go?\"\n\n\"Across the street. There is a bar with a licence and a fire exit and a television that shows baseball in December.\"\n\n\"Good.\" She put the basket down. \"My dryer is breaking anyway.\"\n\nThen she went back up. This is the whole conversation. I have written it down in my head four times and it has never become longer, which is a mercy, and which is a kind of love, and which I am not going to analyse, because analysing a thing is a way of taking it apart until you can put it in a folder.\n\n\n\nSeventy-one people. One cat. Every night is seventy-one people and one cat, and tonight the seventy-one are the same seventy-one who have been here since November, which is a thing I have never seen before in a club, because clubs are made of people who come and go, and this room has become a room made of people who stayed.\n\nThe old man in row three put his glass down a finger's width to the left and then did it again and then looked at his hand, as if it had moved without him, which it had.\n\nYusuf was by the wall with his hands out of his pockets. He has a bag under the stool and a flight on Sunday and a dress in the bag which he will not show anyone and which he has already shown everyone by the way he stands. Cass was at a table with her camera in her bag and her hands in her lap, which is where they have been for two months. Theo was on the scaffolding in a black dress and no wig, and he has said he will do one number and he has said it four times, which means he will do three.\n\nRin was behind the speakers with the recorder and the tape and the screwdriver, doing the thing with the button twice, once to do the thing and once to make sure. At 00:17 she pressed play and the voice came on and the room kept dancing, and I watched her watch the speakers, and I thought about a man in a taxi with a label in blue ink in his own handwriting, and I did not say anything, because a room full of people who have decided to be happy is not a room that wants an explanation from the bartender.\n\nAt 1:40 Theo did his number. He finished on the last note, which is a thing I have never heard him do, and then he stood under the light with his hands down.\n\nAt 2:00 the old man in row three stood up and put four thousand yen on the table and walked to the door and stopped, and stood there for a while with his hand on the wall, and then he went up the stairs without looking back, and nobody clapped, because everybody knew he would be back on Tuesday, which he will not be, which is the whole joke of a room that closes, that it keeps its habits after the room is gone.\n\n\n\nAt 2:40 I took the ledger out from behind the bottles nobody orders. There is one page left. Not one line. One page, which is worse, because a page wants to be filled and a line does not.\n\nI read it. Two hundred and fourteen pages of people who showed up.\n\n2019 — someone slept here.\n2020 — someone didn't.\n2022 — a broken opener. The first one.\n2023 — a man sang in the sink. Nobody knew the words.\n2024 — Momo let a man in who could say rain.\n2024 — Theo's first show, the old man in row three cried, do not tell him I told you.\n2024 — Iqbal's boots, still by the door.\n2024 — Rin came in with wet hair and I thought, there's one.\n2025 — Yusuf danced.\n2024 — someone came in.\n2024 — nobody left.\n2025 — still here.\n2025.02.14 — a bottle broke.\nApril — crossed out. 3.20 — a date.\nMarch 15 — one show. No cameras.\n\nI turned to the blank page and held the pen for a while. Theo was on the stage. Yusuf was by the wall. Rin was at the switch. Cass had her hands in her lap. The cat was on the bar with one paw on my forearm, which is a thing he has started doing, which is a thing I have decided not to name, because naming a thing is a way of taking it into your hands and setting it down somewhere else.\n\nI wrote:\n\n19 March — everyone came.\n\nThen I closed the book and put it back behind the bottles nobody orders, and for the first time in eleven years I did not take it out again at closing to check whether the line was straight.\n\n\n\nAt three exactly the lights came on. All of them. No warning, no countdown. Seventy-one faces get ten years older in one second, and the glue cracks on the stage like a dry field, and the wig on the stool is a small dead animal, and the cat blinks, which is the only creature in the room for whom the light changes nothing.\n\nNobody moved.\n\nNot out of defiance this time. Not like Christmas. This time it was a room that had run out of places to go after three, and everybody knew it, and nobody needed to make a decision about it, because the decision had been made in November by a landlord with a wedding ring on the wrong hand.\n\nTheo sat down on the edge of the stage. Rin came out from behind the speakers and stood beside him. Yusuf came away from the wall. Cass took her hands out of her lap and put them on the table and then on her knees. The old man was gone, but his glass was still there, four thousand yen under it, which Theo will find in the morning and which nobody will know what to do with, and which I will put in the new bar behind the register under a piece of paper that says if.\n\nAt 3:12 people started to go. Slowly. In ones and twos. Not because the music stopped — I left the bass on, very low, the way I did on Christmas — but because a room can only hold a thing for so long before the thing becomes too heavy to stand under, and a room with no tomorrow is the heaviest room there is.\n\nBy 3:40 there were four of us and a cat.\n\nTheo took off the dress and folded it and put it in a bag, and he took the sunglasses out of his pocket and put them on the stool beside the wig, and then he took them back, and then he put them in his pocket again, which is a thing I have seen him do with that object six times tonight and which I have decided is a private matter.\n\nRin said something to him in a voice too low for the room, which is a thing people do in a room with no music, and he nodded, and she put two fingers on the edge of the stage and left.\n\nYusuf said otsukaresama to the door on the way out, which is wrong, which he knows is wrong, which he says anyway.\n\nCass put the camera in her bag and zipped it, which is a sound I have heard twice in four months and which I am putting in the ledger in my head, because there is no room on the page.\n\n\n\nAt 4 a.m. I turned off the lights. Not at three. Later. For the second time in my life I broke my own rule, and I broke it alone, and nobody clapped, and the cat did not care.\n\nI washed the glasses. All of them. One by one. The Claudia glass, the gin glass, the old man's glass with four thousand yen under it, which I washed and dried and put back on the shelf in row three, because somebody has to keep the row.\n\n財務大臣 sat on the stool and watched me. Good cat. The best cat. I have decided not to say why.\n\nAt 4:40 I put the ledger in my bag. Not behind the bottles. In my bag. I have never done this before. I have never taken that book out of this building, and the building will be a wall in four hours, so there is nowhere left to put it, which is the whole logic of a thing that ends.\n\nOn the way up the stairs I stopped at the door with no handle and put my hand on it, the way Momo used to stand there with his hand out, and I did not say the password, because there was nobody to say it to, and because the password tonight was a sentence about a door, which somebody picked, and which I suspect Rin picked, and which she will deny, with too much detail, if I ask.\n\nWASH & GO was bright and empty and the dryers were going. Mrs. Saito was behind the counter folding towels. She did not look up. She said, in English, \"Your makeup today is very suitable for office.\"\n\n\"Thank you,\" I said.\n\n\"Tomorrow is not office.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then it is fine.\"\n\nOutside, the cleaning truck was brushing the gutters. It was 4:58. In three hours and two minutes a crew of men in hard hats would come and take the room apart, starting with the laundromat, and nobody would put it back, and nobody would believe it had been there, which is why I am carrying a book with two hundred and fourteen pages of people who showed up.\n\nA room with a door that opens. That is the whole product.\n\nI sold it for four hours at a time for four months, and then I gave it away, and I would do it again, which is the stupidest thing I have ever done, and which is the only thing I have ever done properly.\n\nThe rain waited until I was at the station and then started, the way it does.\n\nIt had been waiting all night."},{"title":"CHAPTER 35 — EQUINOX","text":"Spring equinox. Day and night are the same length, which is a fact the city does not care about, and which I have been thinking about since four this morning.\n\nAt eight a.m. the crew came. Not seven. Not nine. Eight.\n\nI stood across the street under the awning of a closed flower shop with a coffee in my hand that I had not paid for, because Mrs. Saito had put it in my hand and said, \"Today is free,\" which is a thing she has never said in twenty-two years and which I have decided not to ask about.\n\nTwo men in hard hats went into WASH & GO first. They took out the dryers. Dryer four came out at 8:41, which I know because I looked at my phone, and because counting is what I do. They carried it to a truck and put it on top of a pile of other machines, and it looked small up there, which was strange, because it was a door.\n\nThen they took the wall.\n\nNot all of it. They took a section behind where the dryer had been, which is the place where the stairs went down, and they worked with a saw that made a sound like a person shouting in a language I could not understand, and concrete dust came out and went into the street and got on the shoes of a woman waiting for the bus, who brushed it off without looking, because in this city people brush things off without looking, which is a skill I have spent four months learning and will now never need.\n\nAt 9:20 a man came out of the building carrying a piece of the sign. The light box from the old pachinko hall, with half of a character missing. He put it in the truck. I wanted to take it. I did not take it. A thing you take becomes a thing you have to explain, and I have spent my whole life not explaining, and I am tired, and I am still not taking it.\n\nAt 10:02 the stairs were gone. There was a flat wall where they had been, the colour of the rest of the room, and a man was sweeping the dust into a pile.\n\nThis is the whole demolition. Two hours and forty-one minutes. I had imagined it would take longer, or be louder, or that someone would come out, which nobody did, because there was nobody in there, because the room had emptied itself at three the night before and had not taken anything with it.\n\n\n\nMei was at 梅屋 at 3 p.m. with three customers, one hiding from his wife, one asleep, one new, who asked about the jar of forty-seven broken openers and whether they were for sale.\n\n\"No,\" Mei said.\n\n\"I'll give you five thousand for one.\"\n\n\"Which one?\"\n\n\"The blue one.\"\n\n\"That one has the best personality. Ten thousand.\"\n\nThe man laughed. Mei did not. She was wearing a black dress under her apron, which I had never seen her wear, and which she did not explain, and which she will never admit to, the way Theo will never admit about the wig.\n\nAt 5:30 the last customer went. She turned the television off, which was showing baseball in March, and she took the ledger out of her bag and put it on the bar in front of me.\n\n\"This isn't an inheritance,\" she said. \"It's a bill.\"\n\nI opened it. Two hundred and fourteen pages. I have read four of them, and I will read the rest, and I will not read them tonight, because tonight is for leaving, and reading is for staying.\n\n\"I can't take this,\" I said.\n\n\"You can't not take it.\" She put a glass down. \"Drink first.\"\n\n\"Mei.\"\n\n\"Drink first. Then argue.\"\n\nI drank. Ginger. Everything here is ginger.\n\n\"You should call your mother,\" she said.\n\n\"Do not tell me what to do.\"\n\n\"I am asking.\" She wiped the counter, which was clean. \"I ask because I am leaving tomorrow and I will not be here to ask again. My father is in a hospital in Nakano. He called me sister yesterday and by my name the day before and neither of those is me, but I shave his head anyway, because that is a thing I can do, and it is a thing he lets me do, and that is the whole marriage, and the whole disease, and the whole daughter.\"\n\nI put my hand on the ledger. The cover was red and the corners were soft from eleven years of hands.\n\n\"Why me?\" I said.\n\n\"Because you came in with wet hair and I thought, there's one.\"\n\n\"That's in the book.\"\n\n\"Yes. I wrote it. You should not read your own line. It's embarrassing.\"\n\n\"Are you happy?\"\n\n\"No.\" She poured another glass. \"Both. I am alive, which is not a small thing to write. Ask me again in a year.\"\n\n\n\nAt 6 p.m. I went home. My room has six tatami and a wall for a window and a shelf of seven pans and a box of vials in the fridge with my name on them now, which is a sentence I have said to no one and which I will say to one person someday, and I do not know who, and I have stopped caring that I do not know who.\n\nOn the table: the recorder, the cassette, the screwdriver, and a blue tie that I took from a man who held it out. I have not worn it. I have not put it in a drawer either, which is a thing I would have done a year ago.\n\nThere is a paper bag on the table. It was not there this morning. Theo must have left it before dawn, because he cannot give a thing directly and keep his face straight. Inside is a strip of masking tape with STAY written in my own hand and a pair of black sunglasses, cleaned of patches and folded neatly, with no note.\n\nI put them on. They are too dark for a room with a wall for a window. I took them off. I put them on again. I did the thing four times in thirty seconds and called it a creative process, which is a sentence I learned from Theo, who learned it from nobody, who made it up in a laundromat at 3:06 a.m., which is the kind of thing a person makes up in a laundromat at 3:06 a.m.\n\nOutside, the sky was the colour of a washed glass. The light was the same length as the dark, which is a fact and not a feeling, and which I am going to hold onto, because facts are the only things I trust now, and I have very few of them.\n\nI called my mother.\n\nIt was 7 p.m. in Shibuya and 6 p.m. in Kobe. She answered on the second ring.\n\n\"Did you eat?\" she said.\n\n\"I ate.\"\n\n\"Don't lie to your mother.\"\n\n\"I'm not lying to my mother.\"\n\nA pause. A long one. The kind of pause that has four years in it and fifty thousand yen a month and a word neither of us has ever said in the right order.\n\n\"Rin,\" she said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI sat down on the floor with the phone against my ear and the ledger on the table in front of me and the sunglasses in my hand and the vial box in the fridge with my name on it, and I said the word I have had in my mouth for four years, which is not a big word, which is a small word, which is the smallest word that is still big enough.\n\nI said: \"I am your daughter.\"\n\nShe did not answer right away. Then she said, \"Okay.\"\n\nOne word. Not a poem. Not a forgiveness. A word.\n\nOkay.\n\nIt was enough. It was not enough. Both, which is the only honest answer I have ever given anyone, and which I gave to a woman who asked me if I was happy, and which I gave to a taxi driver who said you were nine, and which I gave to a room that is now a wall.\n\nAt 8 p.m. the lights came on somewhere in this city. There is no club. There is no alley. There is no door behind a dryer. But a room with the lights on is a room with the lights on, and I am standing in one, and there is nobody here to tell me to leave, and I am not going to.\n\nThe rain waited.\n\nIt always waits."},{"title":"CHAPTER 36 — EBBS","text":"A year is not a long time. A year is 365 pills if you take one a day, which I do not. A year is 52 Sundays, and Mei's father has called me sister on 31 of them, by my name on 14, and on 7 he looked at me and there was nobody behind his eyes at all.\n\nA year is also long enough for a laundry machine to break twice.\n\nMrs. Saito replaced it in February. New washer, new dryer, new wall where the dryer used to be. Concrete, painted the same off-white as the rest of the room, and if you did not know, you would never know. If you did know, you stood in front of it for a while, because there is a specific grief in a wall that was once a door.\n\nSomeone had written on it.\n\nNot spray paint. Marker. Black. Two lines, and the handwriting is nothing like mine and nothing like Mei's and nothing like Theo's, who writes like he is stabbing the page:\n\ntokyo is not a place you go.\n\nUnderneath, smaller, in a different hand, in a different ink, so it cannot have been written on the same night:\n\nit's a place that goes with you.\n\nI do not know who wrote the second line. I have my theories and they are all selfish.\n\n\n\nHere is what I know, which is not the same as what happened.\n\nTheo sent a photograph of a form from Berlin, held up in front of a wall with a faction poster on it. LOOK AT THE LITTLE FORM, RIN. THE LITTLE FORM IS REAL. He performs in a club with a fire exit and 64 channels of light, and in January he sent a voice note in which he is crying and laughing at the same time, which is a VEX skill, not a Theo skill. The lights came on at 3 and I looked for the alley and there wasn't one, so I made one. He asked me to keep the sunglasses. They are in the drawer with the tie. I have not told Mei about the ginger. I'm telling her tomorrow.\n\nYusuf sends photographs of Bekasi: the road, a goat, a blue tarp, Aldi's hand on the tarp. Last week: I am living with my husband in the same house. I read it six times. In November: today I say otsukaresama to the goat. She is also working. He taught Aldi the word. Aldi uses it correctly.\n\nCass handed me the SD card at the sink, wrapped in a napkin from an aeroplane, with a note: I was looking for my mother. I'm sorry I used your face to do it. Ninety-four seconds. Neither of us has named them. In March she wrote that her hands have stopped making the shape of a camera. I wrote back: Good. Two letters. It was enough. It was not enough. Both.\n\nMei's bar on the corner of Maruyamacho is called メイ and has a licence and a fire exit and opens from 5 p.m. to midnight. Her father died on 2 November. She shaved his head that morning. She told me this once, while looking at a broken opener, of which she has forty-seven, and she would not sell the blue one, which is the whole language we speak. On the anniversary she put a piece of paper in the new bar with one word on it: if.\n\nKenji drives. In October he gave me a cassette — not the one from the flea market, a copy — with the voice on side B, between two songs, saying the sentence badly and then laughing at himself. I did not forgive him. He did not ask. We had one conversation in a parked taxi and the only part I have told anyone is this: he said you were nine. His son called him mother again in August. Kenji did not correct him.\n\n\n\nI am twenty-nine now. I have my name. It took eleven phone calls, not twelve — I counted, because counting is what I do — and a document in triplicate and a doctor who said, kindly, this will take time, which is what doctors say when they mean this will take paperwork. The vials in my fridge now have my name on them. I still keep the old ones. I keep a lot of things past their date. The blue tie is in a drawer in my room in Shibuya, which is a thing I told myself I would never do and which I did on the second Tuesday.\n\nHere is the ledger. Mei gave it to me on 20 March, after the last customer went, at 梅屋, with the television off and the jar of openers back on the shelf. She said: this isn't an inheritance. it's a bill. I have read all 214 pages. It is not money. It is: 2019 — someone slept here. 2020 — someone didn't. 2022 — a broken opener. The first one. 2023 — a man sang in the sink. Nobody knew the words. 2024 — Momo let a man in who could say rain. 2024 — Theo's first show, the old man in row three cried, do not tell him I told you. 2024 — Iqbal's boots, still by the door. 2024 — Rin came in with wet hair and I thought, there's one. 2025 — Yusuf danced. 2025.02.14 — a bottle broke. 2025.03.15 — one show. No cameras.\n\nThe last page has lines in two hands, the first in Mei's, written on 28 November 2024:\n\nsomeone came in.\n\nUnder it, in a hand I know and have never asked about, there is a small addition, made in March, after the room was gone:\n\nand stayed.\n\nI have stood in front of this new wall for six minutes. Mrs. Saito has asked me twice if I need a washing machine and I have said no twice, which is a lie, I need several.\n\nBehind me a dryer is going. There is no secret door. I have checked. I check every time I come, which is every Tuesday, which is a thing I did not plan and have not explained to anyone.\n\nI want to write something on the wall. I have a pen. I have had a pen for six minutes.\n\nI put the cap back on. I do not write my name. Names are for doors, and this is a wall. The two lines are already here, in two hands, which is more than I can say for anything else that happened.\n\nThen I go upstairs, because it is 2:58, and in two minutes a club that is not anywhere will turn its lights on, and I want to be outside for it. I want to feel it happen to the whole street. I want to stand under a sky that is not a ceiling and watch the city become, for one second, exactly what it is: a room with the lights on, full of people who are allowed.\n\nAt 3:00 the lights come on in a room with 64 channels in Neukölln, and in a bar in Oakland with a television showing birds, and in a house in Bekasi with a goat asleep under a blue tarp, and in a taxi on a wet street in Shibuya where a man has turned the heat up for a stranger.\n\nNobody asks anybody to leave.\n\nOutside, a cleaning truck goes by, brushing the gutters with brushes the size of coffins. I watch it pass, the way I have watched it pass from a hundred doors, and I am no longer waiting for it to stop.\n\nThe rain waits.\n\nIt has been waiting since March. It has been waiting my whole life. It will still be waiting in November, and nobody will ever once tell it to stop, and nobody ever will.\n\nI put the pen back in my pocket and walk home in the rain with my hands in my sweatshirt sleeves, and I am not cold, which is a fact, and not a feeling, and which I am learning to trust.\n\n\n\nFIN"}];
